      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>LOVE may be blind, but he:skips the girl with the squint. • IGNORANT people are people who know less than we think we do. A MAN may be in touch with the toothache without being in sympathy with it. Is infectious diseases were ideas instead of realities, some men would never catch them. WHEN a burg:ar asks the conundrum, "Where's your money ?" it is generally the wisest plan to give it up. TOURIST: "That's Vesuvius, isn't it ?" Guide: "Yes." Tourist: "Will you tell me when the next eruption will occur ?" DESPISE not advice, though even of the meanest —the gobbling of geese once preserved the city of Rome.</p>
        <p>LADY (engaging nurse) "Have you any experience with children?" Irishwoman: "Oh, yis, mum ! Oi used to be a child mesilf wanst." POPE said that it is with our opinions as with our watches—none of them go alike, and ye.rach man believes in his own.</p>
        <p>TEACHER: "What happened when the man killed the goose that laid the golden egg?" Dick Hicks: "His goose was cooked. '</p>
        <p>JINKS "I despise a man who is mean with his wife. Do you give yours an allowance, or what she can wheedle out of you ?" Filkins : "Both."</p>
        <p>AN IMPORTANT AFTERTHOUGHT. —He : "And what would you do if I kissed you ?" She (with dignity) : "I'd call mamma." (After a pause). "But mamma's out this evening."</p>
        <p>CONDUCIVE TO SOLEMNITY.—Mr. Worldlie : "I don't see why these preachers should go about with such long faces." Deacon Hardupp : You would if you had to live on one of their salaries."</p>
        <p>LANDLADY: "That new boarder needn't try to make me think he is a bachelor. He's either mar- ried or a widower." Millings: "How can you tell !" Landlady: "He always turns his back to me when he opens his pocket-book to pay his board."</p>
        <p>"How much do policemen get paid ?" asked a lady of the fixed point at her corner. "Twenty- five shillings a week, mum." " Indeed ; and all found ?" "No mum," replied the truthful bobby, "we are never found."</p>
        <p>A LOITER'S SACRIFICE.—" My dear Laura, last night a beautilul idea struck me how to ingratiate myself with your father, so that he must yield at last, and give his consent to our marriage. You know he is an inveterate snuff-taker, and"- "Ah you are going to present him with a snuff- box ?" "Better than that: I shall at once begin taking snuff myself." "WHY didn't you keep the horse, Bertie, deah boy ?" Bertie : "The howid creature had the impudence to tell me that the horse was bwoken, and I sent him back for a whole one."</p>
        <p>VERY CHEAP TEA. —Housekeeper : "Are you sure that this tea isn't half copperas 1" Dealer (convincingly) : "We couldn't afford to sell copperas at the extremely low price we charge for this tea, ma'am."</p>
        <p>HIS VACATION: "Hello, Harry! What are you doing moving ? " asked one young man of another, whom he met with a big valise in his hand. "I've just commenced my vacation." "Your vacation ? " " Yes ; I'm vacating at the request of my landlady." ANSWERED,—Mr. Baldpate: "I say, doctor, do you think hair dye injures the brain ?" Dr. Smart: "Certainly not, sir, persons with brains never use it." BELLE: "What an absurd man Mr. Snooks is? He told me he hoped I never giggled." Bessie: "And what did you say ? " Belle: "I giggled." INQUIRING CHILD: "Father, there's a lot in this book about, °Aiello. Who was Othello" Father: " Othello ? Why, bless my boy, do you mean to tell me you go to Sunday-school and don't know a simple thing like that. I'm ashamed of you !"</p>
        <p>MR. FON °PARENT : "You are dreadfully de- ficient in geography, Thomas. In fact, you don't know anything about geography." Tommy: "It's not so bad as that. I know the names of loth of citits and countries, but• I don't know exactly where they are." CALLED TO THE BAR: "I say, Jones, you remember that youtig Smith we used to knock about with? I .see he's been called to the bar," Jones : "Ali, indeed! Going to be a barrister, eh?' Brown: " Barrister ? No; he's the potman at the Pig and Whistle." -</p>
        <p>A PARTING INJUNCTION. —Travelling Salesman (to employer) : "Well, I'm off, Mr. Smith. Good- bye ! " Employer: "Good-bye, and a successful trip; and remember, Mr. Bloward, that order is Heaven's first law." "TALK about absent-mL dedness, said a man the other day—" wey, I like it, for, when I was a boy, L worked for a man who was so absent- minded that he discharged me three times in one week and paid me a week's wages each time!" "BEAUTY is no real advantage," urged the earnest girl. "But, it catches the transient trade," was the only comment of the grass widow with blue eyes and golden hair. SAFE FROM THE AUTOGRAPH FIEND. Fond Mother (to teacher) : "Don't you think my boy is bound to make his mark?" Teacher: "I'm afraid so. It seems impossible for him to learn to write." AN EXPLA,.NATION.—CaIIy : "Miss Peterson has • remarkably small eyes." Dally: "Yes, they look small; but she has had a young man in 'em for a long time." A HUNGRY friend at Brummel's table, after the beau had fallen in fortune, remarked that nothing was better than cold beef. "I beg your pardon," returned Brummel, "cold beef is betta Nan nothing." A BIG DIFFERENCE, USUALLY. —Studious boy: "What is the meaning of market value' and intrinsic value ' ?" Father: "The 'market value' is the price you pay for a thing; intrinsic value' is what you get when you sell it to a second-hand dealer." A WAG went to the station of one of the rail- roads one evening, and finding the best seats all taken, opened the door of one carriage and said, "Why, this train isn't going." A general stam- pede ensued, and the wag took the coveted seat in the corner. In the midst of the general indig- nation he was asked, "Why did you say the train wasn't going ?" "Well, it wasn't then," replied the wag, "but it is now." MUTUAL ACCO3IMODATION.—A fire-eating Irish- man challenged a barrister, who gratified him by an acceptance. The duellist, being very lame, requested that he might have'a prop. "Suppose," said he, "I lean against this milestone ?" " With pleasure," replied the lawyer, "on condition that I may lean against the next. The joke settled the quarrel. LOOKING AFTER THE LIVING. —A certain reverend divine, who had lob,/ enjoyed a very excellent living in the metropols, but who was now on his death-bed, was visited by a young expectant, who modestly inquired after his health. "Ah !" cried the dying clergyman, "you cared little about my health; it is my death you are looking after, young man." "You are mistaken, sir, I assure you, tor I am looking after your living !" was the outspoken reply of the other. THE RULING PASSION. --A couple of zealous anglers were fishing for the first time in a sheet of water rented for the purpose, when one of them, through c ,relessness, fell in. His friend, after great exertion, brought him to land, but it was a long time before he recovered consciGusness. No sooner had he opened his eyes, however, than the other asked him: 'Tell me, did you see a lot of fsli ?" BRIDGET'S LONG-LOST BROTHER. Lady (to cook) : "Bridget, [saw a policeman in the kitchen last night, and, as you know, I don't allow that sort or thing." Bridget: "Shure, he's me own brother, mum." Lady: "Bu:, you told me you hadn't any brother ?" Bridget: "Oi have wan, mum, that Oi didn't think worth mintionin,' fur ;le was lost whin a baby, an' Oi niver set eyes on him ag'in until larsht noight." Lady: "Then how did you recognise him?" Bridget: " Oh, well, mum, Oi was standin' at the door larslit noight, an' Oi saw a thine policeman comin' along, an' some mysterious feelin' seemed to tell me— 'Bridget, there's yer long-lost brother!' To make make cert'in, Oi.axecl him in, and sure enough it was I There's no accountin' fur thim mysterious feelin's, mum I"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>SHOW THIS TO SOME WOMAN.</head>
        <p>SHOW THIS TO SOME WOMAN.</p>
        <p>I will send a valuable. instructive. and highly interesting, book which eve-y woman would be gla,l to have Ccntains important and confidential advice and tells both married and single how to keep halthy ani avoid th anxieties so dfstres,in, to the sex. Sent free, securely sealed, for one stair p to cover postage. A,PII,SS : Professor E. LASALVE, 17, Eastbourne Terrace kaddington, London. W,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>"CAPITAL" CHAT.</head>
        <p>"CAPITAL" CHAT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Labouchere's campaign in South Wales has been a success. South Wales likes strong meat,and the member for Northampton gave plenty of it to his hearers. In fact, in all the advanced Radical constituencies now, as at any time during the past ten years, the cheer has always been for" ending" and absolutely sweeping away the House of Lords and its powers. But while this is the universal sentiment of the workers in the consticuences and the mass of the electors, and for that matter of the whole of the most active elements in the party in the House itself, the tactics of the Parliamentary campaign are irresistibly shaping themselves among those who look ahead and see and wish to avoid all risks of division, and they will be those of the Leeds Conference.</p>
        <p>Nobody, I believe, has attempted to dive from a height of 240 feet until Ben Fuller tried it on Sun- day, and met with his death, to the regret of all who have witnessed his daring feats in the Aquarium. The manner of his striking the water will probably never be known, and it will remain an open question whether he fell a victim to the laws of gravitation on the one side and of vital action on the other, or whether he struck the water in such an awkwardly accidental way as to lead to his receiving internal injuries which prevented him from keeping himself afloat. The distance of the dive daily made at the Aquarium is 1401 t., and was done without the slightest injury to the diver. But the line between safety and death may lie some- where between the 140 ft. and the 240 ft., and the acceleration which, under the law of gravity, takes place during the extra distance is, as we know, enormous.</p>
        <p>The municipal bands in London are becoming so ' popular that it is intended to extend the perform- ances next season if the necessary grant can be obtained. The SUM required to provide music for three months in the parks and open spaces of London is .26,500, or nearly double the amount when the scheme was first started. The increase is, of course, entirely due to the demands of the people for more music. The only places around London where good audiences do not assemble are Black- heath, Eel Brook Common, and Tooting Common, and at these places performances will be discon- tinued. If a gratit of £B,OOO can be obtained the bands are to play in 1895 from May to the end of August. It has even been suggested to continue them through September, but it is felt that the often damp end cold evenings of that month will scarcely warrant an extra expenditure of £l,OOO. Bands on early-closing days, for the most part Wednesdays and Thursdays, are in great request. In consequence of this, next season four sections of the Council's band will play on week-days and three on Sundays; but on Tuesdays and Fridays only two , of the sections will play, so that each section will have one day's leave in seven, whilst there will be music every day.</p>
        <p>In political circles in London the explanation of the Forfarshire figures run on the same lines as un- pleasant rumours heard a fortnight ago. It was then freely stated that the seat was in fact a Unionist seat, and bad been partly thrown away in 1892 by the Unionist themselves, who, for one reason or another, did not give a hearty support to their candidate in that election. A few weeks back when Mr. Robson was new to the constituency, it was hardly expected that he would poll up to Sir John Rigby's figures. Latterly the reports have shown that the Liberal party would practically stand as in 1892, and therefore those who did not look too closely, have hoped for victory. What has happened has not surprised those who knew that Mr. Ramsay was a good candidate in himself, had worked with assiduous geniality in a close personal canvass for two years, and had at his back all the reserve of popularity for his name and connections derived from the generosity of his late brother Lord Dall.ousie. Lord Dalhousie had given away a fortune, almost, in unexampled liberality to farmers and labourers,and his brother has ben able to draw a big political cheque en these personal and family assets. But, making all allowances for these facts,the result is viewed with much disgatis- faction in one respect. It was said that in 1892 we had seen quite the worst of the organised Toryism of the manses. But it is clear from this election, as from the loss of Linlithgowshire in 1893. that the Scotch Establishment has heavy reserves at its disposal.</p>
        <p>Most politicians are full of the new Anglo-Russ- ian departure,and if there is any truth in it,as there seems good ground for believing, there is no event which will have a greater effect upon the inter- national policy of Europe or our international relations in Asia. It is causing the pro- foundest chagrin at Berlin, end probably it is not vet viewed with feelings of unmixed satisfaction by France. It is not attributable to any change of Russian policy on the part of the new Czar, for events were marching in that direction before the late Czar's death ; but the present Ruler of All the Russias is evidently not only friendly to it, but:is warmly accelPrating the political rapprochP- ment of the two countries. It has caused much commotion in Tory clubs, where a friendly under- standing with Russia both in Asia and Europe was regarded as an impossibility, or as only possible by the sacrifice of our dearest safeguards. Matters will not go the length of a Russian alliance any more than they went the length of a German one. If Prince Bismark had been replied to by an Anglo- Russian and French rapprochement he would have been compelled to pause in his irritating and haras- sing policy. Our German attachment has more than once brought us to the verge of war with France, and the utmost dexterity of Lord Rosebery has been required to avoid it. Germany can neither help us nor injure us; and the Prime Minister can put our foreign relations on a basis of close friend- ship with the Powers from which our chief danger romes, he will confer an unspeakable boon upon the whole empire.</p>
        <p>Labouchere's little daughter of eleven years of age is already known to all visitors at Twicken- ham for her precocious elocutionary powers, and her equally precocious acquaintance with Shakes- peare. Whether this child understands Shakespeare when she recites passages from his works is another matter. That she has been subjected to the most careful training as an elocutionist all acknowledge who hear her, either at her father's house or on the platform. Young though she is, she sometimes takes part in a public function, and all who were present at the Catholic Clerkenwell Mission on Tuesday night had an opportunity of listening to and admiring her. She owes her early proficiency to the careful instruction of her mother, whose former experience upon the stage is bearing fruit in the development of the genius of the daughter.</p>
        <p>I am told that the problem of providing a motive power for bicycles and tricycles other than that furrished by the muscles of the riders has been solved. At present its exact form is a secret, but provisional protection has been obtained, and the full rights will be extended to a great many things other than cycles which require a small and inex- pensive power. Applied to bicycles it will be possible to provide power sufficient to drive a machine for forty-eight hours over fair roads at an expenditure of lid. The additional weight carried by each machine will be slight, and it will be possible to disconnect it and use the ordinary means of propulsion. Much greater speed will be attained than is now the case, and the evolution of the cycle will enter on a new phase. Whether it will ' quite an advantage in every respect is not such a certainty.</p>
        <p>IT was too rinicutons. Ater tile weaning, az the bride and bridegroom were going down ths aisle to the door, the organist struck up "Onwerd, Christian soldiers, marching as to war." LADY Julia was complaining about the length of o'ime it took her maid to dress her hair. "A full liwour," she said. "And what do you do all the time ?" asked her friend. "Oh, of course I sit and do nothing." "Well, was the reply, "mine takei nearly as long, but while Jane is doing it in my room, I usually take a turn in the garden." AN ANXIOUS QUESTION.-" Just imagine, Miss Ada ! dreamt last night I went with you on the ice. We skated together. All at once you stumbled, but I caught you in my arms, and gave you a kiss." Miss Ada: "Was—was anybody</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>MOXON'S NURSERY BOON</head>
        <p>MOXON'S NURSERY BOON</p>
        <p>(Unequalled for DESTROYING NITS, and PARASITES in the HUMAN HAIR.) OF CHEMISTS and the PROPRIETO2, J. L. M 0 X N, . • 252, PARK-ROAD. LIVERPOOL. ed. and is. per box. Post Free 7t. and is. Please mention this paver.</p>
