      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>Till? EXAMINER, SATURDAY NOVEMBER 24, 1894. LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>Till? EXAMINER, SATURDAY NOVEMBER 24, 1894. LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MT DEAREST' NELLIE, Fortunately for those who have not much money to lay out on the adornment of their persona, the winter fashions differ from those of last year in no very decided manner, and therefore gowns and jackets which have already done duty for a season can easily be made to pass muster. There is, perhaps, more alteration in the shape of the sleeves than in any other feature, thofei of the newest dresses lying rather flat over the point of the shoulder and bulging out low wn, just above the elbow ; but as sleeves of a os,atrasting material to the rest of the dress are still fashionable, it is neither a difficult nor necepiparLy expensive task to replace the old ones, and so bring a last year's gown quite np to date.</p>
        <p>Suppose, for example, the gown to be remodelled Is a dark cornflower blue fa,,,e cloth or serge, made with revers and hat.que and the full high sleeves in vogue last winter, it might be made to look very smart by the addition of sleeves of plaid silk in subdued shades of green and blue, gathered with several rows of running threads across the point of the shoulder, so that there is no protru- sive fulness there, but standing out balloon fashion below. Then the basque could be removed, the skirt made to hook over the bodice under a wide blue velvet band with large paste buckle in front, and a narrow pleated vest of the plaid silk added, the old cloth revers being faced with velvet, and the plain collar replaced by a folded one of velvet with a flit bow at the back. A stylish jacket for the winter is made of cloth in brown, lined with green, with a collar of a particularly becoming shape, edged with Alaska smoked fox, that semi- fitting in front with a large button below the waist,</p>
        <p>Vhbous vie vn, velveb for popularity in mil- linery. Strip.ul rilJiona are pretty made up in bows for hat,, coothin,td with wings or flowers. A black hat bad its trimruing entirely composed of a huge bow of pink awl blank striped ribbon, with a little bird of bright,t I)urriaga perchcd on the side. A beautiful hat I nave just purchased has a crinkled brim of oneniile mar! straw interplaited, with a large loose crown of velvet aid a paradise plume buult led with steel et caws aide.</p>
        <p>In jackets the shapes and styles are legion. The newest open-fronted coat is fashioned with a quite short undulating basque, and will be found es- pecially becoming to short and medium-sized figures, while it is especially well adapted to wear- ing beneath a longer, outdoor coat or wrap. Capes are getting longer, but short people still cling to the short and dainty full styles edged with Thibet or fox. Grebe far is being made into neek-bands with a smart bow at the back, en! it is a favourite trimming on some choice garments for children. A handsome little girl's coat I have just seen is in turquoise-blue Zibelline cloth, with beaver edging and heavy buttons, and a bonnet to match, with loops of turquoise bebe velvet and double strings.</p>
        <p>Blouses are still much worn, and are to be seen In a bewildering variety of materials and styles. Very smart are those of velveteen with wide epaulet-revers turning back from a vest of soft silk or coarse lace over satin, and a capon or cashmere skirt with one of these pretty bodices would make up a nice evening costume quite suitable for ordinary wear. One of the bees shapes in these blouses has a short basque, the fulness of which is arranged in open box-pleats, the velvet belt being finished in front with two rosettes, while the vest Is V-shaped and has a folded velveteen neck-band with a couple of tiny rosettes, and the sleeves have a big loose puff from shoulder to elbow, and thence are cloas-fittiue to the wrist. Flannel blouses are exceedingly useful to wear with last year's serge or tweed skirts, and are, perhaps, most popular in checks and subdued tartans than in plain colours. A comfortable shape is pleated back and front into E. small yoke, and has deep epaulets of flannel edged with a little fancy braid, and velvet collar, cuffs, and belt, while a bodice suitable for milder days is of wool crepon with a full pleated front and braces of wide ve.vet ribbon tied in bows on the shoulders. The basque of this last blouse is worn beneath the skirt, and the wide belt is of folded crepon. Yours lovingly, J ENNIE.</p>
        <p>MUCH BETTER THAN 'STEEL AND PENNYROYAL OTTBY'S UNLABELLED</p>
        <p>STRONG FEMALE PILLS. quickly and Certainly remove all obstructions arising from any cause whatever. Invaluable for married women. Post free, under cover for 14, and 33 stamps. from</p>
        <p>THOMAS OTTEY, BURTON-ON-TRENT P:aase mention MI R napPr</p>
        <p>C'hQmist.</p>
        <p>Only cue out of every thousand married couples live to celebrate their golden wedding,. The healthiest children in the world dwell in the Scottish Islands. They seldom wear shoes before they are twelve years of age. According to a recent writer, women in Korea are permitted to stay obt till midnight, while men are required to be in the house at 8 p.m. Only married men are permitted to wear hats.</p>
        <p>Miss Hughes (Telynores Menai) has been engaged as the principal harpist at the forthcoming National Eisteddfod at Llanelly. Miss Hughes is at present studying at the Royal Academy of Music. Princess Sapeha, of Werkliany, province of Viteb3k, a Russian landed proprietress, has established at her own expense a free eating-house for poor Jews, and has ordered her steward to employ Jews as agricultural labourers upon the same terms as Christians. Miss Blanche Lewis, of Ballinagar, bas just succeeded in taking her M.A. at the Royel University of Ireland. Miss Lewis was educated primarily at Malvern Wells, afterwards going to the Alexandra College Dublin, where she took both B.A. and M.A.</p>
        <p>Miss Dorothea Klumpke, the Californian lady who has attracted much attention lately in Paris for her work in astronomical research, has been made a Doctor of Mathematics by the University of the Sorbonne, after passing an excellent examination. This degree is now for the first time conferred upon a woman in France. Miss Klumpke is only twenty-eight years old. Miss McCormick, a young Californiaß artist, who has long felt that in her own case, at least, there was need for dress reform, has by .gradual changes accustomed the inhabitants of her village home—Pacific Grove—to short skirts without giving a violent shock to their prejudices. From the ordinary length she reduced the skirts half an inch daily, until they barely reached the knee. Leggings of russet leather or corduroy cover the lower part of her legs, and her costunses, wl4ich are tailor-made, are becoming, and even graceful. Such a reform suit is especially convenient to Miss McCormick when she is engaged in painting cattle.</p>
        <p>Where Tact Lies. Visiting our sick friends—though it may be much easier thaa a mourning visit—yet demands a good deal of intuition. Sick people are, says " Cassell's Family Magazine," often cross and irritable,old age is exacting; and we must make allowance for these failings. One cannot have a better example of want of tact than is given in the well-known story of Mr. and Mrs. Carlyle. Mr. Carlyle, as everybody knows, was immersed in his work, and did not very often see his wife when she was ill. She was suffering from violent facial neuralgia, which prevented her shutting her mouth properly. Mr. Carlyle meant well, but "be disliked an open mouth ; he thought it a sign of foolishness." So he said : "Jane, yell find yerself in a more compact and Pious frame of mind if ye shut your mouth." Almost second to this in tactlessness is a story of a lady who visited a little sick cripple-girl of fifteen. She looked at the child with tears in her eye;, and evidently wished to say something kind, but knew not how. At last she took the bull by tha horns. "Poor little girl !" she said ; " how sad for you to have to sit still while others run about and play ! But you are resigned, aren't you ?" Now, this was about the most unfortunate speech that could have been made. The subject of her affliction had never been by any chance mentioned either by the chile or by her friends. It was a fact that could only be made worse by being talked of. The reference was cruel, and most unintentionally so on the part of the visitor, who, happening to be her- self the mother of nine children, ought to have known better. But, as Charles Lamb somewhere says, the real maternal felling is not always—indeed, not oftenest—to be found amongst mothers of large families. A lonely spinster, with real sympathy,would not have made such a blunder as the foregoing.</p>
        <p>THE DOUBLE LIFE: OR, THE HAMPTON MYSTERY.</p>
        <p>By the Author of " DARKWOOD."</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XLII. LORD TREVELYAN IN TOWN.</p>
        <p>Alas the brest that inly bleeds Rath naught to dread from outward blow. BYRON. The Lady Beatrice Hampton sat in a languid attitude in the drawing-room of her father's town house on the afternoon of the same day on which occurred the memorable encounter between Lord Adlowe and the spirited housekeeper of Trevelyan</p>
        <p>Park. Her ladyship was elegantly arrayed in an inde- scribable home costume, made up of shimmering silk and lace and jewels. The expression of her beautiful features was cold and haughty and impas- sive. She was half reclining in an easy chair, and her position commanded a view of the street. Her father, the earl, sat near the fire facing her, a sorrowful look on his proud and handsome face. They had been together an hour or more, chatting lei,urely in a desultory manner, but latterly a silence had fallen 'between them, during which Lord Hampton studied secretly his daughter's face. "Beatrice," he said, at last, "why do you always wear a mask, even to me ? " "A mask, father ? " said the Lady Beatrice, turning her face towards him in questioning sur- prise. "Yes, Beatrice. Even now as you sat looking out of the window, seemingly quite forgetful of me, you did not relax for an instant the strict guard you always keep over your soul, lest it should be- 'tray you through. your features." "Dear father," said the Lady Beatrice, gently, "1 have given you a great deal of trouble, tirst and last, have I not?" Not trouble, Beatrice; yet I own that your in- explicable conduct has greatly grieved me. Will you nevei he frank with me, my child ? " He spoke with an unwonted tenderness, his sor- row on her account visible in every hue of his noble face. "I can (fly say trust in me, father," she an- swered, in a low voice. "Some day, all the mys- tery of my life shall be made plain to you. I have a secret, I own it. Just now my heart is sore, very sore, and I would like rest and peace." A shadow swept over her perfect features--a shadow of wild unrest, of agonising mental pain, of utter anguish of soul. It was the first glimpse Lord Hampton had had below the surface of her polished manners and icy demeanor for years. He shrank back, surprised and wondering. "Beatrice Beatrice !" he said, a moment later, "let me share your sorrow. I see that under all the ice of your exterior an /Etna is burning. Con- fide in me, your father." The momentary spasm was passed, and the Lady Beatrice had resumed her coldness as a mask. Lord Hampton saw that he might as well plead to a statue as plead to her now for her confidence. • "Beatrice," he exclaimed, abruptly, putting away the memory of her sudden weakness to be considered thereafter, "do you know what peo- ple are saying, in the clubs and in society ?" "I do not know, I am sure," said the Lady Bea- trice, langt idly. "Talking over Captain Nes- myth's elopement with Mrs. Landon, perhaps. No? Lady Belford's forthcoming ball, then ?" "They are saying that the haughty Lady Bea- trice Hampton is conquered at last ; that, in short she is betrothed to Lord Adlowe, and that the marriage will soon transpire." "It is strange how people love gossip," ob- served the Lady Beatrice, philosophically. "In this case, doubtless, they have warrant for their talk. Lord Adlowe, without doubt, purposely put the report in circulation." "But you gave him reason to, Beatrice. You are betrothed to him ?" "No, father, I refused him again and again, but he would not accept my decision. And finally to get rid of him, I told him I would take time for consideration and let him know ray final decision when he should again ask for it. But as to marry- ing hitn,l never had the slightest intention of doing 50.,,</p>
        <p>"Beatrice, I must repeat what I said before— your conduct is inexplicable." The Lady Beatrice was silent. "I am to understand, I suppose," said the earl, "that you are as far as ever'from having any inten- tion of being married ?" "As far from such an intention as I have been</p>
        <p>_ during the last seventeen or eighteen years," replied the Lady Beatrice. "I see. You are keeping single from a linger- ing affection for that hated Geoffrey Trevelyau 1" cried the earl, arising and walking excitedly to and fro. "lie has blighted your life, Beatrice; be has ruined your happiness; he has transformed you from a warm-hearted, impulsive girl into a cold and passionless woman! 1 share his uncle's hatred for his memory." At this moment he paused, catching sight in a long pier-glass of a woman's figure in the second drawing-room in the shadow of the sweeping velvet hangings. lie looked at it keenly and wonderingly, and recognised it as the figure of Mrs. Fleck, his daughter's tiring-woman. Mrs. Fleck, unconscious of his scrutiny, and believing herself securely hidden from his lord- ship's observation, was making gestures to her mistress, beckoning to her, and waving a letter to attract her attention. _ _</p>
        <p>The Lady Beatrice, noting the cessation of her father's walk, regarded him curiously, noted the direction of his glances, and detected the figure and movements of her tiring-woman. She started, and a deeper pallor reigned in her pure cheeks. "Come in Fleck'," she said, composedly, retain- ing her wonderful self-command. "You have a letter for me ?" Mrs. Fleck advanced from her concealment, seeming greatly confused, and brought the it tter to her mistress. "Bring it on a salver next time, Fleck," said the Lady Beatrice, languidly taking the missive, and dismissing her servitross with a nod. The eyes of the two—mistress and maid—met in a gaze that seemed to the suspicious earl deeply significant, and then Mrs. Fleck withdrew, and the Lady Beatrice glanced at the suneiscription of her letter. As she gazed the red blood swept up into her cheeks. The address was the one she had coined for Giralda's use, and the handwriting was Gir- alda's. The earl watched her sharply. Conscious of his scrutiny, she tore open the mis- sive, and quietly tore the envelope into shreds, thrusting them into her pocket. "A letter of invitation, Beatrice?" asked her father. "No; one of friendship," replied her ladyship, unfolding the letter, and settling herself for its perusal. "If you will excuse me, father, I will read it." " Certainly ; certainly. Read on, Beatrice," ex- claimed the earl, keeping a jealous regard upon her. "I am anxious to know which of our fashionable friends it is who has chosen to write to you so secretly, and to get the missive to you in such an unprecedented manner through your tiring. woman. Doubtless a love-letter." And he uttered a forced and painful laugh. The Lady Beatrice flushed again, and coolly be- gan the perusal of her letter. The next moment her father and his suspicions were alike forgotten. She was absorled in the Contents of Giralda's letter, with its glad news of Fay's recovery. A bright glow like sunshine radiating from her soul transfigured her features. Her eyes became shining weal of warmth and light. A smile, rich and warm and tender, brooded on her scarlet lips. She looked like one to whom some great joy has come in the Jidda of a drear sadness—like one upon whom a glaaseme morning has dawned after a L. telit of fearful misery. "Safe ! safe !" she murmured, so softly that the low murmur could not he distinguished by her listening father. "Thank God, he is safe !" The light and glow on her face deepened into an expression of the most ineffa.ble peace. The earl watched her in amazement. He had not deemed her capable of feeling like this. He beau to think that the mystery which he had believed to exist was wider and deeper than he had yet conceived. "Beatrice I" he said, gently. His vioce seemed to blend with her happy thoughts. She half turned her head and looked up at him with a smile that warmed his soul. "Beatrice," he said, and his voice choked, " it seems as if my true-hearted, impulsive child had come back from the past when I look at you now Your old self is not dead, as I thought—only masked. Beatrice, be again what you sere befoie that accursed Geoffrey Trevelyan crossed your Dath---"</p>
        <p>He paused, affrighted at the change his mention of that name wrought in her. The light and the glow and the warmth all faded like the hues of sunset from the sky, and a wintry</p>
        <p>coldness and pallor succeeded. She put the letter in her bosom, and resumed her listless cud languid attitude. _ _</p>
        <p>1 he earl was confused and bewildered. "You are a strange woman, Beatrice," he said, -in a troubled voice. "I wish your unknown cor- respondent would have seen you but now. Ile would have been enraptured." The Lady Beatrice smiled coldly. "You have a lover, then, Beatrice?" asked the earl, nettled by the sudden change in her. "You have a suitor whom you favour ? ' "Father, I shall never marry," was the quiet but decided answer. "I shall always remain as I . am now.'</p>
        <p>The earl was about to protest angrily, when the Lady Beatrice glanced from the window, her atten- tion attracted by the sound of wheels. A cab was just drawin6 up before the mansion, and while her ladyship looked, a colossal figure, majestic in all its proportions, and crowned by a grand head surrounded with gray curls, emerged from the cab, and ascended the steps of Hampton House.</p>
        <p>"Lord Trevelyan, father," she said. Lord Hampton hastened at once to the door to meet and greet his old friend. They came into the drawing-room together, arm in arm.</p>
        <p>The Lady Beatrice arose to meet the visitor, welcoming him with a regal grace. The marquis was not looking near so well as usual. It seemed as though a great sorrow had fallen upon him. His haughty, imperious face bore . token of a great and present grief. He looked subdued and sorrowful, yet, withal, impatient of affliction. "As blooming and beautiful as ever, Lady Bea- trice !" he said, with the courtliness of a French officer of the old regime, which he so much re- sembled. "Time does not touch you, it seems, save to glorify. But he is hard on us, is he not, Hampton ?" he added, turning to his friend. "Yes, he is hard on us I" responded Lord Hamp- ton, adding, with some bitterness: "but as you say, he deals gently with Beatrice. Troubles touch bar lightly, Trevelyan. If she is really as placid. 'as she seems her face will always be fair and. un- seamed with wrinkles." "Yet she has known trouble years ago," said the marquis, with unwonted gentleness. "It is her splendid physical health that keeps her so fresh. I was sorry, my dear Lady Beatrice not to see you during my recent visit to town; but I was obliged to return hastily to the Park by reason of an attack of gout. I am quite recovered now 1" _ _</p>
        <p>He sat down in the easy chair by the fire, and warmed his hands at the blaze. _ _ .</p>
        <p>"You are in town on a visit, Trevelyan ?" asked the earl, "or on business ?•' "Business solely, my lord. I came up to see the Lady Beatrice and yourself, and to discover an opera-singer, or theatre actress, whose proper name is supposed to be the Countess of Arevalo. Can you give me any information in regard to _</p>
        <p>her - "I never even beard the name," said the earl. "The name is odd," said the Lady Beatrice. "It has a Spanish sound. Like my father, I can give your lordship no information about her." Lord Trevelyan's countenance fell. "I want to see her concerning her daughter, a lovely young girl whom I adopted as my neice," he said, his features working with sudden emotion. "The young lady has left my house. I presume she has returned to her mother." The Lady Beatrice seemed interested. "The young lady proved unworthy ?" she mid, questioningly. " No—a thousand times, no! She is an angel! I made a discovery about her, and drove her from my house as if she had been a dog—brute that I 'was! I want her back again! I must have her back I shall die without her ! "cried the old lord, stormily. "I shall have her back if I have to search the wide earth for her I" The Lady Beatrice half concealed her face with her tan. "Your discovery must have been important if it caused you to cast her off on the impulse of the moment," she observed. "It concerned her I arentage," said the marquis: "The innocent child could not help it if her father is a black-hearted scoundrel. She does not inherit or partake of his vileness, my poor, little wronged angel !" and he groaned dismally. "Who was her father ? " asked the earl. The marquis did not seem to heed the ques- tion. He was looking at the Lady Beatrice pity- ingly. "Beatrice," he said abruptly, "do you love Or- mond Adlowe ? " The Lady Beatrice looked startled, and blushed. "Love Lord Adlowe !" she said, with the indig- nation of her wifely nature—the second half of her dual life—aroused. " No, Lord Trevelyan. To the contrary, I dislike and despise him." Lord Trevelyan looked relieved. " Another question, Beatrice," he said, with grave kindness. "Do you still love my unworthy nephew—that hound, Geoffrey ?" The Lady Beatrice averted her face, but the two who looked at her could see the blood had receded from the surface, leaving it startingly pale. "I cannot answer you, Lord Trevelyan," she said, in a stifled voice. A deathly faintness stole over her. She believed that Lord Adlowe had made known to his uncle his discoveries at Ihe Evergreens, and that the marquis had come to denounce her to her father. It seemed to her that an iron hand was clenched about her heart, clutching it in a fierce and tighten- ing grip. Her fan fell into her lap. All her powers of self-command could not conquer the mute terror that now enthralled her. "1 pity you, Beatrice Hampton, if you still love that wretch," said Lord Trevelyan, solemnly. "You have mourned him as dead. You have wept for him. You have kept yourself single out ot respect to his memory, true-hearted, noble-souled woman that you are. You would not believe in his baseness, though I swore to it. And now behold your rewttrit ! Prepare yourself for a shock, Bea- trice. Geoffrey Trevelyan lives !" The Laity Beatrice sprang up as if galvanised, a harp cry shivering on her lips. She had been so wrong ht up, that only thus could she relieve her over-tasked soul. She looked at the marquis wildly. • His lordship arose and took her hand. "Yes Beatrice," he said, still solemnly, "Geof- frey Trevelyan lives, fie is in England." The white hand fluttered in his grasp. The wild eyes looked pleadingly and apprehensively into his.</p>
        <p>"Can you bear more, Beatrice? Then listen. He has not been faithful, like you, to his vows of betrothal. He is married to an actress—this Countess of Arevalo, of whom I spoke." "Geoffrey living and Married ! ' ejaculated the earl. "Incredible ! Have you seen him, Trevel- yan ? "No. But I have seen his wife, when I did not know her to be his wife. She is a large, fair- haired woman, with liMg curls, a German-looking beauty, of imposing presence." • The earl uttered an ejaculation the others neither heard nor heeded. The last speech of the marqu,s seemed to have removed a great load from his soul. "You saw his wife, my lord, and spoke with her? " he asked, as if musingly. " I did. He has children. This girl, Beatrice, this Giralda, is his child, as innocent as I once deemed her, as pure and sinless as an angel! I I've her as I loved him. I cling to her in my old age. I will have her back !" The Lady Beatrice drooped her head lowly. "And for the sake of this eirl you will take her father back also?" she asked.. in a hushed voice. "You will forgive him for heL sake?" • The old lord shook his head as a lion shakes his inane. "Never, never!" lie cried, his eyes flashing. "I can never clasp the hand of my would-be assassin. I can never sleep under the same roof with him. But for Giralda's sake I will forego a public prose- cution of him. I will not bring upon him the punishment he so richly deserves. If he will give me hack Giralds„ I will let him live in peace in his late obscurity. But let him not speak to me of forgiveness, or friendship, or reconciliation." The head of the Lady Beatrice dropped still lower. "But if he be innocent, as I have thought?" she suggested, faintly. " Ah, Beatrice, wronged, double wronged, you would plead for him still ! " cried the marquis. "Listen to me. I swear that in the day that Geof- frey Trevelyan proves his innocence of attempting my mercier—aid remember I caught him in the very act, knife in hand—in that day and hour I will fork4ive him. Forgive him ! I will plead to him for liJ.rdou for my persecutions of him. I will get 11On my kucos to him. Tiit. till then let him and tam scheming actress wife keep clear of my Lath. I will let them abore wily on condition that</p>
        <p>they keep clear of me," and the Old lord stretched out his arm in menace, and shook himself in leonine wrath. "They planned, as Adlowe said, to entrap me. They made that innocent child the instrument of their vile plots. I hate them both with a lasting hatred." He relinquished the hand of the Lady Beatrice, who resumed her seat. Her emotions, crowding upon each other, had been too much for her, and she sank back on her cushions, and with a iittl• gasp fainted dead away. (To be continued).</p>
