      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>SOUTHEND MURDER.</head>
        <p>THE SOUTHEND MURDER.</p>
        <p>So far as the. nature of Read's appeal to the Home Secretary is known to the public, he and his friends do not rest upon an alibi at all, but on the circumstantial nature of the evidence and the absence of proofs as to how he spent his time in Southend during the Sunday of the murder. Yet the other day his brother was boasting that he j would submit to the Home Secretary the most, con- vincing proof that Read was 50 miles distant from Southend on the day of the tragedy. If this can be proved tu the satisfaction of Mr. Asquith, the condemned man need not trouble himself either about the circumstantial nature of the evidence or its defects. Although he was unable to give evi- dence on his own behalf in court, the Evidence in Criminal Cases Bill not being yet passed, be ought still to have been able to produce witnesses who could prove his whereabouts on that fatal night. even though he did not wish to compromise any other unfortunate victim who had fallen a prey to him. . Upon the testimony furnished to the court the acquiescence of the public in the justness of the verdict has been absolutely unanimous. There has not been a single murmur raised in the hnmani- tarian camp in favour of Read, who, if he is inno- cent, as he asserts, has but to open his mouth rind account for twelve hours of his existence, and the prison bars will fall back before him. GENERAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>The Czar has an income of two and a half millions per annum. The sum of six hundred and fifty thousand pounds is now what the Prince of Wales is insured for. In time of War. France puts 370 out of every 1,000 of her population in the field ; Germany, 310 ; Russia, 210. Within twenty-four hours from the reception of the news of the Czar's death, no fewer than seven songs, evoked by the event, were being sung and sold in the streets of Paris. A medical scientist states that dismal weather has a bad effect upon the reasoning powers as well as upon the spirits. He says the deductions made on cloudy days often prove to he faulty.</p>
