      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>DEATH OF A BALACLAVA HERO.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF A BALACLAVA HERO.</p>
        <p>We have to record, with regret, this week the death of a well-known character in Penketh, Ser- geant James Donoghue. He was one of the gallant Six Hundred who took part in the famous charge of the Light Brigade at Balaclava. He has been a resident in Penketli for some yesr3 and was well respected by all who knew him. He passed away on Saturday night. He has also been a well known figure in Warrington,he having been bandmaster to several bands until his age prevented him from con- tinuing this employment. We append a sketch of his career in the army. Sergeant J. Donoghue, late of the Bth H.R.I. Hussars, has been in the army all his lile, having: been born in the year 1829 in the sth of Wales Dragoon Guards at Canterbury, Kent. When a boy he joined the 2nd Battalion 60th K.R. Rifles. at Belturbert, County Cavan, Ireland, 18th of May, 1844. He served with that distinguished regiment until the Ist June, 1848, when he was transferred to the regiment of his two brothers who were serving in the Bth Hussars. The three brothers left Exeter in April, 1854 for Turkey. Sergeant Donoghue was field trumpeter to the Earl of Cardigan, at the reconnoissance to Silistria, and at the affair of Bulganalc,field trumpeter at the Battle of Alma, and at the taking of MacKenzie's farm. He was field trumpeter at the Light Cavalry Charge at Balaclava. His horse was shot in the charge, and under a very heavy fire he took all his kit from the back of his fa- vourite horse, and after a very narrow escape made his way back to his regiment. He was field trumpeter at the battle of Inkermann and also at the taking of Kerte!' and Yenikale. He was also present at the Tcher- naya. He was orderly to Sir Colin Campbell, doing duty in front of Sebastepol, and was present at the taking of that fort. He and his two brothers returned home with the remnant of the regiment May, 1856, but went out again in 1857 with the troops sent out for the suppression of the Indian Mutiny. Sergeant Donoghue was at the taking of Kootah, and was one of the Flying Expedition, which for eleven days pursued the enemy, who had made their escape from Kootah. The expedi- tion brought back with them many prisoners and guns. Donoghue was sun struck on the march to Gwalior, and was sent home and discharged, August, 1859, on a pen- sion of Bd. per day ,leaving his two brothers,with whom he had ridden knee to knee in many a wild charge, in India Sergeant Donoghue wore on his breast the fol- lowing decorations—Crimean medal and clasps for Alma, Balaclava, Inkermann, and Sebastopol, Turkish medal, French Cross of the Legion of Heuour, medal for dis- tinguished conduct, Indian Mutiny medal, and clasp for Central India. Since his discharge Sergeant Donoghue has earned a living in different ways being but poorly assisted by the magnificent sum of Bd. per day from the Government. The condition of the survivors of that heroic charge has been brought forward at different times by men wl.o knew well their subjects,but the same justice to a nation's heroes has still been persevered in, and the remaining few of a band of heroes pats away un- aided by the country for which they risked their lives and all. Lord Cardigan's word to the survivors of the neble Six Hundred on the morning following the charge were : "Men, you have done a glorious deed : England will be proud of you, and grateful to you. If you live to get home, be sure you will be well provided for. Not one of you fine fellows will ever have to seek refuge in the woikhouse." No doubt the noble lord fully meant what he said, hut, alas, he was not the only one to be consulted. The number of survivors known to be ex- treme poverty in 1890 was 22, and since then several have passed away some from age and want, and various diseases. Several were inmates of workhouses. Of enurse there were several who were comfortably pro- vided for, and had good situations, but the poor broken down heroes who were unable to do hard work were left by the Government to exist like Donoghue on 81. per day. We Britons are undoubtedly a very generous people and pay lots of money every year for the support of royalty, but your real heroes, when hacked and hewn with perilous service, and worn out with age, are consigned to garrets, the workhouse and oblivion. Well might Tennyson exclaim— When can their glory fade, 0 the wild charge they made, All the world wondered. Honour the charee they made, Honour the Light Brigade, Noble Six Hundred."</p>
        <p>3 MATCHLESS CLEANSER has swept away Athousand worries of Washing Day.—ADVT.</p>
