      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>BETTING PROSECUTION AT WARRINGTON. HEAVY PENALTIES: £75 INCLUDING COSTS.</head>
        <p>BETTING PROSECUTION AT WARRINGTON. HEAVY PENALTIES: £75 INCLUDING COSTS.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Court, on Friday, before Mr.F. Monks (chairman), Aldermen Harrison and Wright, Councillor Pierpoint, Dr. Gornall, and Messrs. S. Chandley and S. Wright, Mrs. Mather, 17, Friar's-green, was charged on remand with keeping a house for the purposes of betting,under the mean- ing of the Betting Act, on November Bth,and divers other dates ; William Mather and Annie Mather were charged with using the house for betting, and Charles Gough, 5, River-road • Michael Barry, 5, Bakehouse- yard ; Wm. Thomas, 10, New-st. ; Martin Jordan, 43, Anne-st. ; George Newton, 37, Laira-st. ; and Thomas Brookes, 15, Laira-st., were charged with aiding and abetting. Mr. Collingwood Hope, barrister, Liverpool, instructed by Mr.J. Lyon Whittle (town cleA).appeared to prosecute on behalf of the police and Mr. Blackwood Wright, barrister, Liverpool, instructed by Mr. Percy Davies, appeared on behalf of the defendants Mather, and Mr. Arthur Browne appeared on behalf of Michael Barry. Mr. Mulholland had been engaged for the de- fence, but he was unable to attend,owing to business at the Liverpool Assizes. Mr. Collingwood Hope in opening the cane for the prosecution, said that Mr. Wright had adopted a course which he was glad to say would save the time of the court, and also save a good deal of inconvenience. The Bench would see that there were three defendants, Mrs. Mather, the mother; William Mather, the son; and Annie Mather, the daughter. The course adopted was to plead guilty on behalf of the mother and son. With regard to the daughter, she was only a young girl, and although he would have been in a position to prove that she had taken an active part in the betting carried on, having reeard to her age, and the position she occupied in the house, he proposed on behalf of the police to offer no evidence against her, and the charge vrculd, there- fore, be withdrawn. Had the case been gone into fully he would have shown on behalf of the police the charac- ter of the house. Mrs. Mather at the time of the cap- ture was the occupier of the house, and was down as such on the books of the Corporation. This house had been continuously under the eyes of the police for some months past, although they had been hindered very much in proceeding by the action of certain ill-advised persons who had no doubt the good of the town at heart rid who had prevented the matters goingon in their or- dinary course. The place had been under the espionage of the police—he used this word, although it might be subject to misinterpretation—and they had been formu- lating their scheme until it had arrived at its climax. He would have been able to show by the evidence of Inspector Spinks that from betting books of the defend- ants during the last six or eight months, there had been 14,742 bets registered. The place had also been watched on certain dates, and 189 persons had been seen to enter the house in three hours. Of course, that was the out- side figure, but 40 people had been seen to go in to the house in half-an-hour. He mentioned those facts as showing the extensive nature of the business which was carried on, and he asked the Bench to take this into consideration when considering the penalty which they might inflict. The prosecution was taken under the Betting Act of 1853,and the penalty was £lOO and six months imprisonment, and also the costs of the prosecution. He could have shown that bets had been made at divers dates during the past six months, and to come to a concrete instance, bets were made by Miss Mather—against whom the charge had been withdrawn—William Mather and Mrs. Mather. On that date, Mr. Hunt effected a raid which -was cleverly and thoroughly carried out. The three defendants were in the room at that time, and the other six defendants about whom be did not propose to sly anything, as they were satisfied with getting the chief persons. The bets were being registered by Mrs. Mather and sl,e had also a considerable sum of money, some £6 151. In reply to a question, she had said, " We burn all the telegrams we receive." Wm. Mather was at the head of the table taking bets and receiving money, and carrying en the ordinary work of a bookmaker. He was in possessien of 13s. 10d. in copper, and this was of some importance as showing the-character of the bets, and the class of men who registered bets in the house. The sum of £lO us. 9d. in silver was on the sofa beside him, and ain iold was found in his pocket. In a safe in the room there was also the large sum of £79 in gold, and £lO in notes. There was also the usual paraphernalia of a betting establishment consisting of 200 printed betting tickets, 43 telegrams, and 100 printed slips re- lating to betting. He need not go further into detail, except to say that the prosecution was in possession of proof that the place was a betting establishment on a large scale as shown by the number of bets registered in their books. He hoped that the magistrates would im- pose a penalty in accordance with the serious nature of the charge. Mr. Blackwood Wright then addressed the court for the defence. He said that he took the course he had adopted because he had been influenced by the fact that he did not wish to see the daughter brought up and con-</p>
        <p>victed. He wished their worships to remember that the father of the defendants William and Annie, died on April 4th of the present year. Soon afterwards the son was made the agent of Mr. John Whittle, of Bolton and Manchester, for betting purposes. As their worships would understand, neither of the defendants could have been using the house for betting purposes for a long time, because the house, until April 4th of the present year, was not their ',curse but the house of their father who had since died. They would also understand that betting could not have been carried on immediately after the father's death, in fact he was instructed that it was only sometime afterwards that the son was appointed agent to Mr. Whittle. The son was under the impression that betting was only unlawful in public- houses, and was therefore mistaken in the law. He was aware that it was no excuse to be ignorant of the law, but in the punishment which the bench might inflict upon him they should consider that question. With regard to the mother he would say that the father hav- ing died on April 4th, the son, as heir-at-law, succeeded to the house. They would, therefore, understand that the mother would have very little control over her son at all. It was to save her daughter the disgrace of being brouabt into the case that he had advised the mother to plead aunty. Be was perfectly aware that Mrs. Mather's name was in the ratr book as the occupier of the house, but the bench woold understand that there were certain arlya.ut-aaea having her name as th occupier. There would then be no serving on juries,and a number of other advantages, r privileffes might he secured when a person's name was not on the rate-book. The son had acted as agent for someone outside War- rinaton, and the mother could not very well stop him, and he honed, therefore, that their worships would—if they did inflict a penalty—not infliet a heavy punish- ment. The son was perfectly willing to undertake all responsibility. Mr. Arthur Browne said that he was not quite clear as to the course Mr. 'Hope was going to take with regard to the other defendants. He wished to know, as he repre- sented Michael Barry.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hone replied that he would withdraw the charge against all of them. The six men were accordingly allowed to leave the court. The mavistrates then retired to consider their decision and after twenty minutes' abgsnce, they returned, and Mr. Monlts sail that the Bench had fully considered the -question, and they reeng.nised what the counsel had said that the legislature considered the offence to he a serions one. They also recognised that the defendants pleaded guilty, which was an admission on their Part that they had set the law at defiance and crone contrary to the law of the country. They also took into account what the counsel for the defence had said, and also the fact</p>
        <p>that it was dpfprvlants' first anpearancP rnattpr_of</p>
        <p>that kin&amp; They were thPrefore not, inclinea to inflict - the full penaltv. They w,•ulrl fine Mrs. Mather, and Win. Mather. 75 ench inchrlinr! engts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>CITRYSkNTTIF,MUM SHOW ANT) CONVERSAZIONE AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>CITRYSkNTTIF,MUM SHOW ANT) CON- VERSAZIONE AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Floral and llorticultural Societ9 is one of the most recently founded institutions in Warrington, hut since its inception it has proved to be not only a most useful society for the promo- tion of the culture of flowers in Warrington and district, hut it has provided the public with oppor- tunities for inapee.ting flowera and plants grown in the town and district by weans of exhibitions. The successful e.h7vsanthemum show held under the auspices of the Society in the Warrington Gymnasium on Wednesday mugt have been very gratifcing to the Promoters. The number of entries was perhaps not so largess could he wished for: hut, the plants on view were in first-cla.se condition. There were five entries in the amateur class for the hest twelve pots exhibited. The number of plants required may have acted an a deterrent ta a number of growers who might otherwise have exhibited as it is not in the rawer of many amateurs to bring from their small greenhouses a dozen fine looking pots of chrysanthemums. The 111CIPT for the amateur class were Messrs. coo jor anti .T. Lockhart and their awards were Ist prize, Mr. Jos. Grounds, 2nd prize. Mr. Jog. Feesom. and 3rd prize. Mr. John Randle. Certificates of merit were awarded to the other com- petitors, Messrs. JAin Rh 7 and A. Rowlinsnn. The amateurs' exhibit, were placed along the left 10.nd side of the building, while on the right, there weg a fine array of chrysanthemums from ladies and gentlemen in the town and district but who did not enter into the competition. Near till doorway, Alderman Frarcomb exhibited a small csllectirm of cut chrysanthemums and near to. a group of plants grown in the park was shown by the Corporation, Mr. Parker, head gardener, being in charge. There were also chrysanthemum exhibits by Mrs. W. Long, Thelwall Heves, Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., Mr. R. Garnett. J.P., Mr. C. T. Bolton, Penketh, and Mr. Boond, Lymm -The group of plants shown by Sir Gilbert Greenall. which were in cbarge of the head gardener (Mr. Lockhart), was by far the largest and best collection in the room, anti included many rare and beautifully tinted blooms. At the far end of the room Alderman Bolton displayed a collection of orchids, and the centre of the room was occupied with various shrubs</p>
        <p>and plants. Refreshments were provided in an ante- room, and in one corner ot the large room a phonogragh was provided. There was a fair attehdance at the open- ing ceremony in the afternoon. Amongst those present were the Mayor (Councillor Fairolonz i), the Mayoress and Miss Fairclough, Mr. and Mrs. F. Minks and the Misses Monks, Mr. R. Garnett, J P., Alder man and Mrs. Bolton, Councillor Smethurst, Mr. J Fulton Smith (treasurer), Mr. James lica,n (secretary) Mr. W. Skelton, Mr. and Mrs. G. Dakin, Mr. Cuorley,Mrs.Long, Mrs. Yonge, Mrs. Down, Mrs. F tirhurst, Mrs. Pilking ton, and others. Alderman BOLTON (chairman of the committee) said he had much pleasure in ~p.ming that Chry,an'hemum Show. He thought they would ail understand that it was not being held from a financial point of view, but simply to keep the aro Ners of the district in touch with the society. Everything there that dty had been lent, and if the phblic would give thi.hn credit for what they had done, they were quite satisfied. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>THE CONVARSAZIONE. The show was opened un'il six o'clock when the doors were closed. At seven o'clock the doors were re-opened for the evening's convereazione. Tv swas a pronounced success both from a numerical and music d p Unt of view Amongst those present in addition to ,nest, of those already mentioned above there were Alderman Fran- comb, Alderman Hutchinson, Rev. J. Warnoek, M.A., B.D. Dr. Gornall. Captaie Buaktee, Birehati, J.P., Unsworth, Willett, G. F. F. Whi.e, S. Wilson, Fergusson, R. Wyllie, Dimtnelow, , and o- hers. Prior to the commencement of the programme proper, several orchestral selections were performed be an orchestral band of about 40 instrumentalists under the conductor- ship of Mr. A. R. Sutton. The eelectiene were" Wedd- ing March " (Mendelssehn), "Graceful D mice" (A. R. Sutton) and valse " P,ssing Clouds" (E H Front). - Alderman FRANC,MB in opening the pr )ceedings re- ferred to the excellent show and said he was el to see it so well appreciated by the public. The secretary had impressed upon him the fact that they had not promoted the show with the intention of money makine. %Veil, if that society was so thoroughly well eff that they did not want money, he did not think he could suggest a better idea that they should assist to pay off the deb': on the Gymnasium (Hear, hear). After referring to the inaueuration of the society he said it was now in a fleurishine state. They had got out of a state of darkness into a seer, of light, and no doubt in years to come they would have that show such a success that people wceld come to it from miles around Warrington. (Applau,e)e " Songs were given at intervals durine the evening by Mrs. Morley Stevenson. Miss McCarthy, and Mr. R. Eccles. Mrs. Stevenson opened the pregranme with an excellent rendition of Sullivan's " Lookine twit," and her second song was "Last Nietit" (Kjerliff) Mr.R Eocles' best effort was in the song "The Monarch of the storm." and received an encore,but did not comply with it as it was arranged that no encores were to he given. Miss McCarthy most effectively sang M "Ave Maria," and "Pack clouds away" (Bttrilll. The orchestral band played several other selections during the evening. Mr.W.Loneshave delivered a short address on "How to grow chrysanthemums" giving many use- ful hints on the planting and housing of the plants. The Rev. MORLEY STEVENSON also cave a 3hort address on "Plants and flowers in Lancashire towns," sneaking mere especially of flowers that would grow hest in War- rington and district. He said he did not profess to be very learned in these matters b t he stood before them as a man who loved his garden and who liked everybody else to do the came. After congratulatine the officials of the seeietv upon the success of the show that day he paid they who lived in such a place as Warrington and had their gardens had certain difficulties to contend with that. peeple had not who lived in the country, But were they to give en and say that, because of the smoke and yanotara and other things in the town, it wee. no 'ice hayine eneteiee like a garden or growing anyV)ine uire fl-eeeee ? He thought if such a question had arisen, that a leek mend that room and a glance at some of the trees grown in the town, might be taken as a straieht enewer tn the ques- tion. What was the great enemy they had tr; eo,tend with as gardeners? He supposed they would all say smoke, and fumes, which were mereo lese deadly in their effects. He was told that a greet deal of the smoke need not be given out, and he was further told that, if the manufacturexs would net seneke eenearnine apparatus to the chimneys, they would not reale peevent what was to them a great nnisa nee. bile won'' e'en save the proprietors actual expense in feel iieed„ Whether that was true ha did not know, but if Reel, an invention had not been made he earnestly hoped that with the advance of science it would he made end tha. they would once more see their atmosphere purifi d F-ren e'mnleeeind consequently their gardns more beset-ire, thin et the Present time. If there was any plan sW whieh smoke could be obviated, in the name of duty, in the name of health,he hopel it would be brought inte daily ‘1... That was the enemy they had to fight against. Bet there was another thing which they must take into neeaieera- tinn. That was the soil. It was a sandy in Warring- ton and sandy soil was Particularly good for the enitiva- tion of particular kinds of plants They had elms to hear these two things in mind—whae eiewees eenee up in sandy soil and also in a smoke. atmosphere. From many experiments he had tried he hiand thee nefhine, grew better in Warrington than these pla-te whieh grew seom bulbs such as crocuses, daffnrinla. hhaebells, narcis- sus, and hyacinths. They might take sneh nlenta into any garden in Warringcen and they wetald grow. Why did these plants do better than other, ? They all knew that plants took in a great deal of their non-ichment from the leaf, and in a smoky etenesnl'ere the little pores were apt to become choked no with the emelt, and dirt. But the plants with bulbs drew their am-miles from the bulb and that being placed nnderernund was not hurt by the smoke. (Applause.) Everyone who had ent a bit of garden ought to have a geed chew every spring. The next best time for flowers in Wereinetnn wee what was called here the hack end, which was !reel, better than the summer. Such foyers ee dehliea. sunflowers, marigolds and flowers of that sort, seemed to d very well, and made the erirden very brieht at the hack end of the year. The difficulty with him was always in the slimmer. He found one of the best flowers to crow then was the carnation. It, would he a, very good thing if some member of the Society weree ter to cove a list of plants,which he had' fennel to thrTve in the tnwn. in conclusion, the sneaker warned hie bee fere to avoid rose-growing in Warrington,ae from exnerienne he found that they did not thrive in the town. Votes eF thanks haying been accorded, a pleasant evening's entertainment concluded.</p>
        <p>Anton Rnbinstain, the eamons musician, on died Tuesday at Peterhof, near St. Pete rshure,•. A St. Petersburg telegram of Tuesdir states that the Czar's marriage will he celebrated on Monday next. A Rome telegram on ,71r-, t ig now certain that over four hundred been catISPCI by the earthquake shecks in Sicily. while the da maze to pro- perty amounts to millions of lire. Mrs. Brindle, wife of the 1-In,llerri of the Belthorn Inn, Blackburn, was on Tuesr.ll7 rnorninf7 forma b- her band dead on the cellar steps, death havinv fannarantly been caused by the fall and explocin, of a. n -raffin lamp. An inch of rainfall represents one hundred tons of water to an acre.</p>
      </div>
