      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>111 E AMINER, SATURDAY NOVEMBER 24 18C)4.</head>
        <p>111 E AMINER, SATURDAY NOVEMBER 24 18C)4.</p>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEE',r</p>
        <p>action. It was also essential not to leave the selec Lion of candidates until tne day of the parish meet- ing. Another good su4gestion is that in places where the Wesleyan body is in a minority, much might be done by approsching other parties and letting it be clearly understood that if a reasonable representation be not given, a poll would be de- manded, as even a minoriter representation would do much toward safe-guarding the interests oE the people. These suggestions are very valuable, and the villagers in the War:ington district would do well to adopt them. Aire tdy in one or two villages around Warrington, steps have been taken in the right direction.</p>
        <p>1200 in Widnes. The interest on tha money paid all the sick pay for the year. They hart also initiated 27 members during the year—The Chairman called upon the Grand Master to perform the ceremony of the even- ing, namely, the presentation of chairs. In doing so Grand Master said there, was no pleasanter duty which a Grand Master was called upon to perform than that 'of being the means of honouring a society's prosperous condition. He observed that tt.e chairs were two larger and smaller, and complimented the donors on two their kindness in making the gift, and had great pleasure in their name and on their behalf in asking the "Admi- ral Nelson" lodge to accept the chairs.—Bro. Webster in response, on behalf of the lodge,said they were proud to have the presentation made, and thanked the donors for their handsome present.—P.G.F. James Whittla re- turned thanks on behalf of his co-donors and himself, and spoke of their mdebetness to Bros.Pierpoint.Diprose, and Flowers. He also asked the Grand Master to accept from him a photograph of the Acton Grange Viaduct and the rock cutting under the viaduct during the construction of the Ship Canal. Bro. Whittle was sure that the photograph would be acceptable because the recipient was a Londoner and therefore intereated in the work of such a great undertaking. The Grand Master suitably responded. A harmonic gathering was then held in which a duet was sung by Bro. W. Fryar and P.G., J. H.Sherlock and a comic scng (in character) rendered with spirit by Bro.W.W.Whittle. All the toasts of the evening were drunk with enthusiasm by the audience. The meeting terminated with the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>When some years ago, the Rev. J. T. Wardle Staf- ford visited Warrington, his burning and eloquent words left such an impression upon his hearers,that when he came to Bewsey-road Wesleyan Chapel on Sunday, last week, it was not a matter of surprise to see the chapel filled. Hr. Stafford has not lost any of his force since his last visit,hut has rather increased it. His two ad- dresses on Sunday would leave a lasting impression upon his hearers. The lecture on John Howard,the prison philanthropist, which he gave on Monday evening last week, was a capital one. He has a most pleasing manner,and has just the right amount of wit to make his lectures spicy. He is very popular in York where he is at present stationed, and the chapel there, which is a very large one is filled every Sunday,although previous to his arrival it was not much more than half filled on ordinary occasions. He has been to Warrington on two occasions previously, the first time being at Bold- street Wesleyan chapel, when he very lucidly ex- plained General Booth's Darkest England scheme, and the second time dt Sankey Bridges chapel,when he lectured on "Moffat." It is satisfactory to know that he is sPonred for next year again at Bewsey- street Wesleyan chapel.</p>
        <p>Seeing that floods are eo numerous up and down the country I have no doubt that a number of War- rington people have been wondering how it is that we have not been treated to our customary inunda- tion. The season is to be found in the presence of the Ship Canal,which acts as a splendid district conduit. We shall have to bid good-bye to the "good old times of yore" when people—in the rainy season—were compelled to get boats in order to pass up linutsford-road. Nor is it likely that the scene of 1867 will ever occur again, viz, when the streets from the police station to Howley Quay were a few feet deep in water. Minor floods such as meadows, etc., there will be, but is it unlikely that we shall have any more inundations on a large scale. But for the Canal, we should, ere this, hava had a flood of more or less magnitude than those of the last few years.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. Rooke, J.P., relates an interesting re- miniscence of the trouble that the late Mr. Charles Lister, J.P., of Lymm took over the consideration of the cases which came before him. He says that Mr. Lister and himself were sitting late at the court at the Snutsford Quarter Sessions, and a man was sentenced to a term of imprisonment. The next morning quite early, soon after eight o'clock, a special messenger arrived from Agden Hall, Mr. Lister's residence, with a note to say that Mr. Lister had felt uneasy at the sentence which had been passed, and that it had disturbed his rest during (loke to return to limits- ' the night. He asked Mr. R ford that day, as the court was still sitting in order that the man might be called again and the sen- tence mitigated. It is very evident from this inci- dent that Mr. Lister was anxious that perfect justice should be exercised in every case.</p>
        <p>The success which attended the first chrysanthe- mum show promoted by the Warrington Floral and Horticultural Society, held in the gymnasium on Wednesday, last week, must fhave greatly encour- aged the officials. The chrysanthemums exhibited amateurs were perhaps not quite so imposing as by could be wished for, but it was proved that even chrysanthemums can be grown in smoky Warring- ton. If the Society can promote a taste tor flowers amongst the public, and further induce people to cultivate them, it will have more than justified its existence. The hints given at the evening con- versazione by the Rev. Morley Stevenson on flower culture in Warrington were most valuable to those persons who are fortunate enough to have gardens attached to their houies. Mr. Stevenson also made some apt remarks on the smoke nuisance. Those desirous of cultivating chrysanthemums were given much information by Mr. Wm Longshaw who read a short paper on how' to grow them. The programme had been so arranged as to give instruc- tion as well as amusement, and in this the promo- ters were very successful.</p>
        <p>The people who watched the Mayor's procession to church on Sunday morning last had their ardour —and their persons—considerably damped while doing so. The weather was mHt unfavourable,for while the Mayor and corporation w.re getting wet with the rain, they had to wade through a large amount of slush in the street. These depressing circumstances did not, however, have a very great effect upon the procession,for his worship was very well supported. •</p>
        <p>The whole affair was only a pageant, however and it does little in the way of promoting the interests of religion. It may be mentioned that Alderman Holmes on each occasion when he was elected mayor, refused to go to church at all, on the ground that it was only a show, and thereby tended to desecrate the Lord's Day. He was bitter- ly attacked at the time, but most people will now admit that Alderman Holmes' opinion was the right one.</p>
        <p>The Rev. E. C. E. Carleton, the Mayor's chaplain, took the oppbrtunity of preaching a very old- fashioned sermon. He appears to be one of the ' few remaining people who believe in passive obedience to the "powers that be" He apparent- ly regards as indefensible,and apparently as almost sacriligeous, any revolt against the ruling power of the country, no matter how tyrannical it may be. No doubt he would depricate any resistance of the persecuted .Stundists of the Jews against the government of the Czar of Russia, and by his doc- trine all risings against tyrannical kings such as ttok place against Charles I,or James II are against Biblical law. Such a doctrine suited the times of Laud and Stafford, but in these days there is an interesting air of antiquity about it.</p>
        <p>The Wesleyan body are making preparations for the coming Parish Council elections, and the Com- mittee of the Liverpool district synod have made several suggestions to the various circuits. They suggest that in every village and town on the cir- cuit plan, the members of the Wesleyan chapel should be called together and measures adopted for securing adequate representation on the councils. They also consider it advisable that other Noncon- formist churches and temperance organisations might be cowulted with a view to securing united</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>"WAS IT A MIRA.O LE ? "</head>
        <p>"WAS IT A MIRA.O LE ? "</p>
        <p>A TEST CASE.</p>
        <p>There are" miracles" and miraculous cures in the air just now, and question arises as to the permanence of</p>
        <p>these cures.</p>
        <p>Where effected, or alleged by quacks and empirics, they are chiefly imaginative cures, and the patient quickly reverts to the old state. Where they are cures by true scientific treatment they are permanent. The patient is always cured for good, it seems, by the partic- ular means which have led to so many and such surpris- ing newpaper reports, namely, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. Some six months ago, Mrs. Briggs, of 9, Tinsley-park- oad, Sheffield, after suffering from an " incurable " disorder which had made her a mental and physical wreck, and left no hope of her living, was enabled to test Dr. Williams then new discovery, with the result that she was promptly restored to complete health. Written to, within the last week (six weeks from the date of the cure), Mrs. Briggs replied, in the firm handwriting of a person evidently healthy and intel- ligent, that she is still perfectly well, and owes her cure to the use of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, and to them</p>
        <p>one. This is the mark of a scientific cure ; you do not get results like this front empirical or imitative " cures " ; it is essential that the true remedy of Dr. Williams be secured, and to do this the full name "Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People" must be seen on the pink wrapper. Pills sold loose or by the dozen or hundred are not of Dr. Williams' formula, :ss hich cannot be imitated or reproduced by retailers. It may be intere at ing to recapitulate briefly the details of Mrs. Briggs' cure, which are of so remarkable an interest that they were specially investigated by the "Sheffield Weekly Independent," a reporter from that important news- paper calling at Mrs. Briggs' house in August last. Six months before,she said,she could not have believed that she would have been then alive. It was im- possible for anyone seeing her now for the first time to imagine the physical and mental wreck she had been for years. Her troubles began with indigestion fifteen years ago, and this had grown worse every year since. Serious complications also set in, until at length she be came totally incapacitated from performing her ordin ary household work. Among other symptoms were loss of appetite, loss of flesh, and inability to sleep, with al, most continuous headache and various other pains. The worst feature ot her condition, however, was the ex- treme nerveusness brought oa by lying awake night after night, which produced a fearfully morbid state of mind,under the influence of which she often felt tempted to make away with herself. Subsequently her heart became affected, and she was troubled with shortness of breath. Her husband and her friends all supposed her to be past recovery. No means of cure within her power were neglected. She consulted three doctors, and was treated in the Sheffield Infirmary, but it was all to no purpose. She consented to try Dr. Williams' Pink Pills only with the idea of pleasing her husband, for she had by that time lost all faith in doctors or medicine. But the firs box gave her ease, and from that time the improvement went on uninterruptedly, her shortness of breath and headache gradually disappeared, and with their depar- ture her appetite returned, until at the present moment she declared she felt better than she had ever done in her life. What seemed to pleased Mrs. Briggs most was that she had completely got rid of her extreme, nervous- ness, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills are a sure remedy for such diseases as rheumatism. neuralgia, paralysis, locomotor ataxy, St. Vitus' dance, nervous headache, scrofula, chronic erysipelaa, &amp;c. They restore pale and sallow complexions to the glow of health, and are specific for troubles peculiar to the female ; whilst in men they effect a radical cure in all cases arising from worry,over- work, or excess of any nature. Manufactured by the Dr. Williams Medicine Company, 46 Holborn-viaduct, London, at 25. 9d. a box, or six boxes 13s. 91.; sent post free if not obtainable at chemists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>ODDFELLOWSHIP AT WARRINGTON. PRESENTATION TO THE " A.DNIIRAL NELSON"</head>
        <p>ODDFELLOWSHIP AT WARRINGTON. PRESENTATION TO THE " A.DNIIRAL NELSON"</p>
        <p>LODGE.</p>
        <p>A large number of Oddfellows of the " Admiral Nelson" Lodge, assembled in the Queen's Hotel, Sankey-st., on thnday night, the occasion of a presentation of four handsomely carved black oak chairs to the lodge by the Grand Master (Bro. John Diprose, Esq., London), on behalf of P.G., F. Jas. Whittle, Bros. J.R. Barnwell. and Thos. E. Greaves. There were also present, Bro. Robert Pierpoint, M.P., (chairman) P G.M., H. Flowers, Prov. C.S., Thos. Edmondson (Skipton), Prey. G.M. Thos. Righy, Prov. G. M. Vincent of London, Bro. S. Turner of Berry and other members and officers of the lodge and committee. The meeting was commenced by the chairman calling upon those present to honour the toast of" Her Gracious Majesty the Queen and Royal Family,"—Bro. J. Emson Prov. C.S. on being called upon, rose and said that he was very pleased to have the privilege that evening of proposing the toast of "The Manchester Unity, the Grand Master and Board of Directors." He was pleased to see so many visiting friends rallied round them that evening.—G.M. John Diprose in response assured his audience of his great pleasure at receiving such a cordial reception and hearing that this lodge was one of the best in the Warring.ton District. He wished every other lodge in the Warrington district the same success as the" Admiral Nelson " lodge which had been estab- lished 51 years and proved a great obligation to its mem- bers. After such a career as that he claimed that the "Ad- miral Nelsnn " Lodge was in a better position than most because it had no troubles about the disposal of surplus capital. For the Manchester Unity he claimed that it was the best friendly society in the world because they had something like 3 millions sterling beyond any other friendly society in the universe. (Hear, hear.) It gratified him to tell them that the order was increasing very much in New York. 1893 was a record year as far as the Manchester Unity was concerned because the total profit exceeded that of any other rear. They had sub- scribed very nearly 1 millions sterling. This was a large sum for the working class of England to put to- gether in twelve months. Their exnenditere had been £1,116,000 and during the ye.ar they had saved upwards of £300,000, the receipts being more than that amount over the expenditure. He hoped that they would al- ways be able to do this because as time went on the expenses were hound to he increased. They had now something like 28,000 members over 65 years of age anti in the year 1935 they would have (he calculated) 109,000 members over that age. He also hoped that the old age pension question would soon be solved andif they set their minds to it he was sure it would be carried nut. There was a large sum wanted for this pension and they would get it if everyone did his hest. A payment of 11. per week from the ages of 3 to 65 would give,as an old age pension, ss. per week. it was also his opinion that they should get as many young men to join the lodge as possible (Loud applause.)—The Chairman then congratulated the Grand Master on the interesting and able address given to them that nialat, and said their appreciation was shown in the hearty manner in which the apnlause was given.—P.P.G.M. James Massey then gave a Lancashire recitation, entitled " Tattlin' Matty " whieh has always been a favourite with his andience.—P.G.M. Henry Flowers then gave the toast of "The Warrington Dis- trict," coupled with the name of Pros'. G.M. Thomas Rigby.—ln response Bro.Rigby said that he was pleased to say that the lodge was increasing its strength numeri- cally. This year they had considered the advisability of taking a room for the purpose of holding their meet- ings free of any licensed premises. He was sure they would get more members if they did FO. They already had a room in mind, viz the old Conservative Club in Academy-st.—Bro. J. R. Barnwell gave a song, and Prov. C.S. Thomas Edmundson, of Skiptnn, proposed the toast of the "Admiral Nelson Lodge associated with the name of P.G. J. Emson," and made a few remarks about the lodge and its satisfactory position. Having been in existence over half a century and having a average age of its members at 35 its position was extra- ordinary.—Bro. Emsnn, in responding, said that last year the ledge held its jubilee at which P.G.M. Flowers was with them. This year, the 51et of their existence, they had the unique position of having chairs presented to the lodge. The lodge was worth £1,077 pounds at the beginning of the year. Of this, £6OO were invested in Warrington and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>EA.RLESTOWN CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>EA.RLESTOWN CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The annual tea meeting and concert in connection with the Earlestown Co-operative Society, was brought to a successful issue on Saturday evening. Tea was provided in the Co-operative Hall, about 450 being present, at two sittings, the tables being presided over by Mesdames Moss, Fenwick, Mottram, Warburton, Eckersley, Goff. Foden, Muscroft, Crompton, J. R. Barr. Lomas and McCawley ; Misses C. Davies, Crawford, Partington, Limond, Holliday, Blair and Webster. After tea a concert was given in the Town Hall, when the attendance was much aug- mented. Mr. John Moss (pr,.sident of the Society) pre- sided, and, besidea the Committee of Management,there were present on the platform, Messrs. Bell (Bolton), Smith (Leigh), Clarkson (Chorley), Watson (Wigan), Birch and E. Holt (Rochdale Pioneers),Baxendale Chor- ley), Owen (Rochdale Corn Mill), Pryor, Forbes and Bickerstaffe (St. Helens). After the concert had been opened by the quartette, "Lass of Richmond Hill," by Misses Kean and Davies, Messrs Kidd and _</p>
        <p>Limond. The Chairman said that at their last meeting held in May, 1892, it was his pleasing duty to congratul ite them on the completion of their new business premises, and now he had the honour of offering a similar compliment (an holding their first gathering in their new Town Hall. He did not intend to review all the work of the Saciety, during the past 12 months, but he must say that,exactly 12 months ago, they were all rejoicing that the lock-out in the coal trade was brought to an end, but he was afraid that although the strike was settled on that day. it had left its effects behind, which some had not I recovered from yet. During that tinge there was great distress in their midst, and they, as co-operators sympa- thised with the sufferers in a practical manner,by grant- ing £lO per week for 1.2 i weeks, £lOO being taken from the reserve fund, and a donation of £25 from the Co-operative Wholesale Society. of Manchester, making a total of £125, which was expended in provisions, and handed over to the different relief committees then in existence. At their last gathering they prided them- selves on the handsome outward appearance of their new premises, and since then they had gone to the expense of having them beautified throughout, and, altogether, he thought he might safely say that they possessed business premises second to none in the dis- trict. It had been his pleasure to visit a good many Co-operative Societies, but he had never found one yet, of the small societies that was better suited for business work than their own. (Hear, hear.) And if the mem- bers would only support them in tbe future as they had done in the past, he had no doubt they would have even a better report to lay before them. (Applause.) The concert was then proceeded with, Mr. W. H. Limond earning an encere for the song,"Off to Philadel- phia," giving in response, "Nancy Lee." Miss F. Kean was successful in the song, " In the chimney corner." Miss Lilian Roberts, of Staleybridge, as an elocutionist, possesses a peculiar cherm for Earlestown audiences,and she is sure of a hearty reception on each visit. It is over four years since she le.st visited the town, and a great improvement is noticeable, although as of old she has the power of taking her audience with her. Her con- tributions on this occasion were," There's trouble in the Amen Corner." " Geese," and, " The Last Shot." At this point the Secretary. Mr. Hargreaves, read his report, which stated that "During the year ending October 15th, 1894, you have paid for goods supplied to you £16,939 is. 5d., and vnu have received back in dividend the sum of £2,289 18s. 9id., at the rate of 2s. sp. in the £, for the year. Of course, this shows a falling off in the receipts when compared with 4 years ago, but this is accounted for by the very low prices of food and clothing, as compared with then. 1 have no hesitation in saying that you have had more stuff for less money, and that, I think, is something to be thank- ful for. You began the year with a capital of £11,315 14s. 7d., and you have added to that as contri- butions to shares £2,987 ss. lltd. ; and you have with- drawn £3,618 9s. 3d. The fact of being able to have command of this amount must have been a great boon to many families in the hour of need. You have received interest to the amount of £466 13s. Id., and at the end of that year you were worth £11,480 Ili. The Penny Bank shows, since my last rep irt, same imorovemen the amount then standing to their credit, £67 14s. 211. t the amount to-day is £lO3 154. 3d. Proceeding with the programme Messrs. G. Kidd and W. N. Limond sang the duet, "Love and War," which was much appreciated, and Miss Davies contributed in her usual finished style, "Th Promise of Life." Mr. G. Kidd was loudly applauded for the song," Come into the garden, Maud," and the quartette party sang, " Evening's twilighc." In addition to the vocal items, Professor Deardon, of Bolton, gave a couple of amusing ventriloquial sketches with his dolls, and Mr. E.Whittle, of Bolton caueed roars of laughter by his humorous con- tributions," You may believe it,"The henpecked club," an, "The story of Mary Ann," for which of course he received the usual encores. Mr. E. Leyland very efficiently presided at the piano, and in addition to accompanying the various items, he gave a selection on the piano. Votes of thanks to the workers, and the chairman brought a successful gathering to a close.</p>
        <p>VISIT OF THE REV. T. ROSS TO WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>On Sunday and Monday, the Rev. T. Ross, who was stationed for three years in the Warrington Wesleyan circuit, and which is at present residing ' in Huli paid a visit to his old circuit. He has been away from Warrington since August in last year, but he received a warm welcome, and has been greatly pleased with his visit, He occupied the pulpit at the Sinkey Bridges Wesleyan Chapel on Sunday morning and evening, and the congregations, especially at night, were very large. On Monday, the rev. gentleman gave an interesting lecture on "Insect life," illustrated by lantern slides, at the Bold-street Wesleyan schoolroom. The chair was occupied by Mr. J. Hartley, who, in his opening remarks, said that it was a pleasure to him to preside that night, as they were come to hear a gentleman whose interest in the church and in the young was such a source of gladness for three years in the circuit. That pleasure he was sure was shared by all present. The LECTURER, who was received with loud applause, said that he need hardly say how pleased he was to come. to Warrington, and to meet so many friends. He was also glad to hear that the Christian Endeavour Society was so successful, and that they were organis- ing the present series of lectures. With regard to his subject that evening, he did not mass to be an entomologist, but he had always had a love For any- thing which had life. His subject was insect life, and some of his friends seemed to think that he was going to lecture upon spiders. He would, however, leave that subject to be dealt with by a distinguished scientist, whom Weslevans were proud to be able to call " their own Dr. Dallinger." He himself proposed to go more into the minutite of insects. Men always possessed do- mestic animals, and also domestic insects. The former he always had by choice, but the latter had always been intruders. He had a number of slides specially, prepared, which when thrown upon the screen, would show that the insects were very beautifui, and he was sure that they would cause them to wonder at the great mind of their Creator,and cause them to adore Hi in and I magnify Him. The great aids in the study of insects , had been the microscope aid the microphone. The latter was able to make andible the movements of in- sects. and he was sure that there were marvellous won- ders yet to be revealed by means of this wonderful instrument. To show the marvellous variety in insects he might mention that while there were 1,500 species of animals, and 500 species of birds, there were no fewer than 500,000 different kinds of insects in the world. Photography as well as the microscope and the micro- phone had also done a great deal in extending the knowledge of insect life. Mr. Ross then had a number of slides containing sketches of insects thrown upon the screen, and displayed all the various orders, describing them in detail. The lantern manipulator was Mr. D. Stone. At the conclusion of the lecture a hearty vote of thanks was accorded the Rev. T. Ross, and the proceedings terminated.</p>
