      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>WARRINGTON DEBATING SOCIETY</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON DEBATING SOCIETY</p>
        <p>DEBATE ON THE HOUSE OF LORDS., At the opening meeting of the present session of the Warrington Debating Society, held on Friday night at the Masonic Room, Bold-street, the chair . _</p>
        <p>was occupied by Councillor Monlcs,and „"subject of debate was "The House of Lords," which was opened by Mr. R. Pierpoint,.M.P. There was a wry good attendance. Mr. A. H. CROSFIELD said there was one important omission in Mr. Pierpoint's speech. He did not say whether he was himself in favour. of the reform of the second chamber. That it appeared to him was the all important querion which was b .foro them. (Applause.) As one of the humblest members of the Liberal parpy,he (Mr. Crosfield) did not think that the second Chamber would ever be an ideal and Satis- factory piece of our constitutional ma-thinery until it had undertone considerable improvement.. (Applause.) _ .</p>
        <p>Ele had only brooght oneextract that niQht. (Laughter). It was from a hi) authority which he constantly used as a corrective to the tware.Litwr,4l class of literature in</p>
        <p>which he found his political viability/a. It was an ex- tract from the " Speetator " whin the past fw weeks. The " Spectator " said " We must get rid of the peers who add nothing to the political capacity of the House, and we must empower every Government to add a fair number of peers; who shall not have the hereditary title. The'House which has to have thR privii*ge of referring greatissues to the people must be athei4ghtful and dis- tinguished Hekise and must not belop-sided, so that it equally stop revolutionary and reactionary proposals." Speakinglor himself' he shtuild.not be inclined to go any further than that. He thought that the bulk of the Liberal party were in favour of a second chamber. Some few had set their hearts on a single chamber, but he for one, thought that that was an impracticability and not desirable. At the same time, a second ctiamber under tho guidance of Lord Salisbury did a little more than check. It wished to control (applause), and •for the matter of that Mr. Balfour let the cat out of the bag in the -House of Commons, a short time ago. It seemed to him that there was another aspect of that case. If it was the duty of the House of Lords to check hasty legislation, surely it was not also their duty to hopelessly waste the time of the country. (Hear, hear.) If the House of Lords was thoughtful ,and responsible men would have considered the seven years of the coutv-res time which had been abserhed in the greatest conflict since he Re- form Bill of 1832. He also legislation, iiitended that a all t the Lords did not always check hasty upon where the legislation came from (Hear,hear,and applause.) It passed the Reform Bill in 1867 in ten minutes, a bill which would now be called revolution- ary, bu.t which had been introduced by a Tory Government. (Bear, hear.) He did not. think it fair that the Helise of Lords should simply support that one party. In conclusion, he said that he was afraid that Mr. Pierpoint would not have any support from his par' y leaders if he advocated any reform of the House of Lords, and lie believed that he would have to subscribe to the party loyalty for which his party was conspicuous. (Applause). The ch bate was continued t•iy Councillors Bennett Roberts, Rev. E. C. E. Car'eton, Messrs. J. Gandy, S Kenyon, and A. E. Benjamitt, and at ten o'clock it wat%, adjourned until Wednesday</p>
      </div>
