      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <p>THE question of payment of mem- The Payment bers and its kindred reform of pay- ment of returning officers expenses was brought very prominently under the notice of the Prime Minister by a deputation on Tuesday. The deputation consisted of the Parliamentary Committee of the Trade Union Congress, and in- cluded such well-know labour leaders as Mr. JOHN BURNS, M.P., Mr. BRoADHURST, M.P., Mr. S. WOODS, M.P., and Mr. J. H. WiLsorr, M.P. The arguments in favour of payment of members are now well-known and they were recapitulated with clearness and brevity by members of the deputa- tion. Without payment of members and of return- ing officers' expenses there can be no true repre- sentation of the people in Parliament. However democratic the franchise may be and how ever strictly the principle of "one man one vote" may be carried out, so long as entrance to Parliament means heavy pecuniary disbursements so long will poor men be discouraged, as compared with more wealthy men, from seeking the -suffrages of the electors. This is, of course, class privilege. It has been recognised as such in almost every other country which enjoys representative institutions and rectified by the payment of mem- bers. In England alone have the financial barriers been allowed to remain in the way of full representation. It has often been said that the payment of members will lead to the corruption of public men, that there will in fact be a greedy scramble for office. 'This argument is really a libel upon the electorate of the country. No man can gain a seat in Parlia- ment—in the House of Commons at all ev outs— except by a majority of votes in some cons tituency, and it will be the fault of the voters if they return a man whose single object is the securing of a stipend of two or three hundred pounds a year. Apart from that, the present system does not al- ways secure a perfectly unselfish service. As Mr. JOHN BURNS said, if members are not now paid in the "meal of money" they are very frequently paid in the "malt of office, of ambition and of isocial prestige." Most people who are anxious for the clean government of the country would prefer as a legislator a man who is content with a modest salary to one who seeks his remuneration in the promotion of more or less bogus companies. There are, of course, a large proportion of the members of the House of Commons who find their chief reward in the discharge of what they con- sider to be a public duty, but these, as a rule, are not the men who anticipate that the payment of members will lead to the corruption of Parliament. Their opinion of the discrimination of the electors and of the integrity of the public men of the country is a little;too high to permit them to ex- pect any such result.</p>
        <p>The reply of the PREMIER to the deputation was no doubt on the whole as sat isfac- tory as was expected. He conceded the principle, and, indeed, with Liberals that has passed beyond the point of discussion. The two difficulties which he alluded to as being in the way are those of time and money. With regard to money, that is not the most serious of the two obstacles. The justice and expediency of the payment of members once being admitted, no great difficulty will be exper- ienced in providing the necessary amount of £200,000 a year, more or less. If need be, some useful economies might be effected in other direc- tions to balance this additional expenditure. The other obstacle—that of time—in the way of the carrying out of this reform is, however, a more serious matter. The legislative programme of the Goverment is already full to overflowing. Is there any possibility of adding to it a Bill providing for the payment of members without displacing some- thing of equal, or, perhaps, in the estimation of many of the Governments' supporters, of greater importance It is quite certain that the Tories will oppose, tooth and nail, any proposal for the payment of members,and it will be for the Cabinet to decide whether, after considering all the cir- cumstances, theyan afford the time to force such measure through the House of Commons.</p>
        <p>Jr is sot unlikely that the policy Boys Brigades. of the Established Church in forming boys' brigades will before long be imitated in more than one quarter. The Irish correspondent of the "Manchester Guardian " comments upon the fact that these brigades are being enrolled al. over Ireland, and he suggests that the Catholic population might take the lesson to heart. As he says" they are fellow citizens of the same empire. They need have no fear, I hope, of exceptional treatment. Let the national schools undertake this work. Nothing would tend more to make the people orderly, systematic, and well-behaved than such a training. And, so far from seeing any risks of collision or trouble, I think the movement would produce the very opposite results. It is only when one section claims and exercises special privileges that enmity and trouble come. The Government wisely and riglAtly do nothing to hinder our Protestant boys from teaching them- selves the principles of the "art military ;" let Catholics enjoy and exercise the same rights, else the old cry of " Croppies lie down will be most certainly revived," This, under the circumstances, ia not bad advice. If Protestant lads are drilled, solely, of course, for the benefit of the tra inin it confers, and for the ties which it creates between the rising generation and the church with which they are connected, Catholic lads may surely be drilled also. The same reason- ing applies to England and to other bodies of Christians besides Catholics and Churchmen. Indeed, there is no reason why this drilling and training should be confined to religious bodies. There are political and social movements all eager to make converts and to enlist the "men of the future." We may in the future have temperance boys' brigades and Socialist boys' brigades all Tnarching through the streets to their own special music. Wo will not say Anarchist boys' brigades because Anarchy seems to be the negation of order and discipline.</p>
        <p>THE betting prosecution which The 13pting P ecutions on Friday resulted in the Warring- tonros. magistrates imposing the heavy .11e of 1;75 in two cases was an instructive affair more ways than one. The protests 9gainst the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN COUNTY COURT.</p>
        <p>There were no cases of public interest at this court held in the Sessions House, on Tuesday, before his Honour Judge Wynne Ftoulkes. Three. adjourned sum_ monses, one default summons, 66 original plaint and 21 judgment summonses were dealt with. THE ILLNESS OF MR. S. B. CHADWICK.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. B. Chadwick, who has for some time been lying seriously ill at his residence in Kingsley, is now progressing very favourably, and his medical attendant (Dr. McDougall) has every confidence that he will shortly be restored to convalescence. FAILURE OF A RUNCORN PAINTER.</p>
        <p>Last Friday night's" Gazette" stated that a receiving order had been made against Samuel Peter Lewis, painter and decorator, Regent-st., Runcorn. COLLIER'S FRESH IRISH DAIRY BUTTER, 10d. per lb. is excellent. —Advt. MARRIAGE OF A FORMER RUNCORN LADY.</p>
        <p>At Christ Church, Sefton Park, Liverpool, on Wednes- day, by the Lord Bishop of the Diocese; the Rev. Canon Gauntlett, of Pershore, and the Rev. R. Irving, vicar of Christ Church; the Rev. J. Noel, incumbent, of Sr. James' Church, Croxteth Park, was married to Fanny Eliza Octavia Brundrit, youngest daughter of the late Mr. William Wright Brundrit, of the Mount, Runcorn. Miss Brundrit is the niece of Mr. John Brundrit, of Runcorn, and has many friends in the district. WESLEYAN TEMPERANCE SOCIETY.—RUNCO RN CIRCUIT. As will be seen from advertisement in another column temperance sermons are to be preached on Sunday in all the Wesleyan churches of the circuit. On the following Monday, a publie temperance meeting will be held in St. Paul's Chapel, when addresses will be delivered by the ministers of the circuit, and other gentleman. Mr. T. Simister will preside. COLLIER'S BLACK BLENDED TEA, 21c1. per quarter lb.—Advt RUNCORN'S NEW ELECTORAL REGISTER.</p>
        <p>There has just been published the newly prepared register for Runcorn' which will be the one in use at the forthcoming District Councils elections. In Runcorn North there are 2,576 electors, divided into the four wards, as follows :—Halton, 429 ; Mersey, 599 ; Central, 895 ; and Bridgewater, 653. Runcorn South contains the names of 1,073 persons, the respective numbers in the two wards being—Newtown, 649; and Heath, 424.</p>
        <p>COLLIER'S 10. BUTTER is the 'gem in the market.—Advt.</p>
        <p>UNITED METHODIST FREE CHURCH, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>In coonection with the Ellesmere-at. chapel, special services were held on Sunday. The preacher at the morning and evening services was the Rev. R. Percival, of Frodsham. The anthem " Come unto Me" (Gounod) was well rendered at the evening service by the choir, under the conductorship of Mr. J. Holford. Mr. J. Jones was the organist. Collections were taken on be- half of the chapel funds, and realised a satisfactory amount. _</p>
        <p>COLLIER'S sell everything for the smallest possible profits. Advt. _</p>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S BIBLE CLASS, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At the usual weekly meeting of this bible class held on Sunday afternoon in the morning chapel of the St. Paul's Wesleyan Church, Runcorn, the president, Mr. B. Kniveton, delivered a most interesting address on "St. John the Baptist." There was a large attendance and the address was much appreciated. A solo entitled "Ora pro Nobis " was exceedingly well sung by Mr. Ambrose Shingler. An organ solo was also contributed by Mr. Wm. Cooke with his usual ability. A TREAT TO SUNDAY SCHOOL SCHOLARS.</p>
        <p>About one hundred scholars connected with the Mill Brow Wesleyan school were provided with a substantial tea on Monday evening. The following attended to the wants of the children: Mrs. Pope, Miss Pope, Mrs. Pilgrim, Mrs. R. Yates and Mrs. W. George. An enter- tainment was afterwards provided, and amongst those present were the Rev. W. George, Mr. S. Pope, Mr. J. R. Frith, Mr. C. Moorfield and Mr. Charles Findlow. The latter part of the evening was spent in games. BRUNSWICK-STREET WESLEYAN IMPROVEMENT CLASS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>"The manufacture of bleaching powder and its pro- perties," was tho subject of a paper read at the weekly meeting of the above named elass on Wednesday evening by Mr. John Henry Darlington. The essayist illustrated his paper with a number of experiments, and explained the Weldon pro- cess. He also gave details of the manufacture of bleach- ing powder. A discussion followed the reading of the paper, taken part in by Messrs. Walter Mordan, D. Bis- brown, John Norman and Wm. Jeffries. Mr. T. Simister occupied th.. chair. Com.= BREAD ill the best money can buy.—Advt. SUDDEN DEATHS AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Eda Evans, aged 28 years, residing in Bridgewater-st., was found dead in bed on Friday morning. She had been ailing for sonic time, but had not recently been peen by Dr. Rohinson who had attended her.—John Rathhone, aged 58 years, sail-maker, of 1, Suffolk-st., was found dead in bed on Saturday morning. For several months deceased had been somewhat addicted to intemperate habits. On Friday evening he retired to rest at 9 30. Next morning, soon after five o'clock, his wife found he was dead. The circumstances connected with both cases were reported to the Coroner, Mr. J. E. Worsley, whe did not consider it necessary to hold inquests on the bodies. THE RUNCORN TECHNICAL INSTITUT/1.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Technical Instruction Committee was held on Monday evening. Mr. Wilmall (the chair- man of the committee) presided, and there were also present: Messrs. S. Taylor, F. J. Norman, J.P., J.W. Lightburn, D. Bisbrown, H. J. Grice, Edward Key,Wm. Hankinson and Dudley, Mr. Lake, law clerk. and4Mr. Marshall, secretary. It was reported that £3,000 had been promised towards the £4,000 required for the new Technical Institute, and surprise was expressed that only £5OO of this sum had been contributed from Run- corn. A discussion ensued as to the best way to raise the remainder of the amount required but no definite decision was come to. It was decided not to have another meeting until after the elections. A PLUCKY RtTNCORN LAD.</p>
        <p>About four o'clock on Sunday afternoon. George Riley, 4 years ef age, son of Mr. John Riley, of 14, Speakman- street, when crossing the footboards at the lock close to the entrance of the Ship Canal, stumbled and fell into the water, which was about 27 feet deep. His brother John, aged 13, hearing a scream, ran to the place. and plunged into the water without the slightest hesitation. He was able to swim, and soon succeeded in laying hold of the little fellow. Mr. S. Pollitt, the lock-keeper,heard the plunge and was immediately in attendance. Having secured a boat hook, he was able to sufficiently secure them until further assistance arrived, when they were both rescued from their perilous position. The elder boy displayed great heroism. They were both able to walk home, and are little the worse for thei mmersion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>FUNRRAL OF A. RUNCORN CAPTAIN.</head>
        <p>FUNRRAL OF A. RUNCORN CAPTAIN.</p>
        <p>The remains of the late Captain Robert Abram, who was unfortunately drowned through the sinking of the flat "Rose "on the Welsh coast during the storm on the 21st ult., and who resided at 24, Albert-street, Runcorn, were interred in Runcorn Cemetery on Friday afternoon. The procession from the deceased's late residence was headed by the Rev. 'l'. Brackenbury, Mr. J. W. Bazley, Mr. Fleet, Mr. Page, and a number of members con- nected with Camden Wesleyan Chapel. Mr. Alfred Riley and Mr. T. W. Chamberlain, represented the firm of Messrs. P. Speakman and Sons, in whose employ Cap- tain Abrams had been for several years. Among the bearers were Messrs. Joseph Clarke and J. Barker, em- ployed by the same firm. After the coffin, walked a number of relatives and friends. The Rev. T. Bracken- bury was the afficiating minister.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN SONS OF TEMPERANCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. George E. Sutton presided at the meeting of this society on Saturday evening. There was a good atten- dance and a paper on the subject " The evils of drink, and what is being done to suppress it " was read by Mr. George Hoult. The essayist spoke of the danger of moderate drinking, pointing out that many were thus led to copy the example set,but were unable to continue to be moderate drink,..rs. In dealing with the remedies, he referred to the value of total abstinence, bands of hope, and Ch7istian effort. He also alluded to Mrs. Lewis' temperance mission in Blackburn, dilating on the fAct that out of 500 cases which had come under her nrAice, all with the exception of six were Sunday school scholars. This he thought, showed the necessity of in- structing the young people in the principles of temper- ance. A hearty vote of thanks was accorded the essay- ist on the motion of Mr. D. Bisbrown, seconded by Mr. S. Henderson,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>.A 1 ' ; • -- – _ . ...... . Immix, – ,</head>
        <p>.A 1 ' ; • -- - _ . ...... . Immix, - ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>EXAMINER, sA'_L-ErRDA.T3 NOVEMBER 24, 1894.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, sA'_L-ErRDA.T3 NOVEMBER 24, 1894.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN FOREIGN MISSIONS. Special sermons and public meetings have been held at Pre,ton Brook, Weston and Weston Point. Mr. John Robinson has previous to the meetings given an exhibi- tion with the limelight lantern of interesting views, illustrating mission work and scenes in foreign lands. The collections at each place are in advance of those Lof last year.</p>
        <p>_ RUNCORN BOARD SCHOOL. At Greenway-road school on Thursday evening next the distribution of prizes and certificates gained by students in the science and art departments during the last session will be distributed by Dr. McDougall, chair- man of the committee. There will be no charge for admission. BETHESDA LITERARY AND SOCIAL CLASS.</p>
        <p>This class met on Tuesday evening when a lecture on Tennyson's " Princess " was delivered by the Rev. J. Simpson. A most interesting and instructive evening was spent. MR. BRIINNER, M.P. The member for the division, Mr. J. T. Brunner, who is at present on the Continent, will, it is expected, open the new Trades' Unionists' Hall in Runcorn, during the first week of the New year. The building is now almost completed, and its entire cost will be defrayed by Mr. Brunner.</p>
        <p>NMEIN</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>SUCCESS OF A LOCAL STUDENT.</head>
        <p>SUCCESS OF A LOCAL STUDENT.</p>
        <p>We are pleased to announce that Mr. H. Crowther, Halton-road, Runcorn, who is a student at the School of Art, Liverpool, has been awarded the doctor's prize, consisting of a silver medal and books, for the best painting from still life. It may be mentioned, too, that he also secured the only first-class certificate for the time test in the same subject at the May examination of this year. TXE FORTHCOMING ELECTIONS AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>There is very little further development to record re- garding the selection of candidates for the forthcqming elections. No definite action has yet been decided upon by either of the two political parties, but a host of names have been mentioned as likely candidates for the six wards. Amongst them may be mentioned the Rev. J. Simpson, Dr. Gastaldi, Dr. McKenzie, Mr. A. R. Nor- man, Mr. R. Posnett, Mr. .1. Kirkham, Mr. J. R. Frith, Mr. J. Horsefield,Mr. W. M. Wilkinson, and Mr. F.Dean. As the Commissioners, with one or two exceptions, in- tend to seek election to the new body, it will be seen that there is every probability of an all round fight. The Liberal Council it is expected will definitely decide upon what action they will take on Tuesday evening. COLLIER'S sell the best quality goods at lowest possible pricaz.—Adct RUNCORN SEAMEN'S INSTITUTE.</p>
        <p>Mr. John Birkett provided an excellent concert on Tuesday evening at the Seamen's Institute. Mr. Shaw, missionary, presided, and there was a large attendance. The programme commenced with a quartette entitled " The Village Chorister "and this was submitted by members of St. Paul's Wesleyau choir. The same party also sang "Laugh and Grow Fat." Mr. Walton Millington contributed a reading " The Yarn of the Nancy Belle." The soloists for the evening were Miss Eden, Mr. Parry, Miss Birkett, Mr. Kniveton. and Mr. Harry Millington. All received hearty recep- tions. Mr. Mason very successfully gave two comic songs "Mrs. Henry 'A wkins " and "Our Little Nipper." Duets were contrikuted by Messrs. Kniveton, and Harry Millington "All's Well " ; by Messrs. Wm. John Milling- ton and Birkett" A Sail in Sight," and by Messrs. Parry and Evans "The two sailors." A recitation was given by Miss Agnes Davies. At the conclusion of the concert, Mr. Shaw proposed a vote of thanks to those who had assisted in the concert. This was seconded by a sailor and carried with acclamaticn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>BILLIARDS.-WIDNES T. RUNCORN LIBERAL CLUBS.</head>
        <p>BILLIARDS.-WIDNES T. RUNCORN LIBERAL CLUBS.</p>
        <p>. _ The annual meeting between the members of the Wines and Runcorn Liberal Clubs took place on Wednesday evening at the Runcorn Liberal Club. There was a large attendance and considerable interest was manifested in the various games. On the previous visit of the same team last year Runcorn won by over 300 points. On Wednespay evening, however, there was *very appearance that this verdict would be reversed,for previous to the last four games being played the Widnesians were 170 points ahead. When Messrs. C. Handley, R. Moore, W. B. Jones and H. Heesom came to play their opponents they succeeded in securing a vic- tory for their side by 37 points. The following were the individual scores :</p>
        <p>RUNCORN. WIDNES. W. B. Brimelow (capt.) 126 J. Parkinson W. Wright Wm. Riley R. Owen C. Handlej . R. Moore l5O J. Johnson Henry Heesom l4B J. Quinn J. Elliott ll6 J. Kershaw H. Wright J. Green F. Ma.sey W. B. Jones Wm. Cobley J. Goodier</p>
        <p>Total 1863</p>
        <p>Total .1826</p>
        <p>FUNERAL OF THE LATE MR. J. H. Deviars. Tha remains of the late Mr. James Henry Davies, 24 sears of age who died on Thursday were laid to rest in Runcorn Cemetery on Sunday afternoon. Mr. Davies was a native of Newfoundland. but came to Runcorn five years ago, and resided with Mr. Stubbs, 34, Eger- ton-street. By his genial disposition he had gained numerous friends. He was an active member of the Christian Endeavour Society connected with Greenway- road Primitive Methodist Chapel,and wasalso connected with the Runcorn branch of the Sons of Temperance. Prior to the cortege leaving the house, the hymn " Lead, kindly light" was sung. A procession was then formed and was headed by Captains E. Davies, W. Yates, and J. Abrams and Mr. C. le Couteur. Then came about 100 members of the Christian Endeavour Society inclu ling the Rev. T. Bramall, Mr. J. Wilson, Mr. Coombes, and Mr. Richardson. About thirty mem- bers of the Runcorn Sans of Temperance came next, amongst whom were Messrs. Thomas Cooke (secretary). D. Bisbrown (treasurer), Thomas J. Holt (sick steward). William Cooke (assistant secretary), G. Holt (conduc- tor), John Pendlebury, James Jones, Peter Pendlebury, Thomas Burrows John Matthews, Joseph Evans, J. Cawley, J. Lightfoot, J. Jackson, and H. Ackerlev. The bearers were Messrs. Thomas Shaw, William dittens, jun., W. Nesbit, W. Dyer, Vtalter Kilcross, Jackson, Alfred Cooke, and Samuel Dutton. On the coffin were many wreaths, forwarded by Mr. Stubbs and family, his fiancé, Mr. William Stubbs, jun., Mr. S. Stubbs, Mr. Jabez Stubbs, Mr. James Stubbs, Mr. Robert Stubbs, Captain Hughes, Mr. and Mrs. Janion, and Mrs. Knowles, Mr. and Mrs. William Stringer, Mr. and Mrs. Gleave, and a large artificial wreath from the members of the Christian Endeavour Society. The Rev. T. Bram- hall conducted the funeral service, and at the conclusion those assembled around the grave sang "Rock of Ages."</p>
        <p>THE PARAFFIN LAMP OUTRAGE Al'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>WIDNES. EXEMPLARY SENTENCE.</head>
        <p>WIDNES. EXEMPLARY SENTENCE.</p>
        <p>Catherine M`Mullen (30) was indicted at Liver- pool Assizes, before Mr. Justice Wills, for having,l on the 25th August, at Widnes, feloniously caused certain grievous bodily harm to Julia Maud WlCay. Mr. Formby prosecuted, and Dr. Commins, M.P., defended.—The prosecutrix stated that on the date in question the prisoner came to witness's house in Water- st. whilst she was out, When witness returned the prisoner used bad language to her, and taking up a burning paraffin lamp threw it at witness. It broke on her shculders, and the burning oil ignited the clothes of witness. Prisoner not only did not attempt to put the fire out, but called out, "Oh, let the burn." Prior to this prisoner had threatened witness. Cross- examined : The row was caused owing to an in- timacy between witness and prisoner's brother-in- law. Wi'ness was sure that prisoner threw the lamp. It was not upset in a struggle between them. Witness did not know that prisoner had been to a wedding that morning, and had had a lot of drink.—The medical evidence was to the effect that M'Kay was badly burnt about the back, arms, and egs. For some time she was in danger of losing her life.—For the defence, Dr. Commins submitted that prisoner did this rash act in a moment when she re- ceived great provocation by the words uttered by M‘Kay ; and that doing it in an angry moment she in- tended no harm to M‘Kay. Prisoner had no idea then of the consequences of her act.—The jury found her guilty.— His Lordship said prisoner had been convicted of an awful offence. The act of throwing lighted lamps was extremely dangerous to life, and it was a great chance whether the person at whom one was thrown would get over it. It was constantly resorted to now by violent people who had no other weapons at hand. The use of the knife was far less serious. Prisoner might have killed this woman, and if she had; nothing would have saved the life of the prisoner. She must go to penal servitude for four years.—On hearing her sentence prisoner fainted, and had to be carried out of the dock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>THE PARISH COUNCIL ELECTIONS.—SeveraI meetings havo been held in Frodsham lately for the purpose of deciding upon candidatea for the forthcoming elections. It is understood that at least three publicans intend to enter the field. A progressive party is also arranging a list of candidates but their names are at present with- held.</p>
        <p>MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT CLAM—A parer on "Music and its interpretaticn " was given on Wednesday even- nto the members of this class by Mr. Watson, of iiverpool. The essayist also gave a rendering of one of own interpretations. A most enjoyable evening was pent. HAYDOCK.</p>
        <p>ST. JAMES' CHURCH.—The annual harvest tea party was held in St.Jatnes' school,Elaydock on Modelay even- ing. There was a very fair number present, and the tea was much enjoyed. The evening was afterwards spent in dincing, to thtt strains of the Haydock brass band.</p>
        <p>1 In wash ing,MATCHLESS CLEANSER is a perfect Treasur, It saves much work and time, to use it is a pleasure.---ADVT</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>1 THINGS HEARD AND SEEN RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>1 THINGS HEARD AND SEEN RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>(BY AN OCCASIONAL CONTRIBUTOR.)</p>
        <p>At present there hangs over Runcorn a dark cloud of trade depression, and there is but a very gloomy prospect for the winter. There does not appear to be even a silver streak to the cloud as an early re- vival of trade seems highly improbable. The Ship Canal Company struck a blow at Runcorn trade when they took away the graving dock which had for so many years upheld a shipbuilding trade in the town This week the United Alkali Company, have found it neccssary to issue notices in their works at Runcorn, Weston, Widnes and elsewhere stating that after the 28th of the present month work in most of the branches will be stopped until further notice. Immense stocks are on their hands, and until they have been cleared off somewhat, work will not be carried on. Messrs. Wiggs, Bros. and Steele, have also decided to follow suit. Such stoppages of work will h.- most seriously felt, and as the workpeople are only just recovering from the last one, many of them are unprepared for it.</p>
        <p>Speaking of this reminds me of a peculiar incident which occurred in Heath-road, Runcorn, a few days age, and which clearly proves that there is al- ready distress in some quarters. A married man with his wife and five children were without food, in the window, declar- ing ea ee novelofp paper of as placed procuring some was adopted. to the neighbours and passers-by that the family was starving, and that they wen unable to get food. The notice naturally created much interest, and a large crowd assembled around the window. In a shoe, time the family had many sympathetic friends who readily came to the rescue with a good supply of food and satisfied their wants, at least, for a short time.</p>
        <p>The transference of the Cheshire parishes of Latchford, Grappenhall, and Thelwall from the Warrington Union to the Runcorn Union should be highly satisfactory to the ratepayers in the Run- corn Union. The three parishes are of considerable rateable value, and as the cost of administration will not be much increased it is probable that there will be in course of time a small reduction in the poor rate. It will be remembered that the trans- ference was made by a joint committee of the Lan- cashire and Cheshire County Councils, but the War- rington Guardians did not intend to part with the parishes without a struggle, and accordingly they appealed against the decision. But Mr. Jenner Fast, Government Inspector,after holding an inquiry has Committee, and be merged into ut hpehreelfdo rteh, et hdeectihsri poafr sthhees aJroei ntto the Runcorn Union. It may be thought by some to be a "robbing of Peter to pay Paul," but of late there has been a tendency to keep to county boun- daries, and to confine the county business within the county.</p>
        <p>With the near approach of the Parish and District Council elections it was only natural that the vari- ous parties should be preparing for the contest and selecting their candidates. Several private meet- ings have been recently held in Runccrn and dis- trict,and names submitted as likely candidates. At Halton an independent party consisting of both Churchmen and Nonconformists have already ar- ranged their list of candidates, numbering twelve, which is the required number for the council. These will be nominated at the meeting held on December 4th. Candidates have also been selected for Weston Point. But in Runcorn no definite decision has yet been come to. A progressive pro- gramme has been arranged by the Liberal Council and the replies of candidates will be considered on Tuesday next when it may be expected to be known definitely, whom the Liberal party intend to bring forward. The Watermen's Union, of which Mr. Gatley is secretary, intend to submit, several names to the Liberal Council as can- didates, and these it may be stated are in favour of the progressive policy proposed to be adopted. On the other hand there are numerous rumours afloat as to candidates to be proposed by the Conserva- tives, and further that others are coming out as in- dependents. Altogether, lively times seem to be in store for the next few weeks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>EA.RLESTOWN AND NEWTON</head>
        <p>EA.RLESTOWN AND NEWTON</p>
        <p>BURGLARY AT EARLESTOWN. An impudent burglary was perpetrated in Earles- town between Monday night and Tuesday morn- ing. The house entered was in Brunswick-road, Newton Common, occupied by Mr. James Thelwell, entrance being effected by the scullery window, which had not been secured. The thief on gaining access ascended the stairs and entered the bedroom where Mr. The!well was sleeping, and after possess- ing himself of a watch and a sum of money, he de- camped by the back door and got clear away. GOOD TEMPLARY. The Earlestown Ebenezer Lodge of Good Tern- lars held its usual weekly session in the Co-opera- tive Hall on Tuesday evening when there Was a good attendance of members and visitors. Bro. A Morris C.T.presided. Four candidates were proposed and admitted to membership. The digest of business of district lodge was referred as to lodge represen- tatives. The married members were responsible for the programme of songs, readings, and selection on melodion, by Cie members, after which a coffee supper was provided and enjoyed by all. FIRE AT THE CO-OPERATIVE STORES. During the progress of the concert which was held on Saturday night in the Town Hall, in con- nection with the Earlestown Co-operative Society, word was brought that a fire had broken out in the business premises of the Society. An examination was at once made, and it was found that some woodwork in the kitchen, near one of the boiler flues, had ignited through the overheating of the flue. Prompt measures were taken, and the fire extinguished before much damage was done. CONCERT AT THE VULCAN. In connection with the Vulcan Foundry Cricket and Association Football Club, a concert was given in the Vulcan Schoolroom on Monday evening. The room was well filled, and financially the concert was a decided success. Two of the artistes whose names appeared on the programme (Madame Rose D'Almaine and Mr. J. Luria) failed to be present, and their places had to be filled at the last moment. The other artistes were principally local ladies and gentlemen, and they very ably acquitted themselves in the various items which they contributed, en- cores being frequently demanded. The following was the programme submitted: Pianoforte solo, Mr. W. Willis; song, "The Irish Emigrant," Mr. P. Hastie ; song, "Thou art so near and yet so far," Mr. P. Mooney; song, "Forget-me-not." Miss M. Con- way; song, "The Alarm," Mr. S. Swann; song, "A bird in hand," Miss J. Roberts; song, "My Gypsy Jane," Miss M. Williamson; instrumental trio, Messrs. Taylor, Harrison and Critcbley ; song, "Ora Pro Nobis," Miss J. Roberts; duet, " Larboard Watch," Messrs. Hastie and Mooney; song, "Out on the deep," Mr. S. Swann; song, "Dolly's revenge," Miss M. Conway; song, "The sparrow on the tree," Miss M. Williamson. The humorous business was supplied by Mr. R. Scarisbrick of Warrington,whose selections caused much amusement. PRESENTATION TO MR. N. SIDDELRY. The presentation of an illuminated address and bible to Mr. N. Siddeley who after 35 years service had lately resigned his position as a member of the choir of Wargrave Church, was made the occasiGn of a social evening in the Wargrave Schoolroom on Monday night. The Rev.J.Carson, rector, the sides- men of the church, and members of the choir were present, and the proceedings commenced with a tea to which about 30 sat down. After tea the Rev. J. Carson read the following address: "To Mr. Nathaniel Siddeley sen., Earle-street, Earlestown. Dear sir.—We the Rector, wardens, sidesmen and choir, of the Parish Church,Emmanuel, Newton-in- Makerfield, desire to show our appreciation of your long and faithful services as a member of our choir During 35 years of membership, it has been your endeavour to support, and further the spiritual and holy welfare of the church and schools so dear to you, and we are sure you will look back with pleasure and satisfaction upon all those years, when your voice was heard assisting the congregation in their psalms and hymns, and spiritual songs. We sincerely regret your retirement from amongst us, and more so from this being caused by failure of health, but we earnestly hope this may soon be restored, and with Mrs. Siddeley and your family you may be spared to enjoy much happiness for many years to come, and as a small taken of our kind remembrances and good wishes we ask you to accept this address, together with the accompany- ing bible." Signed. James Carson, M.A., rector: C. B. F. Borron, C. J. Starkey,wardens ; F. Leyland, choirmaster and organist ; C. H. Lea, see. to the choir. Nov. 19th,1894. The address was tastefully executed, by Messrs. Farmer and Howe of Man- chester. and WrIA enclosed in an oak frame. Mr. Carson in making the presentation made a short but appropriate address, Messrs. Leyland,Mawdsley, and James Wright also speaking in eulogistic terms of Mr. Siddeley's services. Mr. Siddeley suitably replied, and the rE mainder of the evening was spent in a social manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
