      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>BY AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDENT.</p>
        <p>The children's concert given last week in the Town Hall, was a decided success from every point of view, the only drawback being that for such occasions the hall is not large enough, and it is a pity that the promoters of concerts and other en- tertainments which are likely to prove attractive, are restricted to space. NA only is this a hard- ship upon the prcmoters, but the audiences are un- comr.ortably crowded, and 'the heat, as the evening advances,becomes almost unbearable. Tne ventila- tion may be perfect, but when audiences are cramped for space, the heated condition of the atmosphere is more noticeable than when there is plenty of elbow room. I have previously drawn attention to this matter, but 1 suppose we shall have to bear with things as they are, for the pre- sent, at any rate.</p>
        <p>Burglars are about! I suppose this larconic ex- pression will be fully understood by householders, and doubtless be sufficient to place them on their guard. The burglar or burglars are also fairly barefaced, and go about their work in a style which gives one the idea that they are adepts in the art of housebreaking. There has been a burglary this week on Newton Common, and as it is not the first which has taken place this winter, in this neigh- bourhood, it behoves people to be very careful in securing their houses before retiring to rest. The back portion of the house seems to be the favourite mode of entrance, and it would be well if the windows and doors in this portion were specially seen to.</p>
        <p>The candidates for the District Council are bestir- ring themselves, and a canvas of the various words is being carried on industriously. It is astonishing- however, when one becomes a candidate for such a position as district councillor, what a host of acquaintances one suddenly lights upon. At every street corner you are sure to meet somebody whom you know or have known in the long, long ago, especially if that somebody happens to be a resident in the ward you intend to contest. Of course, you do not renew the acquaintance with any intention of securing a vote. That is certainly an idea never for a moment contemplated; all the same you sud- denly find that you know a great many people, and you just like to show them that you have not quite forgotton their existence.</p>
        <p>Then you issue an address in which you hold forth very tempting promises of what you will do providing (always providing of course) that you are elected. But enough ! We shall certainly in the course of a very short period live in a perfect elisium, if one is to believe all that we are told in the addresses. No expense shall be spared to make our lot the happiest on the face of the globe, and yet it will all be accomplished without any addi- tion to the rates. Fudge! How can a man im- prove his house without paying for it? My private opinion is that when the candidates do get in the promises will be scattered to the winds, and they will forget that they ever made any. In the ad- dresses there is not much diversity of opinion, and lam inclined to think the candidates have formed themselves into a clique to give an unanimous opinion on the various questions. We shall very soon be in the throes of the election, and I expect there will be a few surprise packets.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>ALLEGED SUICIDE SUICIDE OF A GIRL AT EARLESTO WN.</head>
        <p>ALLEGED SUICIDE SUICIDE OF A GIRL AT EARLESTO WN.</p>
        <p>Some excitement was caused in Earlestown on Tuesday morning by the circulation of the news that a girl named Louisa Jones, 16 years of age, of 5, Chandos-st., Earlestown,had been found drowned in the Sankey Canal, near Bradley Bridge. The girl was in service at Wigan,and on Saturday came home for a few days' leave. On Monday night she left home about hall-past nine, with the supposed intention of going to Wigan by the 9.45 train, and then appeared in her usual spirits. She returned in a few minutes afterwards for a prayer book. She again went out and nothing more was seen or heard of her until Tuesday morning. Shortly be- fore six o'clock some flatroen found a jacket, umbrella, a pair of gloves, silk handkerchief, and prayer book, the latter containing the girl's name and address, on the canal bank. They commenced a search for the body. but it was not until nearly eleven o'clock that it was recovered, and was at once removed to the house of her father. THE INQUEST.</p>
        <p>An inquiry into the circumstances Was held at the Wellington Inn, Earlestown, on Wednesday afternoon by Mr. S. Brighouse, coroner.—David Jones, father of the deceased, said he brought the girl home from Wigan on Sunday. He went to bed on Monday night, a few minutes before, she left the house to return to Wigan. He offered to accom- pany her to the station, but she refused his com- pany. He knew of nothing that would lead her to commit suicide, and so far as he knew she never had any trouble with any young man.—Jos. Leech, son of the lock keeper at Haydock, depos,Kl to the girl's jacket, umbrella, gloves and prayer book being brought to him by a boatman named John- son, about 6 o'clock on Tuesday morning,who stated that he had found them on the canal bank. Witness took them to the police atation.—Evidence was given by Mrs. Jones, the girl's mother, and David Jones, her brother, and the coroner in summing up, said there was no evidence to show how the poor girl got into the water and came by her death.—A verdict of "Found drowned," was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>WAKE UP. NYOWTON !</head>
        <p>WAKE UP. NYOWTON !</p>
        <p>Nyowtoners ! be up an' doin'— Shake off that greyt Earlesteawn thumb; For its welly spellin' ruin, In the past, an' days to come. Ever sin' th' Owd Cheermon left yo' To the mercy of the Fate, Earlestown Peauncers hey bereft yo' Ov aw else but payin' Rates. Yo'n bin lung enuff i't' traces, Pool&amp; hard to fill their stream; An' its time yo:swopp'd urn places— Them fer th' skirnm'd milk, yo fer th' cream! See that mass o' bricks an' mortar— Just a arkitect'ral joke— Built fer "gas" an' " jaw "—nowt shorter— Fit to make a bull cauf choke! Wed l its caw'd a " Bungler's Folly,"— (Maybe sey'ral hed a share)— Its a " Ode to Melancholy " Noather oblong, reaund, ner square! Well. WOVE th' use on it to Nyowton, Aw should dearly like to know? It wur just as wee! Bowton, Per wot good it is to yo! Up then, lads, its comin' nearer, Wen yole hey a feyghtin' beaut ; An' its gettin' daily clearer, Yo mun chuck some owd uns eaut ! Then yore chances 'ull be betteft, Per wot brass ther is to spend; Its quite sartin' yo've bin fetter't Wl' the goods thay'n hed to vend! Get yo ready fer th' Election; An' aw'd hey yo aw tak' note, Don't send chaps that need direction, Er a warnin' heaw to vote. An' if yo're the winners, send me One o' thoose " Glenlivet " Kegs ; Its not Doctors say 'till mend me— Yo'rea ez allus, Turn o' Pegs.</p>
        <p>TUM 0' PEGS. Rose Bonk, November 23rd. 1894.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>FRODSHAX</head>
        <p>FRODSHAX</p>
        <p>SUNDAY SCHOOL CONVENTION.—At the Frcdsham Wes. leyan Sunday School, on Tuesday evening. a Sunday School Convention, attended by representatives from the various Sunday schools in the neighbourhood, was held. Miss Bessie Dutton, of Moore, read an admirable paper 041" The conditions necessary for securing the early conversion of children," and Mr. N. Humphreys. of Chester, gave a specimen lesson for a Sunday School class, taking the international lesson for the following Sunday. A discussion afterwards ensued, in which Messrs. Thornton, Proud. Ringrose, Swaby, Sant, and Woodward took part. The Rev. R. Renton, Wesleyan minister, oocupied the chair. FOREIGN MISSIONARY MEETING AT FIVE CROSSES.—At the Five Crosses Wesleyan Mission Room on Thursday evening week, a foreign missionary meeting was held. Mr. R. Poanett, of Runcorn, occupied the chair. The claims of foreign missions were advocated by the Rev. R. Renton, Mr. H. Swahy, and the Rev. A. Pearce, who gave a most encourar,ing account of the success which had attended missionary effort in the West Indies. There was a good attendance and a collection was taken on behalf of the Wesleyan Foreign Missionary Society.</p>
        <p>2 Dirt think itself the most abused When MATCHLESS CLEANSI3It SOAP is used.--a.D7r.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>NEW CONFIDENCE TRICK.</head>
        <p>A NEW CONFIDENCE TRICK.</p>
        <p>A new form of the confidence trick, vastly superior to the old one, has been discovered by a Parisian swindler, and practised with successful results. The method adopted has been explained to the police by a coachman who had a small sum of money of his wife's to invest. The coachman, Dupuy by name, went to the Bourse,and among the outside dealers saw a gentlemanly-looking man, who got into conversation with him. According to ttle gentlemanly-lookin,c,, man, it was the simplest thing to realise enormous profits by speculation, "Lend me two hundred francs," he said, "and I will guarantee to double it in less than an hour." The money was lent, and the stranger went off with it. Shortly afterwards he returned, and handed to Dupuy four hundred francs, saying—" That is nothing; it is only a beginning." Dupuy was anxious to make more money in the same way, but was told it was too late. "Come again to-morrow," said the stranger, "and bring with you a few thousand-franc notes, and you will see what you will see." The next day Dupuy returned with 3,000 francs in notes, and handed them to the suc- cessful speculator, who went off with the money, and has not been heard of again.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>SIX GHOSTS IN SIX MILES.</head>
        <p>SIX GHOSTS IN SIX MILES.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Arnott, of Carsethorn, who read before the Dumfries and Galloway Antiquaries, the other evening, a paper on '• Kirkbean Folk-lore," gave the startling in- formation that they had six ghosts down there in a dis- trict of about six miles by three. Of some of these un-, pleasant re-visitors of the moon the leaturer gave parti- culars. One was at three cross roads near Arbigland, and had a tragic story connected with it. A young lady of the family of Craik, formerly proprietress of the estate, was said to have become attached to a young man named Dunn, a grocm or horsebreaker in her father's employment, and one day the object ef her affections was shot dead. In the eyes ot the law the sad occurrence was considered a case of suicide, but popular belief attributed it to the murderous act of ene of Miss Craik's brothers. It was said that the lady was of the latter opinion, and that she went to reside in England,never returning to the place so full to her of tragedy. The remains of Dunn were interred on the Borron Hill, and years afterwards were disinterred by a man in the neigh- bourhood, the skeleton being, it was said, sent to Miss Craik at her request. The apparition of the murdered man was said to frequent the lonely spot where he met his death, and one of his informants spoke also of the appearance here of one of the white ladies so familiar in ghost stories, and whose affection for Kirkhean seemed somewhat remarkable. About half-way between the villages of Kirkbean and Preston Mill, where there is a small plantation on the side of the road and a larger wood on the other, and where the road forms a hollow, anot her white lady was seen, who was said not only to walk on the tops of the trees, but sometimes to acccmpany passengers on the highway.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>NEARLY DROWNED IN A BATH.</head>
        <p>NEARLY DROWNED IN A BATH.</p>
        <p>A VICTIM TO CRAMP.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. Heald, 7, Church-street, an elderly man, had a very narrow escape from drowning on Satur- day evening, in a private bath at the Corporation Baths, Legh-street. Soon after 6 p.m. on that day, Mr. Heald came to the baths and was accommodated with a private bath nearly full of warm water. Saturday being a busy night Mr. Barlow (bath-keeper), was busy attending to his usual duties but about 7.30 as he was walking past the compartment occupied by Mr. Heald he heard a slight knocking but thinking that it was caused by someone putting on his boots did not take any notice of it until hearing it repeated he stopped at the door of the bath and heard a slight moan. Mr. Barlow then lost no time in bursting open the door, when he found Mr. Heald reclining against the side of the bath with his mouth only about an inch above the water which had gone cold during the time that had elapsed since it was put in. The lowsr part of Mr. Heald's body was quite cold, and he was com- pletely exhausted, and could only just manage to move his hand. Mr. Barlow, assisted by Mr. W. Reed, pro- ceeded to restore animation to the unfortunate bather, and after a time this was accomplished. On his coming round Mr. Heald said that he was liable to cramp, and an attack had come upon him while in the bath. He I had swallowed a great quantity of water, and was con- sequently very ill. After being dressed,a cab was sum- moned and Mr. Heald was conveyed to his home. On Sunday night Dr. Peacocke attended the sufferer, and reported that in a few days he would be completely recovered. Mr. Heald never lost consciousness while in the water, but was growing weaker and weaker, and if Mr. Barlow had been a quarter of an hour later it is. almost certain that he would have relapsed into insensi- bility and been past all hope of recovery.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>LYMM.</head>
        <p>LYMM.</p>
        <p>CONCERT.—A popular concert was held in the New- field Institute on Siturday night. The Rev. J. T. Col- lins presided. Recitations, songs, &amp;c., were given by the following :—Mrs. Ernest Crosfield, Madame Ruble, Mrs. Ridgway and Messrs. Peter Holt, W. Warrington, Richard Dixon. and Broadbent. EARLY CRRIeTIANITY OF BRITAIN.—The first of a series ef ten lectures was delivered in the Newfield Institute, rn Monday night, by the Rev. G. W. Guest, on" The Early Christianity of Britain, A.D., 33-597." The lec- ture was illustrated with lantern slides, and there was a large audience. Mr. Horace Bayley manipulated the lantern.</p>
        <p>PRIMITIVE METHODIST ANNIVERSARY.—The anniversary services were conducted in the Primitive Methodist Chapel on Sunday, Rermous boing preached to good con- gregations by Mr. E. Watson. On Monday evening an illustrated lecture was delivered in the chapel by Mr. A. Mounfield, of Warrington, upon the subject of" Recent discoveries in Assyria and Babylon." Mr. G. L. Welford was chairman, and the Rev. J. R. Barnsley took part in the meeting, proposing at the conclusion a vote of thanks to the lecturer.</p>
        <p>LOCAL BOARD. —A special meeting of the L0C.3.1 Board was held in the Board-room, on Tuesday night when there were present—Mr. Gregson Via the chair), Messrs. J. Holt, Brazen- , dale, Whitelegge, Mclntyre, Appleton, Perrin, Ridgway (law clerk), Milliard (clerk).and Mort (surveyor).—With reference to the Heatley sewerage contract, it was decided that the Board accept the sum of ED from the contractor, and relieve him from all further responsi- bility.—Some discussion took place on the widening of Mill-lane, from Reatley Station to Wetgate-lane, the estimate of which was £9l 14s. 11d.—Mr. E. Holt con- sidered that the part of the road from Wetgate lane to Burford-lane was worst, and should be done first.—The Surveyor stated that the other part of the road would cost about £soo.—Mr. Ridgway was sure it would be the best for the parish if the Board borrowed the money which was practically £6OO, and widened the whole of the road.—lt was ultimately decided to widen the whole length of the road.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>MASONIC INSTALLkTION AT NEWTON</head>
        <p>MASONIC INSTALLkTION AT NEWTON</p>
        <p>The Masonic brethren of the Makerfleld lodge, No. 2155, held a very successful installation meet- ing on Tuesday afternoon at the Masonic rooms Pied Bull Hotel, Newton. Bro. E. L. Bent, W.M presiding. There was a large and influential attendance of members and visitors to do honour to the installation of their: esteemed Brother, Thomas Ormand. The following visiting brethren and past masters of the lodge were present, besides the officers and members of the lodge :—Bros. J. D. Murray, P.G. treas. of E. ; W. Goodaere. P.G. Sev. B. of E. Prov. G. Sec. ; J. Fletcher, Prov. S.G.D. ; C. E. flindley, P. Prov. S.G.D. ; R. Brierley, P.M. 2155, P. Prov. G. Supt. of W.; J. Arm- strong, P. Prov. G. Sun. of W. of Cheshire ; W. H. Robinson, F. Pow. : W. Bowden, P. Prov. G. S.W.B ; Stones, P.M. 2155. P. Prov. A.G. Sec :H. F. Greenough, W.M. 148; R. Cobb, I.P.M. 292; P. Aspinall, P.M. 484: R. T. Smith, WM. 1250; I. Leach. I.P.M. 1299; R. Green, W.M. 1354; W. H. Pennington, P.M. 1354; Yarwood. P.M. 1354; E. L. Bent, W.M. 2155; J. Adams, P.M. 2155; P. Garton. P.M. 2155 ; W. H. Arnett, J.P.M. 2155; G. Holme, J.P.M. 2294 ; T. Howard, 148 ; E. Waller, 148 ; R. Barton, 148 ; R. H. Pearson, 148 ; T. Jones, 178; W. J. Fairclough, 241 ; T. Ritchie, 484 ; T. Howard, 484 ;F. J. Emmett, 484; D. F. Maples, 1313; W. Ash- bury, 2294 ;W. Southern, 2405: S. Smith, 2463. The lodgebeing duly opened and the minutes read and con- firmed, the W.M. Bro. E. L. Bent installed Bro. T. Or- mand S.W. and W.M. elect, with full Masonic honours, as Worshipful Master of the Makerfield Lodge for the forthcoming twelve months, in which ceremony he was ably asssisted by Bro. H. Stones, &amp;c. The newly-in- stalled W.M. then appointed and invested the following brethren with the collars of their respective offices Bro. E. L. Bent. J.P.M. ; E Fairclough, S.W. ; C. i. Arnott, J.W. ; S. Hewitt, treas. ; J. Gooding. sec. ; C. H.Bodmer,S.D. ; C. S.Bennie,J.D. ; H. Stones.P.M.D.C.; W. F. Arnot% organist; J. Sankey, J.G. ; C. Starkie, J. Dobb, A. W. Anderton. and J. Singleton (stewards) ; J. H. Galloway, tyler. The W.M. then presented Bro. E. L. Bent, 1.P.M., with a handsome past master's jewel, as a token of esteem of the hrethren, and in recognition of his excellent services as W.M. during his veer of office. Bro. Bent tendered his heartfelt thanks for the gift, expressing the pleasure it gave him to see his services so kindly appreciated. Bro. H. Stones was then unani- mously chosen as the charity representeive of the lodge. Other business being disposed of, the lodge closed in due form, the brethren, about 55 in number, partook of an excellent banquet, provided by Mr. Dorman, of the Pied Bull hotel. The usual loyal and masonic toasts followed. Of these the toast of the " W.M.." proposed by Bro. E. L Bent, 1.P.M., and the toast of "The Masonic Charities" which was responded to with great eloquence by Bro. J. D. Murray, P.G., treasurer of E., need special mention, the former being honoured with enthusiasm, and the latter receiving the hearty acclama- tion due to such a worthy object. Excellent songs given by Bros. W. H. Pennington, P.M., S. Smith, T. Jones and T. Ritchie, enhanced the pleasure of the even- ing, and the Tyler's toast brought to a close the eighth anniversary festival of the lodge</p>
      </div>
