      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>DISTRICT COUNCILS.</head>
        <p>DISTRICT COUNCILS.</p>
        <p>RATEPAYER, MEETING AT TIAIDOCK. A large meeting of the ratepayers of Ilaydock was held in the Colliery Schools, on Thursday even- ing to take into consideration the question of the members Of the district council, and if possible to come to some arrangement in order to avoid a eon- test.—Mr. T. D. Grimke-Drayton said there seemed to be some doubt as to whether the gentleman who presided over that meeting could sit, or vote as councillor or guardian, and it wouid not be fair to ask any gentleman to preside unless he thoroughly understood that. That being the case he felt at once that something ought to be done,and he asked Mr. Ellis what he would advise, and he said unless they could get some ratepayer who would sacrifice himself for the good of the township they should get some outsider. Looking round, the only gentleman that he knew so far, who was not a voter, was Mr. Barrett, and he would move that that gentleman preside over the meeting.—The Rev. J. H. Ridette seconded,and said he disagreed entirely with Mr. Drayton's remarks. —The Chairman said they all knew that the Local Boards in the country were doomed, and that on the 17th December a new Board of District Councillors came into existence. He read the notice convening the meeting, and said the district had been divided into three wards, east, central, and west, with four representatives for each ward.— Mr. Grimke Drayton said the first thing they had got to decide was whether the ratepayers could agree together mutually to select 12 men and no more. If they could come to that arrangement they would :hyoid an election, but if they did not come to that arrangement they must have an election, if it was the desire of the people of Haydock to avoid a contest then the question was, to find out from that meeting whether it was their wish to have only 12 men nominated. Personally, he should be very glad if the ratepayers of Ilaydock could see their way not co have an election, for several reasons. First, the question of the new Council was not very well understood ; second, they knew that in future when elections took place, only a certain number of the Coun- cil would retire. That arrangement was a wise one, and it was wise for the reason that all men knew that when they had a Committee of Management, it was not desirable that the whole Committee should disappear altogether, because when others were elected in their places, very likely they would not understand how the work was done. He thought they could not do better than elect the present members of the Board, and elect three other gentlemen to make the number up to 12, as by this arrangement, he thought the best interests of the township weuld be served. (Hear, hear).— Mr. J. Billinge said it appeared from the remarks of the previous speaker, that the present nine members of the Board int.nded to stand as councillors and if that was so, he should like to know how they were going to distribute themselves.—Mr. Grimke Drayton proposed that amy gentleman who desired to stand as councillor should give his name in to the Chairman, stating what ward he wished to stand for.—The proposition was seconded.—The Rev. J. H. Ridette said he thought Mr. Drayton would secure greater unanimity if he would modify his proposition hy adding that "all who intend to nominate should give in a name."—Mr. Drayton agreed to this, and it formed part of his proposition. The following names were then mentioned as candi- dates for the different wards :—East ward, Messrs. W. Balharry,J. Tinton, J, Turner,Thomas Cunliffe, Thomas Harrison, Thomas Naylor; We‘t ward, Messrs. John Pilling, John Waine, 11. Griffiths, James Laurenson„ William Picton, James Turton and Dr. Dowling; Central ward, Messrs. John Jackson, John Forshaw, James Cook, T. D. Grimke Drayton, Thomas Read, David Taylor, Jacob Billinge, John Norcross. All they) gentle- men having signified their intention of standing. The Rev. J. H. Ridette said the next best thing to be done was to convene a meeting in each ward. If they were to have a compromise. in order to find whether such compromise was possible, it must be done in eacks ward.</p>
        <p>The following were selected to contest the various wards, as Guardians of the poor: East word, Mr. Bal- harry, and Rev. Father Moore ; West ward, Rev. J. H. Ridette and Mr. John Norcross; Central ward, Mr. John. Turton, Mr. J. Billiege and Mr. T. Hartison. A vote of thanks to the Chairman terminated the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>WESTON.</head>
        <p>WESTON.</p>
        <p>SELECTION OF CANDIDATES FOR THE PARISH COUNCIL.— Both the Church and Nonconformist parties in Weston Point have held meetings co decide upon candidates for the Parish Council. The church party have decided to nominate the following five gentlemen at the meeting to be held on December 4th: Mr. Richard Clark, Mr. Moores (Salt Union Works), Rev. D. S. Bowen (vicar), Mr. John JefTs (farmer), and Mr. Rosenberg (local man- ager for the Weaver Navigation). The Nonconformists have decided to nominate Mr. B. liniveton and Mr. J. P. Chadwick and it is expectKl that in a few days several other names will be added. The number o! councillors required is five. At Weston, no steps have yet been taken in the matter.</p>
