        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY SESSIONS. WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. G. Comber and Mr. J. J. Bleckly. -</p>
        <p>DRUNK AND DlsounE-r-ti.i=7-LVVilliam Burgess and Ed. Ryder were charged with bainz drunk and disorderly in Liverpool-road, on the 10:h November.—Fined 53. each.</p>
        <p>DOG 0% NERS FlNED.—Thomas Kaye, Sankey, for al- lowing his dog to be at large on 14th November, was fined 23. 63. in common with the following :—Andrew Rathbone, Penketh, for the 16th November ; Charles Kilshaw, Cuerdley, the 3rd November; John Hunt, Penketh, 12th November, fined 7s. 61 SQUABBLING RELATIONS.—Kate Gerrard was charged with having on the 17th November used threatening language to Elizabeth Harrison, at Sankey.—Mrs. Har- rison, the complainant, residing at Sankey, said that on the date named the defendant used threstening lan- guage to her. Defendant was her daughter-in-law.— Mrs. Brown,stepdaughter of complainant, corroborated. —Defendant was bound over to keep the peace for three months in her own recognizance. OCCUPATION CERTIFICATE.—Thomas Greenhalgh, Croft, was charged with having on the 26:h October last, being the owner of certain houses in Winwick allowed them to be occupied without first obtaining a certificate as to their sanitary condition.—Defendant pleaded guilty. Mr. Kerfoot (nuisance inspector) said that the offence was committed through ignorance therefore he did not wish to press the case. Defendant was ordered to pay the costs. DOMESTIC INFELICITIES.—Peter Griffiths was charged with assaulting his wife, Elizabeth Griffiths, with using threats towards her on 16th November.—The complain- ant said that on the 16th November the defendant struck her and used threats to do her bodily harm. He had struck her several times before.—Defendant admitted the assault and was bound over to keep the peace for three months in his own recog,nizances. FOUND IN POSSESSION OF GAME.—George Foster. an elderly man, was charged with having on the 13th November teen found in unlawful possession of game in Cow Lane, Sankey.—Sergeant Devlin stated that at 11.45 a.m. on the 13th of this month he was in Cow Lane, Great Sankey, near to a wood belonging to the Bewsey estiete. While there he met the defendant with a bass on his arm. The officer being suspicious searched the bass and found in it a hare bleeding from the nose and only just dead. Be asked defendant where he got it and received as an answer "I found it on the road." Defendant offered the sergeant the hare as a bribe rot to give information about it. He was fined 20s. and costs. ALLEGED ROBBERY OF A HAT.—George Cosgrove was charged with having stolen from the person of Martin Haverty in Bridge-st., Earlestown, on the 11th Novem- ber a hat value 4s. Forshaw (Percy Davies and Forsbaw) appeared for the defendant hut as the com- plainant did not appear the case was dismissed.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS. hloNnAr.—Before Mr. Philip Speakman, and Mr. T. Handley. _ _</p>
        <p>LICENSE TRANSFERS.—John Edwards applied for per- mission to sell until the next tr,insfer day at the Mariners Hotel, previously occupied by Joseph Hon.—Samuel Soann also applied for permission to sell at the Friend- ship Tavern in lieu of Thomas Finningham.—Both appli- cations were granted. A DOG OWNER IN TROUBLE.—Wm. Sheen for allowing his dog to be at large unmuzzled at Runcorn on the 4th November was fined the costs, 4s. 6d. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—For this offence in Church- St. oa the 17th inst. Patrick Duggan was fined ss. and costs or in default 14 days' imprisonment. ASSAULTING A POLICEMAN.—Patrick Macdonald, no address, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Lord-street, on Sunday, the 18'h inst., and further charged with having at the same time and place as- saulted P.G. Gregory whilst in the execution of his duty. Prisoner pleaded guilty to the first charge but denied the latter offence.—P.C. Gregory deposed that he was on duty in plain clothes at 10.30 on the date named, in Lord-st., Runcorn, when two disreputable women came , towards him. He informed them who he was, where- I upon they shouted out "Pat and John." Two men came forward, one of them being the prisoner. They struck witness to the ground and also kicked him several times. The prisoner was the most desperate of the two. The other man succeeded in getting away.—P.C. Butler gave evidence as to aseisting the last witness to bring the psisoner to the police station. The oficer was struggling with the prisoner when he first saw him. The second man folowed behind them a short distance. but'afterwards went along Queen-st. He afterwards as- certained that he went over the bridge. Witness, in cross-examination, admitted that P.C. Gregory struck the prisoner but it was in self-defence.—The magistrates imposed a fine of 53. and 7s. 4d. costs or 14 days' im- prisonment for the first offence ; and 10. and ss. costs or 14 days imprisonment for the a.ssaulr. LODGING IN A CAB.—John Steele, who stated that he belonged .to Widnes, was charged with unlawfully wan- dering abroad, and with having lodged in a cab belong- ing to Mr. Dutton, on the previous evening. The evid- ence given by the police showed that about eleven o'clock the previous evening the prisoner was found asleep in a cab on Mr. Dutton's premises. He was awakened and found to be the worse for drink. He was lodged at the Police Station for the remainder of the night.—Mr. Dutton said he had been put to a great deal of trouble by people getting, into the yard during the night time and sleeping in the cabs. He had missed several articles on previous ()evasions, and had also had two cab windows broken owing to the practice.—The prisoners excuse was that he was without lodging money He had never visited the place before. A fine of 54. was imposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>---- AIT UNNATURAL SON SENT TO GAOL FOR Two MONTHS. A case of a young man's brutal and unnatural treat- ment of his parents was brought befcre the magistrates at the Borough Court on Saturday morning. John William Travis (21) was charged with assaulting and beating his mother, Mary Travis, 58, Winwick-road, on the 13th inst.—Mrs. Travis, the complainant, said that the defendant struck her several times on the date named. He had done so repeatedly during the last two years and was the master of both her and her husband, the latter not daring to say anything to defendant when he misha.ved himself.—&amp; neighbour said that she heard complainant scream on the day in question and went to interpose, but defendant told her to go as she had nothing to do with it. Witness said that defendant often ill•treated his mother and she had seen him struggling with his father and had sent her husband to stop him.— Sergeant Chantler said that on Tuesday evening he went to the house in Winwick-road to serve a summons on defendant. He found the complainant outside as the defendant wag in the house and had turned her out. The sergeant said he could see defendant peeping out of the bedroom window and it took him some time to get in the house. The parents of the defendant were, be said, very respectable people and be had known them for a long time, and the defendant had always been a "bad lad."—Mr. Burgess (chairman of the Bench) said that he had not heard a worse case in that Court for many years and defendant would have to go to gaol for two months with bard labour. Defendant (surprised) "Two months !" Mr. Burgess (sternly) "Yes, and you havn't got all you deserve, either. Defendant was then conducted below. A TROUBLESoME INNATE AT THE WORKHOUSE. Edwin Woods, a tramp, was brought before the War- rington magistrates on Monday, charged with being disorderly whilst an inmate in the Warrington workhouse on the 17th inst., and doing wilful damage to windows tothe extent of 7s. 4d. Mr. Shearer, who appeared on behalf on the Guardians, said that the prisoner was admitted into the workhouse on Friday night, and the following morning, he was given a quantity of stones to break before leaving. He was told by Mr. Curry at about ten o'clock that the stones were not broken small enough, whereupon the prisoner without any provocation whatever, deliberately smashed eleven panes of glass with his hammer. It had been ascertained that the prisoner had been guilty of similar condpct previously in other workhouses. He had been to prison seventeen times, and he (Mr. Shearer) asked the bench to inflict an exemphry punishment upon him--P.C. Leonard, and the porter. Curry, gave evidence in corroboration, and the nrisoner said that he had broken the windows becanse he was not going to prison for nothing.—He was sent to vaol for two months' hard labour. STRANGE CONDUCT OF A WARRINGTON MAN. At the Warrington Borough Court on Monday morn- ing. John Hughes, Rose and Crown-street. was charged with wandering bout anparently of unsound mind on the nrovious evening. P.C. Corcoran gave evidence to the effect that at about 10.30 the previous evening,there waa a crowd in Wharf-street. Two boys came and told him that a man was going to throw himself into the river. Witness went to hirn,and asked him what he was doing. Re replied that he was going to see his wife who had drowned herself. He was locked up for safetr.—A boy was also put into the how and said that the prisoner tried twice to throw himself into the river, and when the nolice were sent for, he had got his feet into the water.—Tn reply to the magistrates, the prisoner said that he was drunk at the time. but P.C. Corcoran said he only appeared to have had a drop of drink.—Prisoner's brother, who was in court, said that the prisoner had been going to the bad,nwing to drink and had company. He had twien a great tronble to the whole of his family. He had lately lost his wife, who had, however, not drowneAlierstelf, but died from consumntion.—lnsper- tor Sp'nirsi 93id prisoner had not been snrreqfnl week betting and that had preyeA his mind. 4e sall tnat the prisoner was an inoffensive man, and he wro diicharCiPd by the magistrates, on his brother promising to look after him. MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON ROROT7C4I4 POT,TCF, (7017 RT FRIDAIT.-13efore Mr. F. Monk. (ch,irman). Aldertnen Harrison and Wriobt. Connoitlor Piernoint, Dr. Gor- nail. and Mestirs.S. Chandler and S. Wriqht. DIMNIZXNWESS.—For this offence Patrick Jenr.i MN_ was fined ss. CRITEI.TY TO A "HORSE —ChlrlPsl RnhPeg WI4 chard with wr,rin,, a bor.. in gorgernarket., while in an • nnfit stat'e, on November 30. P.C. Timer grated that g the horce was very thin and emar,iat,d. and vra unfit for wo,k. Insnert,r Ltiff said the animal was systPrna- ticallr starved, and had 9ine been talren on hi g recom- mendation to bo stamzhtered.—Defendant was fined</p>
        <p>10a. WOMF.N'S Qrm/nrs,.—Ma.ryTarlor was chirged with assaulti,g. and beating Catherine Coffee. on November 11th. The evidence of the corrnlainant wag the there was a little dispute between her and defendant, and the latter qtrnek her in the fire. Several witnesses were called ither aide, end the defendant was fined 5. SATURDAY. —PPP"re Mr. J. Vt7lrcresAg (in the chair) and Mr. L Voisev. DRUNK ANT) PiSnP.DEPLY.—Ttif-rmact Starkev wa.s fined sa. for being d•iink and disorderly on Schiol Brow on Saturday morning. , AN ARMY riltftr.wrriv—TTPrhert, Andrewa was charged with deserting from the 2nl Welsh Fusiliers since the 13th in. p.r. Berry deposed to nnnrehending, the prisoner and anolied for tho nanal "reward, which was granted. Prisoner was remanded to Strangeways gaol to awaAt an eacort. Movnky—Befor. the Mayor (Councillnr :T. Fairclort,,h), F. Monks, Alderman Ffarrison, and Mr. J. W. Haw_</p>
        <p>ward. Driturfin4.ms.-17nr thia offmr-n Mrtin Mitnii&amp;l, 3, Ternm's-yard ; Jordan, 22, Dnlman's-lane, we e fined ss. ; and Jnbn JordAn, 22, 'DnlmAn-lane, dis.</p>
        <p>chard. DVIIIIRITIR.—EIorin Guy was remanded to Strantre. wars Ganl to await e=teort. on the cha,rNe of desertino- from fler Majesty's Battalion King's Own Yorkshire Reelment, stationed in Belfalt since Auquet- Alz ANNOYANCE TO PA9SSINGERS liamblett, Silver-square, NVng sent to gaol for 14 daya on the charge a being an annoyance to pas.,envers in Pinner's-brow, on Nr.vember Cunliffe who proved the case said that the nman was causing distnrbances on the spot ;O,(lVe mentioned. and NrIS a thorough nuisanes - TITA CATAPVLT IN lISF ArMlN.—Ttirhard Irons. 176. Knotsford-road, was lined 15. for elischa.rging s catapult. in Friar's Green, on Nov. 13th.—The defendant said that he was shooting snarrows. WEDWBSDA!. —Refer, Mr. John Fairclourth (chairman). and Mr. J. Gt-dds.„ DPtonc VD DISORDRPLY —Annie Bran was chsroed with beim? drunk and dicorderly in Biltermarket-st. on Tumilar nkrht. Ths case was prov,d and &amp;lent-lint sent to an for one month. he was Yery abusive anti appl'A for another seven dayg, but her request was not acce,l.d to.</p>
