      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>MR. CONINGSBY DISRAELI, M.P., AT</head>
        <p>MR. CONINGSBY DISRAELI, M.P., AT</p>
        <p>LYMM.</p>
        <p>A conversazione, followed by a meetina, was held on Wednesday evening at the Lcrnm Conservative Club. Mr. T. P. Norris presided at the meeting, and amongst those on the platf rot were Mr. Coningshv Disraeli, Mr. J. Martin, Mr. A. W. Callender, Mr. E. Gibbon. Mr. P. Kessler, Mr. J. Marsh, and others. The Chairman pro- posed a resolution of con Hence in the Unionist party, and in their member for the division, Mr. CnninoshY Disraeli. The resolution was seeonded by Mr. E. Gibbon and carried unanimously.—Mr. Disraeli said the Radical party had shown a tendency to go on so fast that it was not able to create the machinery to carry out its work. Now that the Radicals were in the majority inthe House of Commons they showed a disp,sition to quarrel with the House of Lerds. It was a had workman who (lea r- relied with his tools. The House of Lords was not the antiquated body they represented it to b.. Day by day and year by year it received new blond in the shape or sons of peers, and took into its ranks distinguished soldiers and sailors. A House like that was not one which would withstand the will of the people. Had the House of Lords intended to defy the people they would have thrown out th. Budget which was a direct attack upon the pockets of the members of that House. The cry of "Down with the House of Lords," was merely intendfd to hide the incapacity of the, Government to mit any sound measure on the statute- book. 'The country was not likely to he taken in by it. "He who cheats me once, shame on him; he whc cheats me twice, shame (in me." said the old Quaker. The House of Lords had stood tip for the working-man in the case of the Employers' Liability Bill, and had shown its sympathy with him in many other ways. He believed that Lymm would shortly have a District Council. They were told by their (apponents that the Parish Councils Bill was a new charter of freedom to the working-man. It was nothinc, of the kind; it only gave to the small ratepayers powers which they had exercised before under different names and through a different medium. There was hardly anything which these councils had ti do which could not have been done before by the ves- tries or the lncal hoards. The Conservative party ought to make their influence felt in the elections for the Dis- trict and Parish Councils, for their opponents were mak- ing political capital of the forthcoming contests. The farming industry throughout the country, Mr. Disraeli went en to say, was in a most deplorable condition, and the only remedy the Government bad to offer was a Royal Commission. English wheat at the present time was selling in Wycombe market at 155., a price which was utterly unremunerative to the grower. When the Unionists came into power they would see that some at- tempt was made •to remedy this state of affairs. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>PEN IZETH.</head>
        <p>PEN IZETH.</p>
        <p>CHURCH OF ENOLAND TEMPERANCE SOC ETy.—On Mn- day night the first of the monthly ehtertainments for the season was held in the infants school. The Rev. J. R. Jones was in the chair. Hymns were sung and recita- tions and songs ware given by the following :—Misses Owen and Foster, Mr. W. H. Owen, Misses Woodward, Dobson, Wheeler. Pownall, Finch and 'Masters Dakin and Foddy. Mr. A. Dignum gave an address on temper- ance work in India. Mrs. Norman accompanied the artistes.</p>
        <p>Salvador Franch, the Anarchist who threw a bomb from the gallery of the Licen Theatre ani caused the deaths of several people, was executed on Wednesday morning. at Barcelona. The prisoner displayed great bravado from the time he recognised that intervention was hopeless until the very last. A greengrocer named Job Hartland was sentenced to death at Gloucester Assizes on Wednesday for the mur• der of his wife and youngest son, aged thirteen. _</p>
        <p>The Argentine Attorney-General has recommended the Government to allow the criminal charges against Jabez Balfour to be proceeded with at Salta before his extradition to Engla.nd. H.M. cruiser Phoebe has arrived at Ascension, with her crew stricken down by fever as the result of her long stay on the West Coast of Africa. Six deaths have nrrnrrnA.</p>
        <p>BIRDS, A N DIALS AND FISH ST(JteVicl), awl mounted in Glass Cases of any design. HEADS and HORNS mounted ale e for Sale. Birds made into Hand Screens or Fire Screens. Dealer in British and Foreign Birds and Animals' Skins, Glass Shades. Stags' Heads. Cabinets Stare Boxes, Nets. for Britterfily and Egg Collectors, —B. CoOK,:, Naturalist and :Furrier, Renshaw-street, Liverpool</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 24, 1894.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 24, 1894.</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>GEO. G. BTJSSEY &amp; CO. are the leading makers o requisites for Football, Hockey, Golf, Baseball, Gymnasia, and all Sports and Games. Illlustrated catalogues may be obtained from the local dealers, or the Sports Manufactory Peckham, London. Trade &lt;CCBC„ Mark.</p>
        <p>to nil in tavour of the visitors. Tue secona halt opened in favour of the homesters who now had the wind in their favour, but although scoring one goal, they were weak in front and were defeated by four goals to one. PETER WALKER AND SONS V. ORFORD.—rhA above teams met at Orford on Saturday to play for the wooden spoon of the 2nd division of the Warrington League. Half-time arrived with the score. Brewery, four goals ; Orford, 2. Being in a minority Orford com- menced rushing, and by a combined effort scored. The game was finished in darkness, but not before the Brewery scored again, and they were able te compli- ment themselves on having won their first League match. Final—Brewery, 6 goals; Orford 3 goals. RUNCORN V. HUNSLET.—The Yorkshire clubs down on the Runcorn fixture e ird are few and far between, and consequently the engagement with the well-known :1 ae,l,•t- .en et Runcorn, on Slturday, was looked for- ward to with inure Lilac' usual interest. In the previous contest at Leeds,Flunslet ran out victorious by one goal. two tries to two tr. es, but it was generally expected that Runcorn would reverse this defeat when they met the Hunslet team at Runcorn. About 2,000 spectators were present when W. Faulkner started the ball, Run- corn being short of Houghton and Taylor. Soon after the commencement Robinson initiated some passing, which was intercepted,but Butterworth saved finely. W. Faulkner made headway, but offside spoiled the advan- tage, Mitchell giving good relief, which gave the Tykes a footing in the home half, but the Cheshire forwards quickly took them back, some interesting play taking place at midfield. Wareer and W. Faulkner made a fine passing run, the ball being taken to the Hunslet lines. The visitors: relieved by a strong forward rush, and a fine kick by Mitchell sent the ball dead. Follow- ing the kick out, Leonard got his kick charged down, and a try ought to have been the outcome, the oppor- tunity being lost in attempting to pick up. Pretty play between W. Faulkner, Cross and Warder, ended in Cross getting in behind the posts, but a forward throw spoiied the efforts. However from the next scrimmage J. Faulkner passed out to Robinson, who gave to Warder and that player scored, but failed at goal. Half-time score: Runcorn 1 try, Hunslet nil. In the second half Warder scored another unimproved try for Runcorn,and Cavanagh for Hunslet also scored a try, a goal resulting. Final score, Runcorn 2 tries; Hunslet 1 goal. RUNCORN RECREATION V. BIRKENHEAD WANDERERS " A " TEAM.—This match took place at Runcorn on Saturday. The Wanderers arrived short of a couple of players, but were assisted by two Runcorn men in Wor- rall and Leathwood. The Recs played their usual team. Birkenhead won the toss and Whitney started. In the first half of the game the visitors had rather the best of the play, Leathwood scoring a try for them. At half time the score was Birkenhead Wanderers one try to nil. The visitors re-started, and a good return being made caused the game to be waged in the Wanderers' territory Booth soon afterwards scored an unimproved try for Runcorn. After a spell of desultory play in midfiel Hughes, standing close up, secured the ball, and again ran over in a good position. Booth in this instance suc- ceeded in placing a goal. Score, Runcorn Recreation, one goal one try and three minors ; Birkenhead Wan- derers " A," one try two minors. WARRINGTON ST. MARY'S V. ST. HELENS "A."—At St. Helens, on Saturday. St. Helens won the toss. Sanders started for the visitors against the wind, and Gormley returned well to centre. St. Mary's at once pressed, with the result that Bennett got in at the cor- ner in the first five minutes. Hampson took the place kick, and succeeded in landing a goal. From a scrim- mage Higginbottom secured and passed to Hampson, who raced away and scored. The same player failing at goal. This vverse roused the homesters, who rushed the leather to the visitors' 25, but were re- pulsed by the smart combination of the St. Mary's backs. St. Helens had now a turn, and the visitors' defence was menaced for a few minutes, but the homesters could not break through the home defence. Each end was visited in turn, when a round of passing by the St. Helens backs was stopped by Roughley. From a scrimmage at half- way Hammon got possession and ran well, and kicked across to O'Brien, who scored, Hampson failing to con- vert. Nothing more occurred up to half-time, when St. Mary's were leading by one goal two tries. to St. Helens nil. After 'the opening exchanges, St. Helens made tracks for the visitors' goal, but the splendid defence of the visitors forced them to retire. Rutter again secured, tricked all his opponents in grand style, and scored a well-merited try. A rush by the home forwards was saved by Burrows, and after a few minutes at half-way the St. Mary's for% ards broke away, and completely bombarded the home goal. Unsworth gave relief by a sneart run to centre, but was overhauled by Hampson in the nick of time. Higginbottom added another try, and Roughley failed. Final result :—St. Mary's, one goal, four tries ; St. Helens " A " nil.</p>
        <p>PARISH COUNCILS.</p>
        <p>THE COMING ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>To-day is the date upon which the assistalat over- seer of the various townships gives notice of the parish meeting will be held upon December 4th in every parish in England and Wales. It will there- fore be an opportune moment te recapitulate the chief items of interest which should be known by the population which lives in the villages. Any person is eligible for a seat upon the parish council who has resided for a period of twelve months within three miles from the parish, so that there is ample choice of selection of candidates. On Decem- ber 4th at the parish meeting, the names of tha candidates nominated will be put to the meeting by the chairman, in alphehetical order and the persons receiving the hightest number of votes, according to the number of councillors required, will then be declare 4 elected. Should the friends of any of the defeate4 candidates be dissatisfied they can demand a poll, which in the parishes in the Warrington District will take place on Dec. 17. This election will be by ballot, and the returning officer will be Mr. J. C. Sutton, clerk to the Guar- dians, or a person nominated by him as his deputy. The elected members will continue in office until April 1896, after which the elections will take place annually. As the councillors will have con- siderable power, and in years to come will have probably increased power. it is of the highest im- portance that persons who have the welfare of the whole community at heart and not that of a certain class, should be Vac( d upon the Board. `NEU begun is half done,anel it, is th-refore to be hoped that the parish councils will begin well by having fit and capable men upon them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>LA.TE CZAR.</head>
        <p>THE LA.TE CZAR.</p>
        <p>RUSSIA'S LAST TRIBUTE TO HIS REMAINS. THE MOST STRIKING PAGEANT OF THE 19Tu CENTURY.</p>
        <p>The remains, now lying in St. Peter and St. Paul Cathedral, St. Petersburg, will soon be committed to their last resting-place. Royal and Imperial families and the Governments of all Europe are honourably repre- sented in this last sorrowful scene, to pay their tribute of mourning and respect. The profound griet,universal respect and sympathy shown by the masses that greeted the funeral cortege in the different cities that were touched en route to St. Petersburg from Livadia clearly demonstrate that the Czar of All the Russias was loved, not only of kigns and princes, but no one unprejudiced can deny that the lower classes of Russia—from Vlarlivostock on the Pacific, to the fortresses of the Caucasus—in one plaintive voice unite their prayers for the soul's repose of their " Peasant Cz tr." All St. Petersburg presents the acme of sincere mourn- in.. Not that conventional mourning prescribed by etiquette, but the mourning of genuine distress ; a grief for a personal loss which echoes in the heart of this great nation. Many acts during his reign are recalled which exem- plify his kindness of heart and sound judgment, sa za- city and perspicuity, his love for peace, his kindness to relieve those suffering from plague and famine, and his domestic loyalty. But we are all liable to mistakes, and it is a deplorable fact that the late Czar was not exempt. Perhaps the greatest mistake that his Imperial Highness made during his eleven years' reign was that.he did not heed the warning given two years ago and resort to the only known Cure for Bright's Disease—Warner's " Safe " Cure—the moment the first symptoms of Kidney disease was manifest. It can hardly be considered a proprietary medicine, but rather a discovery for men arid women troubled with any kind of kidney, liver, or urinary weaknesses as suggested by the depressed feelings, pain over loins, changeable, or loss of appetite, erratic pain under the shoulder blades or occasional white or red brick-dust deposits in the liquid passed. There are thousands of men and women in England to-day who have been rescued and kept alive wholly by the use of this great remedy. And if you, reader, are suffering from any trouble which you do not fully understand, you may be sure that it arises from dis- ordered Kidneys or Liver. If so, heed the warning which the Czar neglected, and save yourself while you have an opportunity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>WATtRINGTON HIGHWAY BOARD.</head>
        <p>WATtRINGTON HIGHWAY BOARD.</p>
        <p>The quarterly meeting of the above Board :was held in Bewsey Chambers on Wednesday afternoon when there were present: Captain T. E. Witbing- ton (chairman), Messrs. Royston, R. Owen, jun., Millington, Lowe, Kerfoot, P. J. Bate. IL Bate, Ormrod, Clare, Grounds, H. B. White (clerk), E. A. White (deputy clerk), Thompson (surveyor). HALTON'S BRIDGE. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The SURVEYOR reported that he had interviewed Mr: Tunstall with regard to the proposed alteration of Hal- ton's Bridge, Penketh. He had consented to allow the Board to have a strip of land of 25 square yards, for £25, and certain other reservations, whereby the road could be improved and raised.—Mr. MituNoToN pro- posed that the Surveyor be empowered to carry out the arrangement provided no other difficulty arose.—Mr. H. BATE seconded the motion which was carried unani- mously.</p>
        <p>PADGATE-LA.NE IMPROVEMENT. The SURVEYOR said that the road near the bridge in Padgate-lane was so narrow, that the owner of the pro- perty, Mr. Antrobus, had offered to give land to make it wider, provided that the Board would put up iron mil- itias and stone coping. This would cost the Board £2B. —The Surveyor was authorised to carry out the scheme, on the motion of Mr. RoirsToN, seconded by Mr. CLARB.</p>
        <p>RESIGNATION OF THE ASSISTANT SURVEYOR. Mr. LESTER THOMP.3ON having tendered his resignation on being appointed clerk and surveyor to the Haydock Local Board, the resignation was accepted on the motion of Mr. OWEN, seconded by Mr. MILLINGTON.—Mr. THOMPSON suggested that the Board do not appoint a new assistant surveyor, but allow him to appoint one himself, ond that the £45 salary be given to him instead. —Mr. OWEN gave notice that Mr. Thompson's salary be increased £45, and that he nrovide his own assistant.</p>
        <p>WINWICK IMPRoVEIIENT SCEIEWIE. The conveyancing of the property in Winwick which is to be pulled down to straighten the road in Winwick was passed.—Mr. ORSIROD observed that the improve- ment would save scores of lives.—Mr. KERFOOT "There have never been any killed yet." (Loud laughter.l— discussion arose with reference to an old beam in the calf house, which was formerly in Win' wick Church. The membars of the Board were in favour of returning it to Winwick Church, buc the CLERK observed that as the property had not yet come into th e hands of the Board, the Newton Commissioners could d as they liked with it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>WYCLIFFE CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH.</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH.</p>
        <p>P.S.A. TEA MEETING.</p>
        <p>The annual tea meeting in connection with the Warrington Wycliffe P S.A. was held on Tuesday at the Wycliffe Hall, Bewsey-road, when a very large number of P.S.A. members attended. Tea was •mm- menced at 7 p.m., and lasted till 8. Owing to the large attendance two sittings were provided. After tea an adjournment was made to the chapel, it being found impossible to provide accommodation for every- one in the Hall. The chair was occupied by Mr. T. Morris, F.G.S., who was accompanied by the Rev. J. Yonge and Mr. Risque of Manchester. At the com- mencement</p>
        <p>Mr. MOHlus said he accepted the chairmanship because he knew he should enjoy himself better there than any- , where else. It was a treat to him to see so many ' happy faces. He was sure that no married woman, daughter, or sister in that room regretted that their husbands, fathers. or brothers had joined the P.S. movement. He did not believe, next to religion, that there was a movement in this town more capille of moralising and christianising the working Men's wives' sad families that the one with which they were con- nected, and he wished it God-speed. It had been so far, most snecessful; The one hour ori Sunday afternnons at the P.S. A. was he thought the most precious to thQ,ce ' peonle of all the hours in the week. Some men did not enjoy themselves as much in two months as the mem- bers of the P.S.A. did in one hour. The other denomina- tion; that had started this movement he hoped would he successful. (Applause.) He wished the Church of England would take it up and aim at getting the Sus- day afternoon movement in their midst and endeavour to make their people as happy as Mr. Yonge and his , committee had done. ,</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. YONGE expressed his pleasure at seeing their Chairman in his place, and said that he intended to make his speech "brief, bright, and brotherly." Fie Mid his committee were delighted to see such a large attendance. It was his opinion that they should make their churches more useful than they were, and more- over, he had ha a desire for many years viz., if it were possible, to have tilu churches in Warrington, and Wy- cliffe Pspecially, open onee a Week for a concert for the people. He bad na doubt that they would soon he able to make their church Df more use than it was. It was already more useful because of the P.S.A.meetinas. (Applause.) What they wanted was to do away with all starch and buckram, and the people would come quicker than ever. Mr. BISQUE then gave a very humorous address, and expressed an opinion that women also should have a weekly meeting. They would, he had no doubt," beat the men hollow."</p>
        <p>Songs was rendered by Miss Cosgrove. and Miss Pem- berton. A trombone solo was well executed by Mr. J. Smith, and an encore was demanded. Mr. S. Butter- field gave as a recitation " The Bashful Man," mid res- ponded to an encore. After a few more -words hy the Chairman and the Rev. J. rouge the meeting ter- minated.</p>
        <p>William Henry Sedgwick, rate collector to the Fedhergh Board of Guardians, was sentenced at the Sedbergh Petty Sessions on Tuesday to six months imprisonment for misappropriating the sum of £373 belonging to the Board.</p>
        <p>The time closed on Tuesday. for reoeiving tenders for the new 21 per cent. redeemable loan of £901,100 required by the Liverpool Corporation. The tot a amount offered by the public was £2,900,000, more than three times the amount required ; and the terms ale stated, on the highest authority to be extremely satis- factory to the Corporation. Mr. Herbert Gladstone, M.P., addressing his constitu- ents at Leeds on Tuesday evening, pronounced himself in favour of a Second Chamber on the lines laid down by Lord Rosebery. The police made a raid on Wednesday on the club pre misesat 59A, Paternoster-row, London, which are al. leged to have been used for illegal betting, and effected twenty arrests.</p>
        <p>An explorer who has recently arrived in Faris from the Central Soudan states tht four Europeans are held prisoners by the Taurez tribes, amongst them being the French explorer, Captain Flatters, who was believed to have been murdered in 1881.</p>
      </div>
