      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>RUGBY. WARRINGTON V. HALIFAX.</head>
        <p>RUGBY. WARRINGTON V. HALIFAX.</p>
        <p>The Halifax team, the Yorkshire Cupholders for the past two years, were left without a fixture on Saturday owing to the suspension of Wigan. The Warrington club accordingly made arrangements whereby their fixtures with Tranmere Wanderers were cancelled, and Halifax taken on instead. A great deal of interest was taken in the match, as the two teams had not met for a number of years, and fixtures with Yorksoire clubs are not of a too frequent occurrence on the Warrington fixture card. Owing to Lancashire and Yorksnire hav- ing county matches on Saturday, there were absentees in both NVarrington and Halifax teams. Jolley was playing for Lancashire, and Dakin also received a tele- gram to proceed to Manchester, but although he went, his services were not required. The Halifax team were minus Firth, Jackson, Rigg, and Riley, and the follow- ing is the full list of the opposing teams. Warrington: Full back, Boscow ; Cunningham, Heesom, Burton, and goores, three-quarters; Bate and Clark, half backs; Turner, Hampson, Taylor, Berry, Nevins, Buckley, Bailey and Andrews, forwards. Halifax: Pugh, full back; Chorley, Keepings, Bottoanlev, and Egerton, three-quarters; Arnold and D. G. Wheelwright, half backs; Ripley, Robertshaw, Miles, W. Wheelwright, Kitson, Knowles, Dickenson, and Webster, forwards. The ball was set in motion by Webster, and Boseow, by a good punt returned with interest. A scrimmage was secured by Warrington, and Pugh just saved his side from disaster. The ball was again secured by Cunningham, and a timely pass to Bate sent the play to the visitors' quarter flag. From the ensuing scrimmp Bate quickly passed to Nevins, and before the FL ax players were a ware Of it,the latter had got over the line. Taylor's kick at goal was a failure. A lead of three points after only three minutes' play was very encourag- ing to the Warrington players, but Halifax kept up their spirits, and the kick out was so well followed that they almost got in, Turner just stopping the ball in time. Keeping' up the attack and clu nging a kick by Boscovv, he was not pulled up until he had reached the goal mouth. The scrimmage was decided in Warrington's favour, and good following up took play to the oppo- site end of the field. At this point a series of short pas- ses among the Halifax fairly baffled Warrington. Keep- ings commenced the movement, which was partaken of by Bottomley, Egerton, Chnrley, and Arnold. The latter player upon being tackled by Burton passed to Ripley, who dodging Boscow, crowned the effort by scoring, and Keepings landed a good goal. Egerton met the ball at the restart, and after a fine run he passed to Bottomley, who, however, was grassed by Heesnm. Clark now gave evidence of his ability to play Associa- tion, for he threaded his way through the whole of the Halifax team by a dribble which extended almost half the length of the field. His effort was further supple- mented by Andrews, but there was no tangible result, Turner attempting to pick up the ball and being tackled by Pugh. Warrington were pres- sing hard at this point of the game, and all the efforts of the Tykes to clear were unavailing. Cunningham had hard lines on several occasions, once being knocked into touch near the corner. Burton now had a drop at goal, but his kick falling short, the ball rebounded off the back of Pugh near the line,and almost let Warringtien in. A minute later, a loud shout announced that Buckley had crossed the Halifax lines in a favourable position. Heesom was successful with the place, and half-time arrived soon afterwards, with the score Warrington one goal, one try; Halifax, one goal. Burton commenced hostilities after the interval, and Knowles failing to respond to his kick off. Clark was almost over the first minute. A rush of the Halifax for- wards was neutralised by alternate passing between Bate and Clark, which ought to have added something tangible. Halifax again gained ground, and for once Boscow misfielded the ball. Heesom however succeeded in dropping on the ball, and thus staved off the danger. Moores who had been very quiet in the first half of the game, now received the ball from Clark, and made a most brilliant run. Finding Pugh in the way near the line he kicked over that gentleman's head, hut the kick being slightly too strong, the ball went dead. Moores again secured the ball after the re-start, and aroused the enthusiasm of the spectaters by another dodgy run and had very hard lines indeed in not scoring. Another opening was spoiled by a knock forward. Halifax were completely overplayed by Warrington, and were very fortunate in keeping their opponents at bay. A sequence of passing was commenced by Clark who passed to Burton, and he to Cunningham only to see that player again knocked into touch at the corner. Halifax now for the first time in the second moiety took play to the half-way line, but their stay was of short duration, for Burton, Bate and Moores, again placed their lines in jeopardy. A series of scrimmages ensued on the Halifax line, and from one of these Bailer passed the coveted line. Turner's kick at goal fell short. This took place five minutrs off time, and Warrington were playing with almost as much vigour as at the commencement of the game. Conse- quently the pressure was kept up, and time after time a score seemed inevitable. Success at length again came to Warrington, when Bate gathering the hall passed to Burton, forced his way ever the line. Nevins took the kick and landed a goal, but for some reason ,the referee refused to allow it. The whistle immediately afterwards sounded, leaving Warrington victors by the unexpectedly large majority of one goal three tries to a goal. The match was a most enjoyable one, in fact, it is the best that Warrington has played this season. There was a total absence of rough play, and the players on both sides are to be complimented on the good temper shown. The game was also a very open and fast one, and most interesting from a spectator's point of View. STOCKTON HEATH V. WIDNES Sr. MARYS.—The above combinations met at Widnes, on Saturday. The visitors were minus the services of Cross (who was assist- ing Runcorn), Lally and F. Isherwood, whilst Widnes had a strong team. The home team kicked off, and several scrimmages took place in the visitors' 25. Getting away from the pack, Smith passed to Broady, who ran strongly, and then transferred to Barber, who had hard lines in not scoring, the Widnes full back touching down. The visitors' forwards nnw played a determined game and rushes] the hall into the home 25, hut the defence was safe and the ball travelled into the visitors' 25, where play settled for a while. Smith secured from a scrimmage and passed to Lnngmare,wh.n transferred to Rrnady,the latter player making a magni- fieent run to within five yards off the line, and then passing to Barber, the latter player had no difficulty in getting in between the posts, but the point was disallowed. This fine effort brought about half-time, no score being registered by either side. Stockton Heath resumed, and immediately pressed, and Hieham from a scrimmage nassed to Bather, who tricked his opponents and scored a clever try. Webster failing at the kick at goal. Broady now secured, and dashed over the line. securing for Stockton Heath a second try, Webster again making a splendid attempt at a difficult angle. Securing from the drop out, Webster made a brilliant run to the line, making a grand attempt to score, hut the defanee was 1-con secure. Time was now called with the visitors on the home lines, leaving Stock- ton Heath victors by two tries to nil.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON EXCELSTORS V. WEASTE.—The first meet- ing of the above teams took place at Warrington, on Saturday. The opening stages of the game saw the visitors attacking, but Knight was the stvimbling block to their progress. A neat run and kick by Melleston took hos-ilities near the visitors' lines,and Walshe quickly secured from a scrimmage, and after eluding several - opponents put in a huge kick, only. to SPO Knight respond. The oval went in touch at half way, where play raged for a• lengthy Period. A nice hit of passing by Raybold, Woods and Appleton saw the last-named running in, Eldlestnn improving. This roused up the visitors, who pressed up to the interval. which found the Excelsiors leading lw one coal to nil. Play in the second half was uneventful, and a good game ended in a win for the Warrington Excelsiors by one goal one try to Weasto nil. "Tut,. CASTLE RUBBER" V. RICTIHOND AND CO. This new organistion enened well on Saturday, when they tenk on Richmond's on their ground in Corsey- lane. Richmond started against the wind, and the Castle taking the ball to the home quarters, penning them on their line for some minutes ; from a rush by the Castle forwards, the hal! was taken over the line, a try being claimed but disallowed. On drnpning out, Richmond attacked for a chart time, until from scrimmage Green receiving from ,Cotton ran to inside the home half and passed to Buek for the latter to score a clever try,the kick at goal failing.. Half time arrived with the score, Castle one try, Richmond nil. In the second half Richmond had the wind, hot the Castle forwards combining better, held their own. Give and take play ensued to the end,and only once did the home team look like scoring, but Spruce relieved with a good kick to touch ; and time being called, the Castle retired the victors by nne try to nil. WARRINGTON RANGERS 2ND V. Moss BAN.—Mosg Bank journeyed to Warrington to try their strength with the Rangers, but had to retire home defeated. The Rangers won the toss, and elected to play with the wind. Some good passing by the visitors, almost resulted in them scoring, but they were repulsed by the hemestere full hack, who was playing a grand game. A fine dribble by Monks, resulted in that player getting over, the goal kick failing. Half time was shortly afterwards called with the score, Rangers 1 try, Moss Bank, nil. The home team kicked off in the second half, and were soon seen to force the visitors to defend, hut a good kick by the full hack, kept them at hay. Soon afterwards Monks again got over, thus scoring his second try, Menne kick- ing a beaullful veal. Time was then called with the score, —Warrington Rangers, 1 goal 1 try, Mnsq Rank nil. NFArroN-LE-WILLOWS V. FARN WORTH C E .T. S. —T IR match was played at Farnworth on Saturday last, the vizitors being short of three of their forwards, hut were otherwise well represented. Newton kicked off with a stiff breeze behind them, Limond scoring for them after ten minutes' play, Rodgers adding another shortly after from long range. Bniton seored another with a swift shot. ,Just before the interval from a corner kick Hughes got his head to the ball and made the score four</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>ASSOOIA HON.</head>
        <p>ASSOOIA HON.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON ST. MARY'S V. WIDNES PARISH CHURCH. —The ahnve match was played on Saturday at Arplev, and resulted in a win for the homesters. Play in the first half was very one-sided, the whistle sounding with the score one to none in favour of the Parish. In the second half the homesters were continually in the visitors' quarters, and succeeded in scoring two goals, eillooly and Jeffers being the scorers. The home goal- keeper was only once called upon to defend his goal,and the game ended—St. Mary's, two goals; Parish Church, ene goal.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON PRINTER'S V. HALTON VlEW.—This match was placed on the ground of the latter, on Satur- day. Halton View kicked off and after a few minutes' play the centre forward scored for the Viewers. The visitors now began to waken up a little, and began a hit of passing play which resulted in Dixon shooting through from a nice pass by Rudge. Shortly after, the whistle blew for the interval, the score stand ng at one goal each. About ten minutes after the restart, the homesters' forwards came down with a.rush which ended in the centre shooting through. The time was now coming,when the forwards again came with a rush and ended the third and last goal being scored. Final result :—Halton View, 3 goals; Warrington Printers, 1 gqal. BOLT TRINITY V. BEWSEY COMBINATION.—This match was played on the ground of the latter an Saturday last. The homesters pressed hard for the first few minutes, hut to no avail. Holy Trinity then getting the ball at their feet soon put in the first goal, half time arriving with one coal to nil. Holy Trinity added another goal to their score, and after having the hest of the game cams off victors, the score being, Holy Trinity, two goals ; Bewsey Combination, nil. BARNTSN ALBION RESERVE V. RUNCORN AssociAnow. —Played at Runcorn. The homesters opened the attack end, after Bill had saved several times, he was twice beaten in rapid succession. The visitors were roused and before half-time Martin notched one. After the re-start the Albions equalind. Runcorn then added a third, and a minute before the call of time Barnton drew level, and the game ended in a draw of three goals each.</p>
        <p>- NEWTON RESERVE V. WARRINGTON L. Sz N.W. ALBION. —This match was played on the Mcanes ground, New. ten, on Saturday last, before a large number of specta- tors, great interest being centred in the fixture, as both teams have an unheaten record in the League table. Hunt kicked off for Warrington. The visitors who were at times dangernus, were playing a good game, and by means of their weight, made thines rather warm for the home defence, but Mills who was in grand form saved repeatedly, and Pendry putting his forwards in posses- sion Beresford after eluding Jones, .screwed in from the line, and scored, amid great enthusiasm. Lee centred finely. Beresford met the hall, and put it out of the reach of Richardson, thus scoring the second point for the homestem Half time was called shortly after this with Newton attacking. Dudg,enn started the second portion, and the visitors playing with the wind made a fierce onslaught on the home coal. With only five minutes to play it looked as if the home team were gninq to win, but one of the hacks fouling the hall ft free kick was awarded the visitors who scored and the game ended in a draw, each side haring scored two goals. ASSOMATTON.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT AMATEUR LEAGUE.</p>
