      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>'NUMBER OF hiEMBERS ON PARISH COUNCILS.</head>
        <p>'NUMBER OF hiEMBERS ON PARISH COUNCILS.</p>
        <p>There will b. a considerable number of parish councils around Warrington, both in Lancashire on '1 Cheshire, awl till number of councillors required is according to the plpulation. The following are the parishes which immediately adjoin Warringtog with the number of parish councillors: Burton- wood, 7; Little Sankey, 7 ; Penketh, 7; Poulton- with- Pearnh ead, 7; Rixton-with-Glazebrook 7; Great Sankey, 6; Southworth-with-Croft, 6; Win- wick-with-H ul me, 6; Woolston-with-Martinscroft, 5 ; Stretton, 6.</p>
        <p>CANDIDATES IN THE FIELD. Up to the present time no very decided steps have been taken in any of the parishes to Secure candidates, with the exception of Stretton, where the whole seven have been chosen and apparently agreed upon, and even the chairman is chosen in the person of the Rev. Canon Dodd. It is claimed in this parish that every interest is adequately represented among these seven men, and if that is so, the villagers have been fortunate in the arrange- ment of their affairs, for that could not be accom- plished very easily in a great many parishes. However, Stretton is not blessed with so many in- terests as places like Little Sankey, at which a meeting was held on Saturday last. This place has quite a number of interests. The largest by far is that of the ironworkers, a rather peculiar element in a village by the way. Then again there are the property owners, who are dreadfully afraid that the parish will be absorbed by Warrington, and there- by have its rates doubled. They appear to believe that if they can elect a parish council, the members of whom are opposed to the incorporation with the borough, this great calamity will be staved off. They are therefore anxious of making a test question of it. The farming interest is in a very finial] minority, and will not count for much in the election. Nothing definite in the way of choosing candidates will be done until to-night, when a town's meeting will be held. It is extremely probable that a poll will be required before the Council is elected. In the neighbouring parishes of Penketh and GreatSankey meetings have been held, hut no candidates have publicly announced themselves up to the present time. The same may be said with regard to Poul- ton-with-Fearnhead. which includes Padgate. There is a great deal of interest in this parish, and although only seven are required upon the Council, there is said to be a ple'hora of likely candidates, and it. seems likely that a poll will be required to settle the matter. Winwick-with-Hulrne, which also includes Orford. has not yet awoke to the sense of its responsibilities, and it is probable that the first election will pass over very smoothly. In the parishes of Rivton and Woolston, the difficulty .is to get the people to relise the importance of the Councils. A number of the electors want to know of whatever URP is this council. They, in fact, are very much in need of enlightenment. With such conditions as these, it is very improbable that any Dell will be required. Candidates are in the field in the Stockton Reath ward of the Appleton parish, and things promise to be rather warm in that dis- trict. The Vicar of Burtonwood in his parish magazine calls upon th. people to elect men who would do limiest work, and not men who wish to be elected just for the honour of the thing. This is sound advice, and the parishioners would do well to act upon There is clear evidence that the elections are evoking a vast amount of interest, in many of the villages around, and probably before the time for finals elPetion comes it will be found that polls will be demanded in the majority of parishes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>RUNCORN TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION CO 171S1ITTEE.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION CO 171S1ITTEE.</p>
        <p>At the Town flail on Monday evening, a meeting of the general Teehnieel Tnstruction Committee was held. Mr F. Wiswall, neenpied the chair and there were also present 11 ,ssrs. S. Taylor, F. J. Norman, IT. J Griee, J. W. Lighthiirn, Bishrown, Dudley, Edward Kea' and F. A. Lake (law clerk). and RP. Marshall (surveyor). The position of affairs in connection with the Institute were discussed at some length, and the best means of raising more money in order to free the building from debt were under consid- eration. It was stated that application had been made to the Local Government Board for borrowing powers for £1,250. If they succeeded in raising further sums that amount would only he required for a short period. Otherwise it would have to extend Perhaps over 50 years. This would mean the reduction of the £4OO for main- tenance to £450 per annum. It was generally felt that there was a doprecsion of trade in the town, and that it was difficult to approach tradespeople as well as working people for snbscriptinns. References were made to the prejudices against the institute in the minds of, many ratepayers as evi- denced by their statements. Some went so far as to say that it would he a "white elephant" and that it ought not to have been erected in its present situation. On the other hand it was shown that the present site was. after careful consideration, thont,ht to he the best, and in this matter the Con:mitten were supported by General Festina who had examined the- sites. It was also thought that in all probability if the school had not been at its present site they would not have received the grants from the Cheshire County Council and the Science and Art Departments They had succeeded in realising a considerable sum towards the cost and when the place was established and put in proper working order they would have £350 a rear for maintenance pur- poses. It was considered that it would be a pity to have any burden upon the institute and the memuers of the Corn mitten expressed their resolve to try and liquidate the amount owing as speedily as possible. It was how- ever decided not te take further action until after the elections.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>}TALTON.</head>
        <p>}TALTON.</p>
        <p>THFI NEW COUNCTL.—SELPCTION OP CANDIDATES.—At Halton. an independent party has been at work for some time, and several meetings have been held by them for the purpose of selecting candidates fro the district council. They have ultimately decided to suppert the following gentlemen and they intend to nominate them at the gathering to be held on December 4th: Messrs. Joseph Stokes, Thomas Wright Joseph Pritchard, William Cunningham. J. M. Baguley, Wllliam Cullock, John Baguley, George Leathwood, James Looker, Joseph Astbnry, Robert Hughes and Joseph Stubbs. All these gentlemen reside in the district, and they include both churchmen and non•conformists. With the exception of Mr. Cunningham all have con. sewed to sta-d, hut provided this gentleman should withdraw from the contest. Mr: A. Once will be substi- tuted. A meeting was held in the parish last Friday evening under the presidency of the Rev. W. Life Stone. The independents have determined to support the can- didature of Mr. Thomas Wright for the position of district parish cq)uncillor for the parieh.</p>
      </div>
