      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>BAZAAR AT RUNCORN. A GRATIFYING RESULT.</head>
        <p>THE BAZAAR AT RUNCORN. A GRATIFYING RESULT.</p>
        <p>Friday's proceedings of the bazaar in aid of the funds for the proposed new church at Weston were openedby Master Birdie Wood, seven years of age, on of Canon Maitland Wood. There was a large number of juveniles present, and the proceedings were specially suitable for them. Two other children, Miss Fanny Abel and Master Joseph Bankes, moved and seconded a vote of thanks to Master Wood for opening the bazaar, and this was carried with hearty cheers. Children's entertainments were given during the after- noon.—The opening ceremony on Saturday was per- formed by Mr. Eustace Carey, who spoke of the fur- thering of true religion, not the opinions of any particular denomination. They all had their little differences, but the points upon which they were cer- tain, were far more important than all the others put together. He had some friends who conscientiously objected both to bazaars and raffles. For his own part he liked them both, and thought them perfectly inno- cent. (Applause.) His friends would urge that raffles led to gambling. Well, in the letter they might, but in the spirit they did not. He then destribed the difference between rafflng and pmbling, pointinz out the harm- lessness of the former.—Mr.Philip Speakman, J.P.,C.u., proposed a vote of thanhs to Mr. Carey for opening the bazaar. Mr. John Ford, jun., seconded, and it was carried with acclamation. Various entertainments and concerts were given on Friday and Saturday. The members of the Mersey Mission Drum and Fife Band gave songs, recitations, ch,ruses, and selections. They were conducted by Mr. W. Shaw, jun., whilst Mr. Shaw, the missionary, presided. Tll3 artistes at another con- cert were Misses Simkin and Heatherington, Messrs. J. Booth, H. Branch, A. G. Davies, G. Wilkinson, C. Pownall, G. F. Parr, H. Newton, and Maddock. Mr. A. •G. Davies, humorist of Liverpool, gave an excellent mirth-provoking drawing-room entertainment. His collection of waxworks was greatly appreciated. On Saturday afternoon Mr. Ravenscroft's band played selec- tions, and in the evening a concert as given by Misses Comerford, Stubbs, E. D. Wright, and Gregory ; Messrs. Branch, W. E. Lancaster, A. B. Richmond, of Chester Cathedral Choir ; J. Rhodes, A. E. Davies, J. Clare, and C. Pownall. The accompaniments were played by Mr. W. H. Woodward, organist of the Rotrir Parish Church, and Mr. R. Birkett Musgrove, organist of Frodsham Parish Church. The members of the Runcorn football team also contributed a number of songs. During each</p>
        <p>evening the Weston Church Brass Band-, under the con- ductorship of Mr. Walter Tudor, paraded the streets of the town playing numerous selections.—The net takings during the four days are estimated to reach £B2O.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>RUNCORN FREE LIBRARY.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN FREE LIBRARY.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Free Library Committee held their Monthly meeting in the Board room on Tuesday evening. There were present: Mr. IV. Handley (chairman), Rev. J. Simpson, Messrs. W.Hankinson, B. Pope, H. J. Grice, T. Riley, and J. D. Jones (librarian). The librarian (Mr. Jones) submitted his monthly statement for which it appeared that the books issued for the lending department numbered 1926, and from the reference department 59, showing an increase on the corresponding month last year of 403 in the lending department and 24 in the referenee depart- ment. The books were distributed as follows : theology, 38 ; philosophy,lo ; science, 81 ;history. 148 ; poetry, 8; Action, 1,506 ; miscell Ineous, 194. The report was unanimously adopted. The following gifts were reported.. Prom Mr. John Simpson: The Ladies Cabinet, 4 'pigs; 1833-4 ; Mayor's Tourist Companion, 5 vols., 1 798. Curiosities for the Ingenious, 1831 ; Bushnell's Christian Nature ;Freeman's Disestablishment and Disendowment; Beerte's Voiceless teachers ; Johnson's Mental Sucepti- bility; Dodds Railways,Steamers and Telegraphs ; Birk's on Matter and Ether; Arnott's Survey of Human Pro- gress. From the Irish Unionist Alliance, vol 111 of pub- lications, 1893 by Mr. W. Lister. From Mr. W. Hanley ; " a seleotion of psalms, and hymns adapted to the feast, festivals and various services of the Church of England, to be used at Runcorn Church "(Runcorn, printed 1823) From Mr. John Robinson,Cyclists' Touring Club monthly Gazetteer." From the Rev. A. J. Jameson, Poulton's "Colours of Animals, their means and use." The Chair- man (Mr. Handby) said it was a source of satisfac- tion to know that they had now commenced a musical library. They had about 90 volumes which were -standard works written by leading musical writers. Mr. Hankinson and Mr. H. J. Grice also expressed their satisfaction that such a step had been taken, hoping that it would be appreciated by the public. The re- mainder of the business was of a routine nature.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>DODGES OF A. BOY WRIT-SERVER.</head>
        <p>DODGES OF A. BOY WRIT-SERVER.</p>
        <p>" Yes, sir. I've planted a few writs in my time," said be, to a representative of "Chums," with the air of a man who had accomplished many notable feats. "And though I've given it up lately because I fell into a better job, I'm rather fond of the work. Being so young-looking, of nona,,n, was a great aid_aa_the frdira You are after don't suspect anything of a mere boy ; and I daresay I've often got in where the smartest of men would have failed. Half-a-crown a time they used to give me and my railway fare, and a nice little addition it was to the wages I got as office-boy in odd hours. Well do. I remember my first job. It was to serve a man who kept a little shop in some back street over the water, and, the writ concealed in an envelope. I planted it on him quite easily. Into the back shop he Went to read what it wa.i, and a second after I heard him roar in a ferocious voice, Where is he 1" Needless to say, I took to my heels on the instant, and didn't stop running till I was half-a-mile away. Another time I was told off to effect service on a man who had defied the efforts of all the other clerks. Quite by chance I discovered in a local paper that he was advertising a bicycle for sale, and, being determined to tarn the extra half-sovereign which the firm had offered to anyone who could succeed in serving him, I availed myself of the Opportunity this knowledge offered to me. I called at the house, and, showirlg the cutting that I had taken from the paper, asked if the advertiser was in. The bait took, the gentleman himself appeared, and a second later he was in possession of the writ, and I twelve-and- sixpence the richer. Ever teen threatened with violence for my temerity in intruding upon them? Oh, yes. Once or twice an excited debtor had lost his temper on having the important piece of Paper thrust into Lis unsuspecting hands and has vowed vengeance upon me, but usually I have been able to escape. Once an irascible greengrocer shoved nae by the shoulders out of his shop, but, on my return- ing and explaining to him that what lie had done consti- tuted technically a deliberate assault, he begged my Pardon, and, anxious to avoid fwrther complications, begged my acceptance of a pound of pears, two pome- granates, and a cccco-nut ! Still with all my successes, I haven't always come off best. I went one autumn evening to South Londcn to serve a writ, and being unable to find the house named in my instructions, though I had arrived in the road in which I knew it, to be situated, I ventured to inquire of a passer-by if he knew of such a place. Wrangson Villa F—Wrangson Villa?' said he, I know the name somewhere. Oh, yes; it's over by the post-office, about a mile and a half away. There's a road of the same name as this there.' Thanking him for his assistance, thither I quickly repaired, only to meet with no better success, for, after a long tramp, no place o anything like the name could I discover. A month or so after, the debtor being pro- duced in open court, I recognised in him my artful Informant af the apocrypnal situation of the house I was in search of."</p>
