      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>LIBERALISM. AT ST. HELENS. THE HOUSE OF LORDS CONDEMNED.</head>
        <p>LIBERALISM. AT ST. HELENS. THE HOUSE OF LORDS CONDEMNED.</p>
        <p>A public meeting, under the auspice a of the St Helens Liberal Association, was held in the National Schools, Sutton, St. llelens, on Tuesday night. There was a very large attendance, and Councillor C. Walsh, who presided, was supported by Mr. T. Shepherd Little, M.P. for Whitehaven, Court. John Forster, the adopted Liberal candidate for the borough, Councillor J. Jones, Councillor J. P. Mearns, and others.—The Chairman said that measures for the good of the country had been frustrated and thwarted by the Tory party in a ruthless and shameful manner, and great responsibilities rested upon the Tory party for having thrown out useful measures of a non-politi- cal character. So long as they had the House. of Lords, with its great power and influence, thwarting those measures and casting them out, never would they have justice done to the people of the countr.y—Councillor J. P. Mearns moved the following resolution :—" That this meeting heartily approves of the administrative and legislative action of her Majesty's Government, and hopes that ihey will persist in their aetermination to make the decision of the House of Commons prevail.' He said the time had clearly arrived for the people to say that some improvement of the Upper Chamber was needed, and that it should no longer remain simply a committee room for the exercise of Tory principles. Councillor J. Forster, the adopted Liberal candidate for the borough ,who was received with cheers, seconded the resolution. He thought the resolution properly ex- pressed the political situation of the present day. They certainly approved of what the Liberal party had done during the past two years under the most trying cir- cuinstances—he almost said provoking circumstances— b, cause on every opportunity the Conservative or Unionist party had endeavoured to obstruct to interfere with, and to minimise their labours. There was no wonder, therefore, that at Trades Unionist congresses, a railway servants' congresses, and at tremendous demon- strations a hich had been held in different parts of the country, the action of the Tory party and of the House c) Lords in interfering with, in spoiling and throwing out the work accomplished in the House of Commons was unanittiously conclensned. (Applause.)—The resolu- tion was carried unanimously. Mr. T. S. Little, M.P., afterwards addressed the meeting. He said the Liberal party were entering upon some of the most important questions which had engaged their attention during the present century, and asked how any one could defend the House of Lords with ita 500 members havine as much power over the legislation this country as the whole of the 35 or 40 millions of ts ople comprising the population of the entire king- doin</p>
        <p>MEnicar. CowrnoveNcEs.—We have received from the '• Lewis RPniedies " cfrice, 23, Budge Row, Canon-street e.ays "Scivnce Siftings " an excellent little contrivance for dealing with asthma and headaches ; for the latter tra,hdy we have found it edieacious, and see no reason why it should not prove cqually useful for dealing with asthma. We have also seen another pi, co of apparatus f,r at c. Over a spirit lamp is plaCed a container tilled with Otenneals. Over this is a metal drum con- taining a. 1, he drum being pierced with holes and having reeept axle tor asthma powder at the top. When t he lamp is betted tlpi material in the container vapour- imoregnatino the wool and diffusing its fumes, which, together with the burning of the asthma powder, produces an atmospheric condition which is calculated t, benefit the avtlimatical, winle, owing to the work done by the wool, the air of a room remains more per- Ina nently el-hrged than is usually the case with such contrivances.</p>
