      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>LKINSON'S 'IAL ELIXIR</head>
        <p>LKINSON'S 'IAL ELIXIR</p>
        <p>THE MAYOR OF WIDNES ON THE BENCH. AMUSING SCENE IN THE POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>The Mayor of Widnes (Alderman S. Sadler) took his seat on the bench for the first time since his election on Tuesday at the local police court. The other magistrates on the bench were Alderman Timmis and Councillor Gossage, and before the commencement of the ordinary business of the ernirt Alderman Timmia said it was their pleasing duty to welcome Mr. Sadler to that court. He thought that all present would agree with him that it was a very for- tunate thing frt. Widnes that they had Mr. Sadler there to take part in the work of that court. They knew that thtty had the right man in the right place.—Mr. C. EL Lewin (deputy magistrates' clerk) and Superintendent Keighley both assured his Worship that he would have the support of the officials in the discharge of his duties. —The Mayor, in reply, thanked the gentlemen who had spoken and said he trusted that he would do nothing which would disturb the unanimity of the business of the court. The first prisoner brought up was Rose Kelly, who was charged with creat:ng a breach of the peace on Saturday night. Superintendent Keighley said the prisoner had been before the court 114 times previously, and had only been out of gaol a few days after serving a month's imprisonment.—The Prisoner : I knew Mr. Sadler was coming here this morning, and I thought I would come here and welcome him. I did this on pur- pose. (Loud laughter.)—The Mayor : As this is the first case which has come before me, we will discharge you this time with the hope that you will not come here again.—The Prisoner (as she was leaving the court) : God bless you, Mr. Sadler. Long may you reign. (Loud laughter, in which his worship joined.)</p>
      </div>
