        <p>tttters to the &amp;Ker. IN APPEAL FOR HELP. Sir.—Will you kindly allow me, through your paper,to appeal to the charitably disposed for help for our releif fund. I shall be glad ta receive parcels of cast-off clothing which are specially useful at this season of the year. There is at present a great deal of distress in the town, and I am being appealed to almost daily for assistance. The fullest enquiry is made in all cases before relief is given. Any help in money or clothing will be gratefully acknowledged by yours faithfully. H. TAYLOR, Police Court Missionary.</p>
        <p>188 Longford-St., Warrington. THE LENTIL AS AN ARTICLE OF FOOD. Sir,-1 should like to bring before the notice of your readers the advantage and desirability of a more preva- lent use of the lentil as a form of food. Its claims upon us are many and the reasons for its taking the place of other more common foods are conclusive. I find on .computation, that a meal possessing a larger proportion of flesh-forming and health-giving constituent elements than almost any other form of diet, can be prepared ata cost less than one penny for each individual. For this ridiculously small sum, a workingman can obtain a diet of a most pleasing palatable nature, and more sustaining for performing a day's work than he can obtain from those foods he too often indulges in, and, in this age when economy is a supreme factor in life, here, surely lies the very acme of cheapness. If the value of the lentil were more generally known, I, as one who pos- sesses great faith in its rich qualities, am certain it would soon occupy a place as the staple article of food in this country.—l am, etc., A. W.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN DISTRICT SCHOOL CONCERT. Sir,—l have had many congratulations on the success of the concert given by the children in the Town Hall on Thursday November 15th, and, with your permission I should like it to be publicly known how grateful I am to all who in any way contributed to bring about this desirable result. First of all, to the staff of teachers in my own department of the school, unqualified praise is due for the unstinted labour they have given in the pre- paration of the entertainment ; to Mr. Stringer (who has worked with a loyalty and devotion deserving of the highest praise ever since he came to the school) and Miss Nash for their admirable training of the boys and girls for the drills and dancing and their help in the teaching of the acting and recitation in "Fairy Fay " ; to Miss Molyneux and Miss Adams for their able assistance in the preparation of the choruses in the operetta and the part songs—an arduous task; to Mrs. Lane—always ready to give a helping hand—for the pains she bestowed on the dress of the 'king' and 'hops' and many other details —ably helped by the lady teachers named above; to Miss Howarth who spent 80 much time in the get up' of the 'fairies' who did her such credit on the concert night— and to one and all for the many other little odds and ends which help to ensure suceess in an undertaking of this nature. I would also tender my acknowledge- ment to Mr. R. Smith—a friend from Warring- ton—who very materially brightened up the 'act- ing' part in Fairy Fay.' In addition to the Staff of my own department I must express my grititude to Mrs. Brown for her valuable help and Inggestions ; and to the other teachers of the infant department—especially, if I may say so, to Miss Rodgers, Miss Tripp and Miss Attfield, for their cheerful and willing assistance; so the parents, myself and teachers are deeply indebted for the evident pains bestowed on the dress of their children—to mothers of children in the chorus equally with those who took a more prominent part. To those gentlemen who placed at our disposal the timber and various appliances used in the erection of the gallery, etc., and to all who assisted in the arrangements of the crowded hall on the concert night, and in the sale of tickets before that time, our thanks are also due. Last, but not least, all teachers wish to say how highly they were pleased with the way all the boys and girls acquitted and behaved themselves in this, their first performance. Mere was only one drawback the whole of the evening, and that was the regret of the teachers that Dr. Flandyside (through a recent sad bereavement) and—if I may be allowed to say so—Mrs. Handyside were not able to take an active part in the proceedings. The teachers more than any outsiders know how mach they themselves and the school owe to the able management, th,. courteous treat- ment and kindly interest that Dr. Flandyside ever takes mall matters relating to the welfare of the school.— Yours truly, ALEX BROWN, Head Master. ""</p>
        <p>_ P.B.—After defraying all expenses the teachers will be able to send to the Treasurer the sum of £lB 7s. 6d., on Thursday night's entertainment. The proceeds of Friday's performance will be used to proyid‘z a treat for the children who took part in the concert.</p>
        <p>THE INGRATITUDE OF THE SEMINAL CONTROVERSALISTS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l must confess to a feeling of concern at the silence of the champions who of late marshalled them- selves in battle array in defence of the doctrine of the death of the dead parts of a see 3. Where, now, be the Goliath's and Gamaliel's of this immortal suisject ? Can It be that the one small pebble of" fact " from the sling of experiment has smitten the" mighty ones" of ortho- doxy to judicious silence ? But alas for their Chris- tianity ! Here was an excellent opportunity for the display of one of the Christian virtues—himility—and with it the gentlemanly virtues of gratitude and apology for injuries inflicted upmn another. Does Mr. Ben Chavil think it either Christian or gentlemanly to allow such epithets as "caviller," " sciolist," " idle jargon," " word mongering,"" presumptuous feol," &amp;c., etc., Which he flung at ene who merely wished to lay the bare truth before him—does he think it righil to allow these choice fits of polemical rhetoric to remain unre- called ? It may seem strange but it is nevertheless true that I have not received the thanks of a single Christian for the positive demonstration I have given of one of their doctrinal errors! And this from people who pro- fess to be so anxious for the truth ! The attitude of the professing religionist in relation to the scientific worker is both amusing and depressing—amusine to see the purblind imbecility with which the religionist will admire, and himself trot out, the facts brought to light by the scientist so long as he is unconscious of their dogmatically destructive effect, and the howl of pious e inn which N the calm sublimity of his soul when he does realise the fact; and the haste he makes to break the phials of religious invective upon the devoted head of the man who demonstrates this to him—while it is depressing to see how little sway has reason when fanaticism "holds the fort." Science may dig for her facts, m.le-like urvier ground.</p>
