        <p>FROM THE MOMENT OF BIRTH USE CUTICURA SOAP.—It is not only the purest, sweetest, and most refreshing of nursery soaps, hut it contains delicate emollient properties, which purify and beautify the skin, and remove skin blemishes, occasioned by imperfect cleansing at birth and the continued use of impure soaps. Guaranteed absolutely pure bY analytical chemi4s of the highest standing, whose certificates of arla!FflE accom- each tablet.</p>
        <p>The match at Melbourne 11Pt.wPen Mr. Stoddart's eleven and the Victorians was concluded on Wednesday. The Englishmen won by 165 runs. The agitation amongst shipowners as to the transfer of vessels to other figs continues. Last month tlare were eleven steamees and ten Failing, VP=SPig removed from the British to foreign registers. It is significant that five of the steamers went to japan.</p>
        <p>- •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <p>or store said facts in some learned lumber room, and receive pious applause and patronage; but once let her show the imcompatibility of these facts with some mouldy old myth or dilapidated dogma, and some ultra- sanctimonious flap-doodle-finding " rant" come easier to him than patient investigation—lifts up his hands and eyes in holy horror and presumes to denounce demons- trable facts as " Godless science!" It is curious to note how prone men are to forget that all truth must be God's truth; and the reluctance they show in discarding the man-made formularies of a faith eviscerated by these drastic facts.</p>
        <p>There is one consideration I would like to mention. Is it incumbent upon us as a nation or individually to carry to foreign countries and peoples statements which we cannot reasonably accept ourselves? The further dying of already dead things is an essential dogma of Christianity. As such it is part of the Common Law of England. And millions of good money are annually spent in its inculcation here, and thousands in its propogation abroad. Would it not be better, ere carrying our "cranks" abroad, t get them straightened at home? The money so wasted might be much more helpfully— and I think religiou-lv—employed here. It is not a pleasant reflection for thQse who contribute to foreign missions to know that it ha 3 required £11,804 93. 6d. to get one poor imbecile Moslem girl to call herself a Christian. But this we have on the authority of Canon ITaylor at the Church Congress. The men who ask for, and get, the hard earned pennies of the poor—in pocket or in intellect—are not, in my view, at all respectable. lhat any presumably educated man can be found to preach such an untruth at such a cost, is an evidence of what man can be brought to, by beginning with him young and paying him well when old. In the balance of vested interests a little gold outweight all doctrinal difficulties.—Truly yours, JOHN PEERS. November 18th, 1894.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>FOREIGN COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>FOREIGN COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Please permit me to ask a little explanation on the above from Mr. F. Monks. 1. With regard to what the Belgian workmen are doing, we have been told it takes in the iron trade in Belgium at least three men to do the same amount of work as two Englishmen. 2. That in the local trade the Belgian collier produces annually 187 to 172 tons as compared with from 302 to 1307 tons by colliers in the United Kingdom. Cost of coal at pit bank Is in Belgium about 2s. per ton higher than in the United Kingdom. _ . . .</p>
        <p>3. That iron worked in Belgium is very common and inferior—something like white iron in South Wales. Now, will Mr. Monks give us a full statement showing the actual cost of labour, not the rate to individuals but the amount of work given in return for amount of wages paid, and what is the relative rate of wages paid in South Wales for working similar iron ? With regard to America, does Mr. Monks expect that with all her stores of coal and iron and the energy of our best men who have gone there we can expect to hold their markets ? One thing is certain, they pay about double our rate of wages. One word as to royalties. The average cost per ton of pig produced in the whole of the United Kingdom for royalty is 4s. 6d., and not 6d. in Germany, and, as pig is used for making bar iron, how does Mr. Monks recon- cile his statement that royalty does not effect this branch of industry ?—Yours respectfully, T. MALKIN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>ELLESMERE-ST,, WESLEYAN MISSION.</p>
        <p>The annual foreign missionary sermons were held a Ellesmere-at. Mission on Sunday morning and evening, the preacher being Mr. Roylance, of Moore. There were good attendances at each service, and collections were taken in aid of the missions. The annual meeting was held on Tuesday evening when the attendance was not large. The chair was occupied by Mr. W. C. Nicholsen. The Rev. Owen Watkins in giving the annual report, said that foreign mission were formed before the Missionary Society. It was not the Society which created the missions but the missions which demanded the forma- tion of the Society. The first collection ever taken for foreign missions was taken at the Methodist conference, and although the ministers were hut poor, the sum of £BO was realised. The pioneer of foreign missions was Dr. Coke, who died at sea on his way to Ceylon. Six young men who accompanied him, proceeded to Ceylon where a strong mission was formed. The income of the Society during the past year was £122,211 183.6d.which he regretted to say was £5,000 less than the previous year. The expenditure was £128.517 16s 11d. The total debt on the Society was £28,000, which was one of the saddest things be knew. He appealed to the people to do their utmost to lessen this debt. The War- rington circuit, in spite of the coal strike did extremely well last year,the sum of £272 93.5 d. being raised. The meeting at Ellesmere-st. mission last year was the fists ever held,in the mission and the sum raised was £1 35.9 d. —chairman in addressing the meeting said that he accounted it an honour to be upon the missionary platform. He had two brothers who had worked in the mission field in India, so that he knew something of missionary work. Whenever he attended a missionary meeting he always felt warmed, and he was as enthusiastic about the work as ever he was. He hoped that they at Ellesmere-st. would stand on a firm basis, and they would do their best for the mission cause.—The Rev. Owen Watkins said that next to preaching the gospel, his greatest privilege was to go the length and breadth of England proclaiming the work which was going on in foreign lands. Me. Watkina went on to give an account of his work in the Transvaal. Describing the Boers, he said that they were as white as Englishmen. They were descendents of the Hugienots, and were very devout, but narrow-minded. There was no mission to them as they were members of tha Dutch Reformed Church. The most ignorant of the Baers lived in the Transvaal, and formerly believed that the black men had no souls. The mission to the black people in the Transvaal, commenced 13 years ago, and last year they had 7,000 people meeting in class, and 3,000 people attending the chapels. A great nui lber of the workers were black men. It had always been his desire to get to Central Africa, hut he did not expect now to do so as he was afraid the doctors would allow him, but he was glad to say that his son was going in about a year to South Africa. He hop:id that he would be able to cross the Zambesi and get to the district of the great lakes. Mr. Watkins then proceeded to give a most interesting account of his work among the Mashona people.—During the meeting a collection was taken in aid of the missionary c-iuse.and the proceedings were brought to a conclusion with the usual votes of thanks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>CAIRO-STREET _CHAPEL,</head>
        <p>CAIRO-STREET _CHAPEL,</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. H. Drummond delivered the second of a course of sermons on new statements ot old doctrines to a large conaregation on Sunday evening. The subject was " Belief in cied." He said that he did not wish to suggest that any of his hearers should have a difficulty in believing in God, the source of life and the Father of their spirits. For many of them doubtless it was an in- separable part of their most sacred experience. But then they were face to face with this fact, that there was a growing number of men eager to know how the central faiths of religion were effected by Scienee and our deeper knowledge of human history. The material- ist and the agnostic were ready with their reply. Simply to take refuge in an ancestral belief, saying the argu- ments which satisfied their fathers were gaod enough for them, was a policy of despair, and could not produce conviction. "Mv object," said the preacher, "is to tell you as plainly and simply as lean, why in the broad light of day, when science has pushed its way to the furthest confines of knowledge, and criticism has spoken its last word on the Bible, I find it reasonable to express my faith in these words. God is, God reigns, God loves." Turning to the witness of the external world, he pointed out that the difficulties which science has been supposed to nut in the way of Atheistic belief arose to a large extent from the small and often false arau- ments men had been aceustomed to use for God's exis- tence. An infinite God required an infinite universe. The universe was reasonable; it bore witness to thought as well as power. Such was the witness of science to God. There was one reasonable power ordering and controlling all things. Another and higher witness came from the facts of the moral life of man. They could only account for these facts as they existed in individual experience and human history by the belief that there was a law. of righteousness to which conduct must con- form. Another point was thus reached. The Power whom they called God was a righteous power. An- other. and the most serious cinestion of all for religion remained. "Can God love man ?" That was a great difficulty. How could the God of the infinite nnicerse love a norm feeble human life? Some men annarently thought that God did not love them unless He erders , events to suit their convenience and makes life happy and prosperous. That was not reasonable, and it was not true. But if by love was meant that the Divine. . power was full of tenderness and forgiving,ness for human life, and that the mere sense of His presence might be strength and peace, then to believe that was n.rfectly reasonable. It was based upon facts of exper- , ience which could not be denied or explained away, so that cenviction was reached that the power who lived through the infinite fields of space. who reigned over human lives as righteousness. dwells as eternal love in the hearts of men. Here then in the facts of the nni- verse and of individual experience was a reasonable foundation for faith in God, a ftith which would only broaden and deepened their knowledge, grow from more to more, and disclose ever new treasures of comfort and peace as, forsaking the broken customs of false and imaginary theological knowledge, men return to that fountain of living waters. God was, God reianed, God loved. That was the great affirmation alike of the intel- lect and the heart.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 24, 1894. NOTES ON GAMES AND INEW BOOKS, MAGAZINES, ETC.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 24, 1894. NOTES ON GAMES AND INEW BOOKS, MAGAZINES, ETC.</p>
        <p>Now that Christmas is approaching, a noticeable increase of publications in the book-market is apparent. New editions, annuals, and books of all descriptions herald the approach of the gift-season, for which original productions are appearing rapid- ly in every shape and form. Judging by the quality of the new comers,this season bids fair to excel its predecessors in gratifying the taste of the book- world. From present appearances " output exceed- ing the demand" is not likely to be the publishers' stock-phrase for the season of 1894-5. The style of Ithe new issues does not seem to have altered much i though perhaps there are a few less novels and more works dealing with the arts and classics. Most of the predominating characteristics of the previous year's editions are the same, but still there is a slight and appreciable difference. The follow- ing are some of the publications which have come under our notice during the past few weeks. MACMILLAN AND CO.—" A Modern Buccaneer "is the title of a story of absorbing interest by Rolf Boldrewood. The hero—or part hero—is one Elilary Telfer, a native of Sydney, Australia, who after being comrade to the " Buccaneer "in many startling incidents, becomes a peaceful trader, and husband to a beautiful Norfolk Islander. The" Modern Buccaneer" is William Henry Elacston, a tall and immensely strong man—but not a ruffian. As the author says of him he was "a man twho, no matter what his faults may have been,possessed qualities which, had they been devoted to higher aims in life, might have rendered him the hero of a nation." In app( arance,the book is handseme,and well and openly printed. (Price 3s.6J.)—Another excellent novel from the pen of Mr. F. Marion Crawford is found in" Katherine Lauderdale," in which the author excels himself in :I o fineness and delicacy of its producti,re The pathos is admirable and the humour spontaneous, while the char- acters are everything that could be desired in their naturalness and originality. A depth of feeling is appar- ent, while the story runs on with the well-regulated rythm of an experienced and popular writer. The heroine is a handsome girl with a strong individuality, while the other main character in the book does net at first impress the reader very favourably, but improves with acquaintance. The binding is neat and tasteful, while the page letter- pre.s is fine and does justice to the book. (Price 81.). A. new series of novel and tastefully bound booklets iscom- menced by the same author in a tale entitled" Love in Idleness,' which though ssnall is full of incident and life. It is a description cleverly-told, of the manner in which a couple fall in love during the idle hours of a summer holiday. The tale is conversational and abound- ing in smart repartee while the "grand finale"is in every way satisfactory to the hero and heroine, as well as the reader.—The second of the same series is found in "The Story of Sonny Sahib " by Sara Jeannette Duncan (Mrs. Everard Cotes), an interesting little story of a young English boy's life with ail Indian Maharajah,subsequent to the great mutiny. One of his parents is dead and the other at the time of his appropriation by this poten- tate is in hospital several hundred miles away. After many strange incidents "Sonny Sahib" at last finds his remaining parent. Throughout this little book the in- terest never flags, and the style is light and fresh (price 2s.).—Flora Annie Steel, who so much resembles Rud- yard Kipling in her style and knowledge of Indian life, comes to the fore again with another of the beautifully bound: Cranford series called "Tales of the Punjab," illustrated by J. Lockwood Kipling. The tales have the conventional Eastern flavour, and it is intended that the text should interest children, and at the sama time the copious notes at the end of the book should render it valuable to those who study Folklore on its scientific side. The spice of strangeness and originality with which these stories are flavoured gives them an interest unattached to any other production ef the folklore order. "The Fables of .sop " selected,and their history traced, by Joseph Jacobs, is another of the same series. It is very cleverly illustrated by Richard Heighway. The author has adopted from the various versions of these in fables those which seemed mast suitable in each case, and told them anew in a way calculated to interest child- ren. Notes summing up what is known as the provenance of each fable are also included in the contents of the issue. The un,qualled letterpress and cleverly exe cuted illustrations which are characteristics of this edition cannot be overlooke4 by all professed judges of what tends to make an excellent book, (Price 6s. each.) CASSELL AND CO.—From these publishers we have an enthralling novel entitled "Out of the Jaws of Death," a production from the pen of Frank Barrett. In this volume, startling incidents abound while the author's undoubted capacity for portraying the tragic is called to his aid in more instances than one. A strange, impul- sive, slum waif is the heroine of the tale. She is lifted from the dregs of poverty to the position of a lady by the kindness of Taras the hero. The latter is a Russian prince with a taste for sculpture with which he shows in vivid forms the faults and crimes which the Government of that country favours, and thus makes himself liable 1 to be sent to Siberia. Undoubtedly this book is one which will command the attention of all lovers of a 1 stirring romance; its strange and unlooked for develop- ments possessing a fascinating charm to the reader.— "Yule Tide" (Cassell's Christmas Annual) bids fair to excel even last year's issue which was so deservedly popular. It contains an original novel by J. McClaren Cohban, entitled "The Avenger of Blood," a stirring tale in which love and adventure, intri- ague and diplomacy play a prominent part. Original illustrations illustrate the story throughout. In addition there is also a magnificent coloured plate. by W. E. F. Britten, the subject of which is " Hide and Seek " ; two coloured plates entitled "Companions of the Bath" and "A Deputation," are beautifully reproduced from original paintings by Robert Morley, R.8.A.; four pictures painted in three tints. Two of these are repro- duced from original drawings by Prescott Davies, and are entitled respectively " Sweet Seventeen "—a hundred years ago, and "Sweet Seventeen "—to-day. "May I Have the Pleasure 2" and "The Wassailers "are from the facile brush of W. Rainey, R. 1., and reproduced in three tints. Price 14,—a 2s. novel "The Still House of O'Darrow," by Irving Batcheller, is the next that we would wish to call attention to. The tale, as its name suggests, is one of mystery, tho solution of which keeps the reader busily and interestedly engaged up to the last chapter. Incid- ents in social life, well portrayed, are woven into the story and throw a pleasing light on the otherwise dark nature of the narrative. Though astonishing, the solu- tion is well-worked out and harmonises with the depic- tion of the novel's chief character. F. V. WHITE AND CO.—We have received from this firm the usual Christmas novel by John Strange Winter. The title is "The Stranger Woman" and the novel is written in the author's best style. It is striking in its character- istics and has, of course, the usual complement of amusing contretemps which oistinguish John Strange Winter's books, together with the more serious side of life. Really, a desirable shillingsworth. _ _ .</p>
        <p>DIPROSE. BATEMAN AND CO.—These publishers have issued a novel by the well-known novelist. J. Fogerty the title ot which is " A Game of Chess, or when Jew meets Greek." The tale abounds in sensational inci- dents of the most stirring character. A plot of absorb- ing interest runs through the book, in the weaving of which the author displays s pleasing deftness. Humour also has its due position in the novel. (Price is.) " Dip- prose's Annual "comes to the front again with its varied productions of grave and gay. Arnong the authors are Leopold Wagner, A. F. Robertson, Jean Middlemass and others. The number of tales is 13, all of a superior char- acter and meriting careful perusal. Several are illu- strated, thus adding to the general attractiveness of the book. The published price is one shilling. "HOLLY LEAVES."—The popular. Christmas number of the " Illustrated Sporting and Dramatic News" will be published on November 26th, and special attention is called to the coloured supplement which will be given. This year's fixture is a reproduction by photographic I transfer, of J. W. Godwards "Reflections," and fermi 'a companion to last year's remarkably successful "Memories," by the same artist. Seventeen full-page illustrations are given, together with numerons minor sketches. The literary contents are as usual of a sport- ing and dramatic turn, but written in a tasteful and • rst-class style. The price is the same—is. THE YOUNG LADIES' JOURNAL.—The Christmas num- ber of this magazine centains two parts and seven supplements. These parts comprise a large supply of serial and complete stories. " Her Master," by the popular writer, Mrs. Lovett Cameron, is begun in the D,cember, and runs through the two parts. "The Gray Wolf's Daughter," by Gertrude Warden, and "Mildred of Firlarnis," by the author of " Milly Lee," are con- tinued, and " Madge," by a very favourite writer, is begun in the Christmas part. There are besides a num- ber of complete stories,some charming verses,Christmas amusements, table decorations, and excellent cookery recipes, many of them for Christmas cookery. The fashion illustrations are numerous. The seven supple- ments consist of a picture entitled" Love's Eloquence," a number of coloured designs for painting on articles of wood-work, a large coloured plate of Paris evening and fancy ball dresses for ladies and children,a music supple- ment consisting of a song and a waltz by Mr. Val Marriott; a bright little drawing-room play; an ex,el- - supplement, illustrated, of Christmas stories, and a supplement of charming dresses for ladies and children. The two parts and seven supplements cost. is. 6d. _</p>
        <p>WELDON'S JOURNAL.—TIie. Christmas double number of Weldon' Ladies' Journal (6d.) is indeed a budget .f fashion fancies. Two ehromo plates are given away entitled "A Willing Team" and "Twixt Wealth and Poverty." reproduced in the best form of chromolitho- graphy from original oil paintings by C. P. Garland and W. H. Trood. which will be much appreciated, and a large coloured plate of the newest creations in toilettes for home and evening wear is also given away. The number contains complete instructions for cutting out and making the newest creations for the present season and a host of information dear to the hearts of our fair readers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>The Warrington football public were treated to a piece of good fortune on Saturday, which they do not very often have. They had for their visitors, the famous Halifax team, who for the past two years have carried off the Yorkshire Cup, and who stand high in the York- shire League. For this piece of good fortune, they have to thank Wigan, who by doing what they ought not to have done, are not allowed to do anything at all except vegetate in idleness, until February Ist. Wigan were " down " to meet Halifax on Saturday, but as the inex- orable County Committee refused to let them meet any- body, Halifax were left without a fixture. Mr. Warren, the energetic Warrington secretary, immediately made arrangements whereby the Tranmere Wanderers who were due at Warrington, were dropped, and Halifax were taken on instead.</p>
        <p>It is not often that Yorkshire clubs have visited the Wilderspool enclosure of late, the last team being Brig- house Rangers. Halifax have been there before, but it is a number of years ago,when they were rather severely beaten. They attempted to explain this defeat away at the time by saying that they had only sent an " A " team. Consequently on Saturday. although there were only two days' notice of the match, there was a good gate of same 5,000 present, at the match.</p>
        <p>Those who were present at the match, were well re- paid for their pains, for the match was the most enjoy- able one yet seen on the enclosure this season. The game was pleasantly contested, without any rough play, and of a very opm character. Warrington in fact played as open a game as Halifax. Consequently there was not the same monotonous series of scrimmages which help to speil the Rugby game. The result of the game came quite as a surprise to. everybody. No one expected War- rington to win ta the extent of fourteen points to five. This result was not the result of any bad play on the part of the Tykes, but it was the downright good play on the part of the home team which gave them the vic- tory. I don't think they have ever played better this season. If they will continue on the same lines, they will soon increase the " gates,"</p>
        <p>- Each team were short of regular players. Halifax had four men helping Yorkshire to beat Northumberland, and from the Warrington ranks Jolley was also missing, and helping Lancashire to draw with Cumberland. Hokin had also been sent for to Manchester but did not play. Thorniley was another absentee from the War- rington ranks. Thus while Warrington were chiefly weakened forward, Halifax were chiefly weakened among the backs. The match was a most exciting one, and great was the shout when Warrington scored within three minutes from the start. This lead was not, how- ever, kept up long, for Halifax replied by obtaining a goal from a try, thus gaining two points. Warrington were leading again by half time, for they also obtained another goal. This they further augmented in the latter half by two more tries, and thus run out easy winners.</p>
        <p>With the exception of the Mona match, Warrington have not scored so often in one match before this season. To score four tries in one afternoon is indeed a feat for Warrington, who appeared to have lost the art of scor- ing. However, let us hope that this is the turning point, and that in future they will be able to utilise their chances better. The scorers were Buckley. Nevins, Bailey and Burton, and the goal-kicker, Heesoin, and the goal triers, Taylor,Turner,and Nevins. lam glad to notice that Barton was able to score. This player has had thc vilest of luck, and has repeatedly been spoiled when almost on the point of scoring. His bad luck at Lancaster is still fresh to one's memory.</p>
        <p>The Halifax players are a sturdy lot, but they never appeared to find their feet on Saturday. Whenever they appeared dangerous, they were always so unfortunate to find Boscow in the way, and with only one exception, they always feund him an insurmountable barrier. He played his best on Saturday. The three-quarters were also in good form, and it looks as though that division will soon be all right. Heesom and Burton made capital centres, and the wines, Cunningham and Moores, were very speedy. Moores' debut as a first team player at Warrington. was quite a rovelation. He is rather slim, but he has any amount of pluck and speed, and there is no doubt but that Warringtan have found a really first- class player In him. The half backs played well on Sat- urday. Bate excelled in tricky play. while Clark appeared to have developed a mania for playing the association game, and several of his dribbles evoked great enthusiasm from the crowd. The Warrington forwards were the superiors of the other side, and almost every time carried the scrimmages. The division in which Halifax showed up hest was the three-quarter. The short passing which Keenings and Co. repeatedly indulged in.completely baffled Warrington at first. The movements of the men were extremely pretty to watch, and they played a very attractive game.</p>
        <p>The Warrinetlniana to-day 'journey north, to meet the Kendal Hornets in Westmoreland. Victory has almoat invariably followed them when engaged with the Northern club, and therefore, I look to see the same result again. To keep up the long line of victories the following team has been selected to do duty for War- rington: Boscow, full back; Cunningham, Burton, flee4om, and Moores, threequarters Bate and Clark. halt backs; Turner, Thornilev, Nevins, Andrews, Taylor, Berry, Bailey, and Elampson.</p>
        <p>I notice that Jolley has again been chosen to fight for Lancashire against her great rival Yorkshire to-day. He appears to be a regular fixture in the Lancashire team. lam also glad to see that Dakin has at length been definitely chosen for the same match, and lam sure that he will do credit to himself. He has been very near being chosen several times, and it was rather rough on him OR Saturday last at the match against Cumberland, when he was wired for, and was just stripping for the match, that Sydney Wilson came riding up in a cab to take his place in the team, and thus Dakin had to dress again, and become a spectator instead of a player. He is this time, however, on the team, and not on the re- serve, so that his turn has come at last.</p>
        <p>By the way, Lancashire's play against Cumberland does not augur very well for the Yorkshire match. After the big scores made against Cheshire and Westmoreland. the Cumberland match was thought to be almost as- sured, when to the astonishment of everybody, Lanca- shire were very lucky even to draw. The Lancashire players. however. always a npear to rise to the occasion when they meet the rival "Rose," and it is not out of the range of poszibility that the "cocksureness" of the Tykes may receive a severe shock.</p>
        <p>The Warrington St. Mary's team continued their vic- torious career on Saturday and severely heat the St. Helens "A " team at St. Helens. I think that by this time they have a rigtht to call themselves the champion junior team of the district. Everybody who comes in their way, be it at home or away. receives the same treatment'. and it would he difficult to find the team which will be the first to put the brake on. One evening paper on Saturday, was so impressed by their cloinls that it had the result of the match with St. Helens "A " four times in the same edition. They ought to be be mightily flattered by this great attention which is shown them.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath are the most dangerous rivals which St. Mary's have to fear.a.nd the latter are as yet the only team which have beaten the former. It should be a match worth seeing when the return is played at Stock- ton Heath. Stockton Heath hat a St. Mary's team on Saturday. but it was the Widnes, not the Warrington Sr. Mary's. They had to fi,sht hard to win the match however, for the chemieals were a sturdy lot. Runcorn appears to c'tnsider Stoeleon Heath a suburb of it.elf. and are therefore trying what the Stockton Heath players are made of. They tried Barber againt Swinton, and on Stturday they took on CroFs. I hear that both players did exceedingly v;Tell, and no doubt the Runcorn team will be greatly strengthened with them.</p>
        <p>FARRIERS.</p>
        <p>The Orford Harriers had an inter-club race on Satur- day last with Northwich Harriers at Warrington Orford Harriers Headquarters. Me. S. Hartshorne's the Railway Tavern, near Bank Quay Bridge. The Northwich Harriers credited their club with 23 runners and War- ringtnn Orford Harriers had 13 runners. Two from each club were sent to lav a trail. and they covered about 8 miles of nice country and a splendid run was enjoyed. The two clubs set to work about 4 o'clock. A very fast pace was made by H. Grounds, of W.0.H., and Eiwa.rds, of N.H., ancl. the route was through Bur- tonwood and round the fields towards the Butchers' Arms and round Sankey Chapel and straight along Liver- pool-road home again. The little wonder of the War- rington W.O.H. came in alone about 600 yards in front of C. Booth, N.H., closely followed by J. W. Mather. and the run ended in an easy thing for the W.O.H. who won by 27 Doints. The Warrington runners finished as follows Grounds, 1, J. W. Mather, 3, J. Price, 4, F. Woods, 5. S. Bradley, 6. J. Ninety.liffe. 7 ; 26 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <p>linthon's is the Soap to buy. As everybody knows—. It quickly drives the dirt away, But won't wear out the c ones !—Adv.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>P.S.A. AT THE Y.M.CA.</p>
        <p>There was a good meeting at the Y.M.C.A. rooms on Sunday afternoon, the room being well filled as usual. Mr. J. S. Green presided, and the address was delivered by Mr. Stewart (secretary). who based his remarks on "Courage and Patience." Miss Cosgrove contributed two solos:entitled, " The Lost Chord," and," The Precious Name,"respectively. Mr. F. Hickman was the accompanist. THE CHEMICAL TRADE IN RUNCORN AND DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>Notices have been posted in the works of the United Alkali Company at Runcorn,Weston,Widnes, and other places, stating that after the 28th work in the bleaching and caustic departments will cease until further notice. This is in consequence of the very large stocks the Company have at pre- sent on hand, and until they have been consider- ably reduced there will be very little prospect of a resumption of work. The stoppage affects a con- siderable number of men in the places named.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>STARR-BOWICETT APPROPRIATION.</head>
        <p>STARR-BOWICETT APPROPRIATION.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening an appropriation meeting connection with this society was held at the s:,c- retaries' office, Cairo-st. Mr. R. Oddie, of Lymm, presided, and Messrs. T. Perrins and It Whittle were appointed scrutineers. Master Gilbert Sim- cock drew the numbers, and Nn.242 was announced as the result. 'ibis entitled Mr. Samuel Williams, of Padgate-lane, to £2OO for 12,i years free of in- terest. A vote of thanks to the Chairman and scrutineers closed the proceedings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>OT D- ST. W ESLEY A N PEOPLE'S SERVICE.</head>
        <p>OT D- ST. W ESLEY A N PEOPLE'S SERVICE.</p>
        <p>There was a large eJugregation present at Bold- st. Wesleyan Chapel at the weekly people's service on Sunday afternoon. The programme of music was an exceptionally attractive one, including selections from Mozart's Twelfth Mass, an andante from Schubert, and a march. These were rendered In Capital style by the band, as was also "The Holy City," by Miss Mary Bennett, who is a rising soprano. The congregation was accompanied in the singing of the hymns by the orchestra, amongst whom are some talented instrumentalists.</p>
        <p>FAIRFIELD SCHOOL.-GIFT FROM Miss WILSON P.A.TTE N.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening, an entertainment was held in Fairfield schools to raise funds for the liquida- tion of the debt upon the schools. A very success- ful and varied programme was gone through, the performers being the infants attending the school. Considering that all the children performing were under nine years of age, the result obtained reflects the greatest credit on the teachers who had the task of training them. The Rector, who presided, briefly addressed the audience and said the debt on the schools amounted to .£1,500. Towards this amount he had had a grant of £2OO from the National Society, and that morning he had received a letter from Miss Wilson-Patten in which she promised .£2OO, and if this could be met by Christ- mas with another .£2OO, she would then give it them for a Christmas gift.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>WRITE STAR MINSTRELS ENTERTAINMENT.</head>
        <p>WRITE STAR MINSTRELS ENTERTAINMENT.</p>
        <p>On Monday ovening an entertainment was given by the White Star Minstrels, in St. Paul's Infants' School room when there was a large audience. ' Messrs. A. G. Wallington and W. Harris acted as stage managers, and Mr.H.Atkinson as interlocutor. Mr. T. Glover ren.,.ered the song "Storm Fiend," in an able manner, and the comic song "Mary's gone wid a coon„' by Mr. J. Emson, caused roars of laughter. Mr. S. Hassell was well received for his rendering of "My Boyhood's Love," and Mr. G. Newton sang" Norine Maureen," in an able manner. Mr. G. Coggins amused the audience with the comic song. " Climb up, chillun," and the first part of the programme was brought to a close by Mr. J. W. Dockrey singing "Mary of Argyle." The second part was commenced by Mr. J. Emson, with the comic song, ' Irishman in France," and Mr. W. Brobbin Rave a concertina solo. Mr. F. Scattergood sang in faultless style, "Our hands have met." Mr. Benjamin (cartoonist). portrayed several likenesses on the blackboard, for which he was cheered, and Mr. Pownall made a good impression by the singing of "The Village Blacksmith." The entertainment was brought to a close with the comic sketch, (specially composed for these minstrels by Mr. G. Coggins) entitled, "A Birthday Party." The fol- lowing took the characters :—Messrs. G. Cogginso J. Emson, G. Newton, S. Newall, 11. Jones, and S' Yeardley. Miss Bentley acted as accompanist. CHESS MATCH.—WARRINGTON V. PEOPLE'S INSTITUTE. MANCHESTER.</p>
        <p>This, the first league match in which the local club has been engaged this season, took place at the Y.M.C.A. on Saturday evening. As will be oen from the score giveH below, the home team won. Several of the games played were very interesting, and closely contested. Canon Dodd was particularly unlucky in losing as he held the upper hand throughout the game, until playing hurriedly in order to allow his opponent to catch his train, he threw away all and mpre than all his advantage. Mr. Taylor's opponent was fortunate in securing a draw on a position that was apparently against him. Score:—</p>
        <p>MANCHESTER. Canon Dodd 0 E Haughton 1 F Dunne 1 W Grimes L Chandler l_ W Rayner</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>O H Mason C W Perry 1 P Hodson G F Taylor k E Hodson A Wood</p>
        <p>Total 5</p>
        <p>Total</p>
        <p>Next Monday the Ardwick Club visit Warrington to play their league match when a very close fight is anticipated. The Warrington team however will probably be strengthened by the inclusion of Father Firth.</p>
        <p>HILL CLIFFE AND STOCKTON HEATH UNITED BAND OF HOPE.</p>
        <p>The first meeting of the Hill Cliffs and Stockton Heath United Band of Hope was held on Monday night in the Hill Cliffe schoolroom, which was filled with an appreciative audience. The President, the Rev. J. S. Hughes, presided, and in his address briefly explained how the society had been formed, on an undenominational basis and that, the meetings would be held in rotation at the different schools in the locality—Baptist, Independent, Methodist, and Wesleyan. A strong representative committee has been formed with every prospect of working a vigorous and healthy society. The meeting on Monday evening was most encouraging and success- ful in every way. A long and interesting pro- gramme of recitations was gone through by the children who mustered in strong force. Addresses were delivered by Mr. Henry Eyes and Mr. John Brereton. Mr. A. B. Kenworthy presided at the organ, and Mr. Dallows discharged the duties of secretary, Hoyle temperance melodies were used, and a collection was taken at the close. The meet- ings are to be held fortnightly and are to be open to all classes, sects and creeds who are willing to help in thus interesting and instructing the children during these winter nights. CONCERT AT THE VM.C.A.</p>
        <p>A very successful concert in connection with the P.S.A. was held in the Y.M.C.A. lecture hall on Wednesday evening. Mr. T. Morris, F.G.S. was chairman and with his preliminary address made a few remarks about the value of the P.S.A.'s in general and the Y.M C.A. P.S.A. in particular and concluded by wishing them every success. Mr. Hassall then gave the song, "Mary of Argyle" in a skilful manner and was followed by Miss J. Jones (Bolton) with" The last Dream." Miss Jones was very successful and quite charmed the audi- ence with her efficient renditions. A Lancashire recitation was then given by Mr. Furness in an amusing manner. Mr. W. H. Wallington was highly appreciated in his touching rendition of " The:Little Hero." Our local conjuror (Professor T. Acton) then gave a series of clever tricks in legerdemain which served to completely mystify the audience. After on interval of a few minutes Mr. Hassell initiated the second part of the evening's entertainment by singing, "Alice where art thou," in an excellent manner "The Dream of my heart," was then given by Miss Jones and in response to an encore she gave, "The Holy City." Mr. Morris being unable to stay any longer here left the room and appointed Mr. Kent to take his place. Mr. Kent then moved a vote of thanks to the chairman, ladies and gentlemen who had contributed to their pleasure and all others who had taken welt in the concert. The motion was accorded unanimously. Mr. Wallington re- commenced with" The Skipper of the Ives," and res- ponded to an enthnsiastic encore. By request Mr. Furness then gave, "Nomination Day," and Mr. Hassell followed with the song," Killa rney,' in an appreciative manner. The singing the national anthem concluded the entertainment.</p>
      </div>
