      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <p>WARRINGTON DEBATING SOCIETY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>ADJOURNED DEBATE ON THE HOUSE OF LORDS.</head>
        <p>ADJOURNED DEBATE ON THE HOUSE OF LORDS.</p>
        <p>The debate which was commenced by Mr. B. , Pierpoint, M.P., at the Masonic Rooms, on Friday night last week, upon the House of Lords, was re- sumed on Wednesday evening. Councillor Monks presided, and there was again a large attendance, Mr. Pierpoint being among those present. The debate was resumed by Mr. NEILD, who said that he desired progress in legislation, but he would rather move too slowly than too quickly. Lord Rosebery had not given sufficient information as to the manner in which he would deal with the Lords, but was adopting Mr. Gladstone's policy of asking for a blank cheque. He (Mr.Neild) was not in favour of a hereditary Chamber. He thought they should appoint life peers, and when the two Chambers could not agree to adopt the referen- . darn.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. DIGGLE was in favour of keeping the House of Lords as it'was at present, as "it was through the instrumentality of the House of Lords that England had gained her great empire." (Laughter.) Mr. C. LYON would make the House of Lords representative without being directly in touch with the people. He would propose to give to Town and County Councils the right to nominate a member for the second Chamber, and only allow judges and a few executive officers to be ex-oficzo members. He would allow the members to sit seven years, and to keep the House in touch with the country, he would cause one-seventh to retire each year. He would allow the House to be on the same lines in regard to its power as the present House of Lords, and restrict its veto to twice exercised,after which he would then propose to have a referendum. The qualifications he would propose to be the same as the House of Commons, with the addition of peers and clergymen, who would also be eligible for the House of Commons. He contended that this would bring municipal policy and imperial legislation into closer touch with each other. A,lr. WESTBROOK contended that the opposition to the House of Lords merely rose because they were doing their duty. Mr. HORTON said that it all depended where the legisla- tion originated from whether the House of Lords passed it or not. He wished to know whether the question of free education was ever the subject of an appeal to the country, and yet their lordships passed that simply because a Tory government had brought it in. Mr. C. PLINSTON observed that he had to follow a Liheral, which was an evidence that there were not sufficient Conservatives to be found to discuss the sub- ject fully. He contended that the House of Lords simply vetoe3 the bills which Lord Salisbury told them to do, and this was proved by the fact that they knew days before the division took place what the majority would be. They had been told that the House of Lords simply impeded legislation, but were they to wait ten, a dozen, or perhaps twenty years until it was the sweet pleasure of the House of Lords to pass the bills? Many billshad been before the House of Commons, which had been rejected by the Lords year after year, but they had eventually passed them. Mr. HALL said that they always found in that society more talkers among the Radioals than amongst the Con- servatives. The Radicals werermore excitable, and he thought that they would find that the Conservatives were the thinkieg party. (Laughter). The House ef Lords was objectionable to a party in a hurry, and this was the case with the Liberals. He thought that it was mistaken tactics on the part of the Liberals to agitate against the House of Lords, for he reminded them how ill-omened all previous attacks had been. Mr. BRUCHEMITS said that he did not represent either Liberal or Tory' but the I.L.P. They wanted a reform of the House of Commons as well as the House of Lords. His party did not care a rap about the agitation against the House of Lords, and would prefer the Conservatives in power to the Liberals, because the House of Lords would pass measures coming from a Conservative gov- ernment, but not from a Liberal government. The CHAIRMAN said that as far as the discussion bad gone that evening and also on the preceding evening,the general conclusirn was that the time had not come for the ending of the House of Lords. He would be sorry to think that anything of that sort was likely to happen, because it had served useful purposes in the past. He also thought that the experience of foreign countries and the colonies proved that a second chamber was really necessary. They had heard a good deal about hastening slowly. He did not mind hastening slowly when they were making some progress. (Hear, hear.) But with the House of Lords constituted as it was at present, it was not ready to give considerate and im- partial treatment to a Government composed of Liberals as to one of Conservatives. If they always took the same care with Conservative measures as with Liberal measures be would not think very much about it. They were too inclined to agree without any discussion with any measure introduced by a Con3ervative Govern- ment and they checked entirely everything which was introduced by a Liberal Government. He felt a little disappointment that the member for the borough had not expressed more clearly his opinion upon the subject of mending the House of Lords. It was very well to discuss the subject in an academic manner, but when a gentleman was practically engaged in meeting the difficulties which existed between the two Houses, he expected to bear some opinion expressed by him as to how those diffi- culties could be met. He gathered that the Conserva- tives themselves were not satisfied with the House of Lords as it was at present constituted. It was not for them to ask the Liberals to restrict the House, they themselves ought to assist equally with the Liberals. He was pleased that Mr. Lyon had made suggestions with regard to the reform of the House. His speech was really the only speech which dealt fairly with the , matter, and he placed before the meeting many impor- tant points in dealing with the re-organisation of the , House of Lords. There was one great drawback to giv- ing the nomination of members to the Town Council.</p>
        <p>They were all striving to free municipal matters from political control and excitement,but if they put it in the power of the Council to nominate members of the House of Lords, they would get deeper and deeper into the mire of politics. It seemed to him that a govern- ment ought certainly to be allowed to nominate certain members. They ought to be able to reward their sup- porters in the carrying on of the government of the countrv. They might arrange elected members, and also official members such as judges. He would not include the clergy, as' he thought that the hishons ought not to sit in the House of Lords. (Applause). He also thought that with a referendum, they would be able to get the voice of the country better, and that the business of the country would he greatly simplified.. Councillor ROBERTS said that they had heard a good deal that night that was about as foreign to the subject as it could possibly be. They had heard a good deal that night about having matters referred to the People. He had a sort of an idea that the questions which had been brought up for legislation in the House of Commons during the past session of parliament had been referred to the people and the people's will had been given upon them. The principle cf hereditary legislators was inde- fensible. The country had accepted for good or for the principle of democratic government,and no one who had aecepted that principle could justify the existence of the Helm of Lords as it was at present constituted. They would not have the hereditary principle in the Town Connell, or the magistrates' bench, ear upon any , ether interest, and it was therefore absurd to think that in the fiewe of Lords this principle should exist. He would pass to the spiritual peers. He could understand the veneration of the members of the Church ef England for the fabric itself, but he thought it was a mistake in the interests of the church that the bishops should he in the House of Lords simply by virtue of their being clereymen of the Church of England. The church was no longer the church of the people, but was simply a large and numerous,?and wealthy church, and if it was right for this denomination to have repre'senentiyes in the Hrmse, it was equally right for the dissenters or the Roman Catholics to have them also. Referring to the TL. P. movement, he asked the members of that party which party had passed all the legislation for the working classes of this country ? The Liberal party it was to whom they were indebted. Mr. Bruchemite might know the history of France well,'but he evidently did not know the history of England. Mr. PIERPOINT in rising to reply said that he had been very much interested in listening to the debate that evening and also on Friday. He had been reminded that he had not put forth any policy with regard to the House of Lords. It was not for the Tory side of the House to prepose an alteration of the system with which they were reasonably content. They must remember that if the House of Lords were reformed they would make it more powerful, and he did not suppose that the Litrrals desired that. Again if they had a singlo chamber they might have a House of Commons which after having existed to its normal term, would pass a resolution to sit for another ten years, and who was going to stop them? (Mr. Nield : The people, by agi- tation.) He did not believe in that kind of control. Better have it as it existed at present and be controlled by the House of Lords. Mr. Pierpoint then proceeded to defend the House of Lords in their action with regard to the Employers' Liability Bill, and the Evicted Tenants Bill.</p>
        <p>The proceedings afterwards terminated. 41. HELSBY.</p>
        <p>-- METHODIST FREE CHURCH.—Mr. S. Lumley, evange- list, commenced a series of mission services at the hove-nn mod church on Sunday. The services are to be continued until the 30th inst. There have been good congregations.</p>
        <p>THE PARISH COUNCIL. SANKEY BRIDGES,</p>
        <p>A meeting was held in the Sankey Bridges Wes- leyan schoolroom,en Saturday evening, to consider the steps to be taken at the forthcoming Parish Council election for Little Sankey. The chair was occupied by Mr. J. Summersgill. A long discussion arose, in which the question of the proposed ab- sorption of Little Sankey by in the borough of Warrington was discussed; and several members of the meeting, including the Chairman, expressed themselves as very strongly opposed to the scheme. Mr. Greeuwell proposed that this should be made a test question, but it was pointed out that the meeting had not been sufficiently made known to the villagers, and it was accordingly decided to I postpone the proceedings until the following Saturday, when candidates will be chosen, and definite volley decided upon.</p>
      </div>
