        <p>DISTRICT COUNCIL ELECTION. NEWTON WKIZ TO THE ELECTORS. LiDZES AIVD GENTLZMEN, I have been. requested to offer myself as a Candidate for your seffrages, and knowing your requirements. and having obtained the majority of votes in this Ward at the last Contest, I have much pleasure in placing my services at your disposal. I amaware the Committee of Selection have not seen fit to include me' in their list of Candidates; but as, I have the interest cf,the whole of the Ratepayers at heart,and not a few only. I do not regret that I have to cothe before you as an Independent Candidate, and I shall go tothe Poll still unre- pentant butanxious to do my best for everyone. I am in favour of a Water Supply for the District of which the Council will have the sole control, and which will there- fore leavens free from anyone. I also endeavour to abolish Gas Meter Rents, and modify the deposits paid by Gas Consumers. I shall desire in your interest that the Council shall have a closer supervision over the rates received, so that the Rate- payers may know to what accounts their rates are being applied and for what perposes. I know we shall have to face a large expenditure on Sewage matters. and I desire to keep the Council to the most moder- ate estimate, and not allow them to increase it at their pleasure without regard to your wishes. lam of opinion that we should keep the Central Pest Oellee in Newton: other places may need better postal facilities which we will not deprive them of, but the cost of all these should come from the Poet Office Department, and not from the rates. I haveadrocated and, if I am elected, will still further advocate the increwe of aecoinmodation in our Town Hall for the recreation of the people who use it, and I shall be prepared tourge the expeniiture of some portion of the rates for ouch purpose, as I think we are as much entitled to a convenieat Town Hall for public purposes as anyone else. In short,,Ladies and Gentlemen, I am prepared to advo- cate such measures as will be for our benefit, without regard to any clique, as I offer myself for election a perfectly ludo- peedent Candidate. May I remind yon that this Election is under the provi- sions of the Btllot Act, end therefore you can exercise your franchise witebout fear or favour. I ans. Ladies and Gentlemen, •Xours obediently, ftOBERT STUART. Laurel Bank, 218 t Nuvesnber, 1894.</p>
