        <p>inaction of the Watch Committee in regard to the enforcing of the betting laws have lately been both numerous and emphatic. The Committee and its apologists have not said—because they could not—that no betting took place but it has been said that they have done all that could be reasonably expected of them to prevent infringe- ments of the law. The suggestions that the hands of the police had been " tied " were indignantly repudiated. Yet when once the police were set in motion they had no difficulty in making their "catch," and less diffi- culty still in winning their case. There are few people in Warrington, unless we except the members of this very innocent Watch Committee, who will not freely admit that similar results might have been accomplished by similar means almost at any time during the last few years.</p>
